final_output:  ['\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n1. **Bob\'s Attributes:**  \n   - Bob is **smart** (given).  \n   - From premise 9: *All smart things are green*, so Bob must be **green**.  \n\n2. **Green → White:**  \n   - From premise 10: *Green things are white*, so Bob (being green) must be **white**.  \n\n3. **Consistency Check:**  \n   - If Bob were **not white**, he would violate premise 10 (since he is green). Thus, "Bob is not white" leads to a contradiction.  \n   - No other premises conflict with Bob being white.  \n\n4. **Additional Validations:**  \n   - Bob’s whiteness implies he is **not young** (premise 12: *White things are not young*).  \n   - Since Bob is smart and not young, premise 13 requires him to be **nice**. This does not contradict any given information.  \n   - The final premise (14) confirms Bob must be **big** if green and nice, which is consistent with his derived attributes.  \n\nThus, **Bob must be white**, making the statement **true**.  \n\nThe correct option is: A', 'Okay, let\'s try to figure out if Bob is white. Hmm, first I need to go through all the given information step by step. Let me list out the premises again to make sure I don\'t miss anything.\n\nFirst, the context says:\n\n1. Bob is quiet.\n2. Bob is smart.\n3. Charlie is quiet.\n4. Charlie is not white.\n5. Fiona is young.\n6. Harry is big.\n7. Harry is nice.\n8. If something is quiet and white, then it is green. (So, Quiet ∧ White → Green)\n9. All smart things are green. (Smart → Green)\n10. Green things are white. (Green → White)\n11. All smart, young things are big. (Smart ∧ Young → Big)\n12. White things are not young. (White → ¬Young)\n13. If something is smart and not young, then it is nice. (Smart ∧ ¬Young → Nice)\n14. If Bob is green and Bob is nice, then Bob is big. (Bob: Green ∧ Nice → Big)\n\nThe question is whether Bob is white. The options are True, False, or Unknown.\n\nLet me start by looking at Bob\'s attributes. From the first two p