1), then the dog must chase the cat (as I thought earlier). So the dog chases the cat. Now, if the dog chases the cat, then according to the same conditional "If something chases the dog then it chases the cat," but here the "something" would be the dog? Wait no, the dog is chasing the cat now, but the antecedent requires chasing the dog. So if the dog chases the cat, that doesn\'t trigger the conditional again. \n\nWait, the dog now chases the cat. Then, is there any other condition that applies here? Let\'s see. The conditional "If something chases the cat then it sees the dog." So if the dog chases the cat (as per the first conditional\'s result), then the dog must see the dog (since the "it" here is the dog). Wait, the dog would see the dog? That seems odd, but logically, if the dog chases the cat, then the dog must see the dog. But the problem states that the dog sees the cat (fact 6). So maybe that\'s allowed. But the conditional says "sees the dog," so the dog would see itself. That\'s possible in logic, even if it\'s strange.\n\nBut how does this help with the tiger seeing the dog?\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the tiger is doing any chasing. The cat chases the tiger (fact 3). If the cat chases the tiger, does that trigger any conditionals?\n\nLooking at the conditional: "If something chases the tiger and the tiger eats the cat then the cat sees the mouse." The cat chases the tiger, so "something" (the cat) chases the tiger. But does the tiger eat the cat? The problem states the tiger eats the dog (fact 10), but there\'s no mention of the tiger eating the cat. So the second part of the antecedent (the tiger eats the cat) is false, so the conditional doesn\'t apply. Therefore, the cat doesn\'t necessarily see the mouse because of that.\n\nAnother conditional: "If something sees the dog and it chases the tiger then the tiger chases the dog." Suppose the something is the cat. The cat chases the tiger (fact 3), and does the cat see the dog? Well, the dog sees the cat (fact 6), but that doesn\'t mean the cat sees the dog. Unless there\'s a condition that says if the dog sees the cat, then the cat sees the dog? No, that\'s not stated. So unless the cat sees the dog for another reason, this might not apply.\n\nAlternatively, if the cat chases the tiger, and if the cat sees the dog, then the tiger would chase the dog. But again, unless we can establish that the cat sees the dog, which we don\'t know, this might not hold.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let\'s try to approach the question directly: Is the statement "The tiger does not see the dog" true, false, or unknown?\n\nWe need to see if the tiger sees the dog, or if it\'s impossible, or if it\'s possible but not necessarily.\n\nLooking at the given facts, there\'s no direct statement that the tiger sees the dog or doesn\'t. So we have to check if any conditional statements force it to be one or the other.\n\nThe main conditional that could lead to the tiger seeing the dog is the one that says: "If the tiger is green and the tiger is big then the tiger sees the dog." To make that apply, we need both the tiger being green and big. But we don\'t know if the tiger is green. The context only says the tiger is nice, so that\'s irrelevant. Also, is the tiger big? The dog is big (fact 5), but the tiger\'s size isn\'t mentioned. Unless there\'s a way to infer the tiger is big.\n\nAnother conditional says "If something sees the mouse then it is big." If the tiger sees the mouse, then the tiger must be big. But do we know if the tiger sees the mouse?\n\nThe context states that the mouse does not see the tiger (fact 9), but that doesn\'t mean the tiger doesn\'t see the mouse. The tiger might see the mouse or not. Since there\'s no statement about that, we can\'t assume either way. Therefore, we can\'t conclude the tiger is big via that route.\n\nAlternatively, if the tiger is big for another reason, like being the tiger (maybe tigers are typically big?), but the problem doesn\'t state that. We have to go strictly by the given information.\n\nSo, without knowing the tiger\'s color or size, the antecedent of that conditional (green and big) can\'t be confirmed, so the consequent (tiger sees dog) can\'t be enforced.\n\nAnother angle: Is there any other way the tiger could be seeing the dog?\n\nLooking at the conditional "If something chases the cat then it sees the dog." If the tiger chases the cat, then the tiger would see the dog. Does the tiger chase the cat? The cat chases the tiger (fact 3), but that\'s different. Unless the tiger also chases the cat. Is there any condition that makes the tiger chase the cat?\n\nLooking at the conditional "If something chases the dog then it chases the cat." Since the cat chases the dog (fact 1), then the cat must also chase the cat. But that\'s the cat, not the tiger. So that doesn\'t involve the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, if the dog chases the cat (as per the first conditional\'s result), then does that trigger any conditions for the tiger?\n\nThe dog chases the cat, so if the dog is the "something" in the conditional "If something chases the cat then it sees the dog," then the dog would see the dog. Which again, is possible, but not helpful for the tiger.\n\nHmm. Let\'s consider another conditional: "If something sees the dog and it chases the tiger then the tiger chases the dog." Suppose the tiger chases the tiger? Not sure. Wait, the "something" here could be the cat. The cat chases the tiger (fact 3). If the cat sees the dog, then the tiger would chase the dog. But does the cat see the dog? The dog sees the cat (fact 6), but the cat might not see the dog. Unless there\'s a condition that if X sees Y, then Y sees X, but that\'s not stated. So unless the cat sees the dog, this doesn\'t apply.\n\nAlternatively, if the tiger chases the dog, then the tiger would see the dog? Wait, no, that\'s not stated. Unless chasing implies seeing? Not necessarily, unless there\'s a conditional that says so. The only