tated except that the lion needs the mouse (statement 3). So the mouse doesn\'t need the tiger, so the antecedent of 18 would be "if someone is cold and sees lion, then..." but since the mouse is cold and sees the lion, then they must need the tiger, but they don\'t. Wait, that\'s a problem. Because according to the given info, the mouse doesn\'t need the tiger. So that would create a contradiction. But maybe the problem is that the mouse must need the tiger, which isn\'t stated, so maybe there\'s an inconsistency here?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake here. Let me parse statement 18 again. It says: If someone is cold AND they see the lion, then they need the tiger. \n\nThe mouse is cold (from seeing the lion via 11) and the mouse does see the lion (statement 4), so according to 18, the mouse must need the tiger. But the context doesn\'t mention the mouse needing the tiger. The tiger needs the mouse (statement 9), but that\'s the other way around. So this would imply that the mouse needs the tiger, but that\'s not stated. Therefore, there\'s a contradiction here? Or does that mean that the mouse must need the tiger, so the information given is incomplete, so we can\'t determine?\n\nWait, no. The problem states all the given information. So perhaps we can infer that since the mouse must need the tiger (due to the implication in statement 18), but that\'s not in the given data, so maybe there\'s an inconsistency? Hmm, but in logic, we have to work with the given premises. Let\'s see:\n\nThe premises are:\n\n1. Mouse sees lion → mouse is cold (from 11).\n\n2. Therefore, mouse is cold.\n\n3. Since mouse is cold and sees the lion (from 4), then by statement 18, mouse must need the tiger.\n\nBut in the given context, there\'s no mention of the mouse needing the tiger. The tiger needs the mouse (statement 9), but the mouse\'s needs are not stated. Therefore, according to the premises, the conclusion from 18 would require that the mouse needs the tiger, but that\'s not in 