ar being green (statement2) is the only valid color because of implication18? But why would that override the blue?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the statements are all true, so the bear is all three colors, and thus the statement "the bear is not blue" is false because it is blue. So answer B.\n\nAlternatively, the problem might consider that an object can\'t have multiple colors, so the initial statements are contradictory, making the color unknown. But the question is about "not blue"—if the bear must be blue (statement1), then even with contradictions, the fact that it\'s blue is given, so the answer is B.\n\nWait, but the problem says "based on the above information". If the information includes conflicting statements about the bear\'s color, then we might have to treat them as all true, leading to an inconsistency, but in that case, can we infer anything?\n\nIn logic, if you have a contradiction (A and not A), then any statement can be inferred (principle of explosion). But that\'s probably not the case here; the problem is likely designed so that the statements are to be treated as consistent, and the contradictions are resolved via the implications.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first three statements are not all true at the same time. For example, maybe the bear\'s color is determined by the implications. Let me think:\n\nSuppose the bear is blue (statement1), but implication18 could require it to be green if certain conditions are met. But those conditions aren\'t met, so the bear remains blue and green? That doesn\'t resolve the conflict.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct approach is to see that the initial statements are all facts, so the bear is blue, green, and red. But the question is whether it\'s not blue. Since it is blue, the answer is B (False).\n\nAlternatively, maybe the color statements are meant to be exclusive, so only one is true. For example, if the bear is green (statement2), then the first statement (blue) is false. But how do we know which 