oes chase itself, then the statement is false regardless of other contradictions. \n\nAlternatively, the fact that the mouse must chase itself creates an inconsistency, but the question is about the truth value of the given statement under the premises. If the premises require the mouse to chase itself, then the statement "mouse does not chase mouse" is false. The inconsistency between that and the unestablished seeing of the bear might mean the premises are inconsistent, but the question is about the truth of the statement within the system, not the consistency of the system. \n\nTherefore, following strictly the given premises without considering contradictions between them, the answer would be B) False. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the mouse can\'t chase itself because that would require seeing the bear, which isn\'t stated. But the premises don\'t state that the mouse sees the bear, so we can\'t assume it does. Therefore, the requirement from premise 14 isn\'t met, so the mouse doesn\'t have to chase itself. \n\nWait, this is conflicting. Let me try to structure it logically:\n\nPremises leading to the mouse chasing itself:\n\n1. M is nice (6).\n\n2. All nice people are cold (19). Assuming M is a person → M is cold.\n\n3. Therefore, M is nice and cold → by 15, M chases M.\n\nBut then, by premise 14, if M chases M → M sees B.\n\nBut M\'s seeing B isn\'t established. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion that M chases M requires M to see B. Since M doesn\'t see B (as not stated), the antecedent of premise 14 (M chases M) would have to be false to avoid contradicting the implication (since if A→B and B is false, then A must be false). \n\nTherefore, if we assume that M doesn\'t see B (which is possible since it\'s not stated), then to maintain the implication in premise 14, we must conclude that M does not chase M. \n\nThus, the statement "The mouse does not chase the mouse" would be true. \n\nWait, this is a different approach. Let me explain:\n\nIn logic, if we have A → B,