For example, rule 10 says if something is young, then it is green. But Bob's greenness is already given. The problem is whether the statement \"Bob is not green\" is true. Since the context explicitly states Bob is green, the statement \"Bob is not green\" must be false. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's a hidden contradiction. Let me see if any of the other information could affect this. Let's check Bob's other attributes. Bob is not nice (given). Also, the question might involve some logical deductions that could affect Bob's status. Let me see:\n\nThe rules say that if something is big, then it is nice (rule 12). But Bob is not nice, so Bob cannot be big. Because if Bob were big, he would have to be nice, which contradicts Bob being not nice. So Bob is not big. \n\nAlso, rule 14 says if something is furry, then it is big. So if Bob were furry, he would have to be big. But we just concluded Bob is not big, so Bob cannot be furry either. Therefore, Bob is not furry. \n\nNow, rule 13 says if Bob is furry, then he is young. But since Bob isn't furry, that implication is vacuously true. Doesn't affect anything else.\n\nNow, the key point is Bob's greenness. The first statement says Bob is green. But rule 10 says if something is young, then it's green. But do we know if Bob is young? Let's see. \n\nIs there any information about Bob's youth? If Bob were furry, then he would be young (rule 13), but he's not furry. Alternatively, if Bob is kind, then he would be young (rule 8). But to be kind, he must be nice (rule 15: nice implies kind). But Bob isn't nice, so he can't be kind. Therefore, there's no indication that Bob is young. \n\nHowever, Bob is already stated to be green. Rule 10 says that being young implies green, but it doesn't say that greenness requires being young. So Bob could be green without being young, unless there's another rule. Wait, let me check rule 9: Young and big things are green. But that's a separate condition. \n\nWait, perhaps there's a rule that all green things must be young? No, rule 10 is \"If something is young, then it is green,\" which is one direction. The converse isn't stated. So being green doesn't necessarily mean young. Therefore, Bob's greenness is given, and there's no rule that would make him not green. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"Bob is not green\" is false. So the correct answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe context explicitly states \"Bob is green,\" which directly contradicts the question's assertion that \"Bob is not green.\" While other rules may influence Bob's attributes (e.g., he cannot be big or furry due to being not nice and rule 12), these do not negate his stated greenness. Since the premise confirms Bob's greenness, the statement claiming he is not green is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
