ement 13 (assuming I counted correctly). Wait, let me recount the statements to make sure I didn't miscount.\n\nWait, the context lists 12 statements. Let me check again:\n\n1. Bob is green.\n2. Charlie is nice.\n3. Charlie is smart.\n4. Charlie is white.\n5. Erin is nice.\n6. Harry is nice.\n7. Harry is white.\n8. If Charlie is green then Charlie is blue.\n9. All nice people are cold.\n10. All cold people are blue.\n11. Rough people are green.\n12. If someone is blue then they are green.\n13. Blue, green people are white.\n14. If Bob is smart and Bob is blue then Bob is not green.\n15. If Charlie is white then Charlie is nice.\n16. All white people are smart.\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted earlier. Let me check the original problem again:\n\nThe context given has the following statements:\n\n\"Bob is green. Charlie is nice. Charlie is smart. Charlie is white. Erin is nice. Harry is nice. Harry is white. If Charlie is green then Charlie is blue. All nice people are cold. All cold people are blue. Rough people are green. If someone is blue then they are green. Blue, green people are white. If Bob is smart and Bob is blue then Bob is not green. If Charlie is white then Charlie is nice. All white people are smart.\"\n\nSo that's 16 statements. Let me list them again properly:\n\n1. Bob is green.\n2. Charlie is nice.\n3. Charlie is smart.\n4. Charlie is white.\n5. Erin is nice.\n6. Harry is nice.\n7. Harry is white.\n8. If Charlie is green then Charlie is blue.\n9. All nice people are cold.\n10. All cold people are blue.\n11. Rough people are green.\n12. If someone is blue then they are green.\n13. Blue, green people are white. (Wait, maybe this means if someone is both blue and green, then they are white?)\n14. If Bob is smart and Bob is blue then Bob is not green.\n15. If Charlie is white then Charlie is nice.\n16. All white people are smart.\n\nOkay, so 16 statements. Now, moving on.\n\nBack to the problem: We need to determine if Erin is not smart. The options are True, False, Unknown. The question is whether \"Erin is not smart\" is true, so we have to see if Erin is not smart (so the statement would be true if she isn't, false if she is, unknown if we can't determine).\n\nFirst, let's note that Erin is nice (statement 5). From statement 9: All nice people are cold. So Erin is cold. Then, from statement 10: All cold people are blue. So Erin must be blue. \n\nNow, from statement 12: If someone is blue, then they are green. So since Erin is blue, she must be green. \n\nBut also, statement 13: \"Blue, green people are white.\" Hmm, the wording here is a bit ambiguous. It might mean that if someone is both blue and green, then they are white. So, if someone is both blue and green \u2192 they are white. \n\nSo Erin is blue (from cold) and green (from being blue), so she must be white as well, per statement 13. \n\nNow, statement 16: All white people are smart. If Erin is white, then she must be smart. \n\nWait, so let's track Erin's attributes:\n\n- Erin is nice (given)\n- Therefore, cold (statement 9)\n- Therefore, blue (statement 10)\n- Therefore, green (statement 12)\n- Because she's both blue and green, she must be white (statement 13)\n- Since she is white, she must be smart (statement 16)\n\nTherefore, Erin is smart. Therefore, the statement \"Erin is not smart\" would be false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check for any contradictions or alternative paths.\n\nIs there any way Erin isn't smart? Let's see:\n\nSuppose there's a way that Erin isn't white. Let's see:\n\nTo be white, she has to be both blue and green (from statement 13). But since she is blue (because cold) and blue requires green (statement 12), then she must be green. Therefore, blue and green \u2192 white. So she must be white, leading to smart. So unless there's a way she isn't blue or green, but she must be blue because she's cold. So no, she must be white, so smart.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that could prevent Erin from being