he lion chases the rabbit (statement 2). So substituting the lion into statement 8: If the lion is rough and it chases the rabbit (which it does), then the rabbit is rough. But we know the rabbit is cold, which is green. But does being green have anything to do with roughness? Not directly stated yet.\n\nWait, maybe another angle. Let\'s see if there\'s a way the dog could be rough. Let\'s see if any of the premises imply that the dog is rough or not.\n\nLooking at statement 10: If something chases the lion, then it is rough. Is there any mention of something chasing the lion? The dog chases the squirrel, the lion chases the rabbit. The squirrel visits the dog. The lion needs the dog. Hmm, unless someone else chases the lion. The premise 14 says if something needs the lion, then it chases the lion. So if an entity needs the lion, then it must chase the lion. \n\nThe dog is green, so from statement 12, the dog needs the lion. Therefore, the dog needs the lion, so by statement 14, the dog must chase the lion. Wait, that\'s a key point. Let me check that again.\n\nThe dog is green (from earlier steps), so by statement 12, the dog needs the lion. Therefore, because the dog needs the lion, according to statement 14: If something needs the lion, then it chases the lion. Therefore, the dog must chase the lion. \n\nIf the dog chases the lion, then according to statement 10: If something chases the lion, then it is rough. So the dog, which is chasing the lion, would therefore be rough. \n\nWait, that\'s a chain of reasoning. Let me retrace:\n\n1. Dog is green (from statements 9-13).\n2. Therefore, dog needs the lion (statement 12).\n3. Therefore, since the dog needs the lion, by statement 14, the dog must chase the lion.\n4. Then, by statement 10, since the dog is chasing the lion, the dog must be rough.\n\nTherefore, the dog is rough. But the question is whether the dog is NOT rough. Therefore, the statement "The dog is not rough" would be false, so the correct answer would