, then it can\'t be something else," but looking at the rules, most of them are about categories and implications. The first four statements about Anne are direct assertions, so unless there\'s a rule that contradicts them, they should hold.\n\nLooking at the general rules:\n\n- All smart things are furry. (So Gary being smart makes him furry, which is given, but that\'s about Gary, not Anne.)\n\n- Big, blue things are round. (Not directly related to Anne unless she is blue, but she\'s white.)\n\n- If Gary is cold then Gary is smart. (Again, about Gary.)\n\n- All blue, furry things are big. (Doesn\'t affect Anne unless she\'s blue and furry.)\n\n- If Gary is furry then Gary is blue. (Still Gary.)\n\n- Round things are cold. Fiona is round, so Fiona should be cold. But the context already says Erin is cold, and Fiona is round, so Fiona must also be cold due to the rule. But that\'s okay, no conflict here.\n\n- Cold, white things are furry. Anne is cold and white, so according to this rule, she must be furry. Which she is, as stated in the third point. So that\'s consistent.\n\n- Round things are blue. Fiona is round, so she must be blue. But the context doesn\'t mention Fiona\'s color, so that\'s an inference but not conflicting.\n\nSo for Anne\'s case, all the given attributes in the context are consistent with each other and the rules. Since the context explicitly states "Anne is cold," that\'s a direct assertion. There\'s no rule that would negate that. Therefore, the statement "Anne is cold" is true. The answer should be A) True.\n']
