tement "Anne is not big" is false. So the correct answer would be B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there\'s another angle. The problem mentions Fiona is young and not quiet. Fiona is young and furry? Wait, Fiona is not quiet (fact 5) and young (fact 6). Let\'s see if Fiona is furry. The context doesn\'t explicitly say Fiona is furry. Wait, but let\'s see:\n\nFiona\'s attributes are not quiet and young. The only thing about being young and furry is premise 11: If something is young and furry, then it is quiet. Fiona is young but not quiet. Therefore, if Fiona were furry, then by premise 11, she would have to be quiet. But she\'s not quiet, so she can\'t be both young and furry. Therefore, Fiona is not furry. So Fiona\'s not furry. But that\'s just an aside.\n\nBack to Anne. Since Anne is furry, she must be nice (fact 10), so nice implies big (fact 9). So Anne must be big. Therefore, the statement "Anne is not big" is false. Therefore, the answer is B.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other rule that might interfere. For example, the fact that Anne is not quiet. The only premise that mentions Anne\'s quietness is premise 12: "If Anne is quiet, then Anne is cold." But since Anne is not quiet (fact 3), that premise\'s antecedent is false, so the implication holds, but doesn\'t give us any new info. So that doesn\'t affect the big/nice/furry chain.\n\nAnother check: Are there any other properties that might affect whether Anne is big? The only path is through being furry → nice → big. Unless there\'s a rule that says something else. Let me check all the premises again:\n\n1-7: specific attributes of individuals.\n\n8: Kind → furry.\n\n9: Nice → big.\n\n10: Furry → nice.\n\n11: (Young ∧ furry) → quiet.\n\n12: Anne quiet → Anne cold (but since Anne isn\'t quiet, irrelevant).\n\n13: (Kind ∧ big) → young.\n\n14: (Kind ∧ ¬furry) → ¬young.\n\nSo none of the other premises interfere with the chain that Anne is furry → nice → big. Therefore, the conclusion must be that Anne is big, so "Anne is not big" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Suppose someone argues that maybe "nice things are big" is a biconditional? Like, only nice things are big? But the problem states "Nice things are big" which is a one-way implication. So being big could have other causes. But in Anne\'s case, she\'s nice, so she must be big. Even if there are other ways to be big, the fact that she is nice makes her big regardless.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion holds. The answer is B.\n'] 10%|▉         | 59/600 [1:34:12<10:35:01, 70.43s/it]
