big. Therefore, the answer is still True.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that could prevent Harry from being big? Let\'s see:\n\n- There\'s no rule that says "young people cannot be big" or anything like that. The only rules are the ones given.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check another angle. The problem states "If someone is young and white then they are big"—so that\'s a biconditional? Or just one way? The way it\'s phrased is "If someone is young and white then they are big." So it\'s an implication, not necessarily the other way. So being young and white necessitates being big, but being big doesn\'t necessarily require being young and white. So that\'s okay. So Harry being young and white requires him to be big. So yes, he must be big. Therefore, the answer is A.\n\nHmm, but wait, the original problem might have a trick. Let me check again all the given facts and rules to make sure I didn\'t misread anything.\n\nWait, the initial facts for Harry are only "Harry is young." So no other attributes are given. The rules, however, impose that being young makes him white (so he is white), and then being young and white makes him big. So yes, that\'s correct.\n\nAnother check: Let\'s see if there\'s any other rule that could override this. For instance, the rule "If Bob is big then Bob is white" — but Bob\'s bigness isn\'t stated, but he is white. So that rule doesn\'t add anything unless Bob were big, but since he\'s white, the implication holds (if he were big, then white is true). But that\'s not relevant to Harry.\n\nAnother rule: "Cold, young people are rough." Since Harry is young and if he is cold (from being blue), then he would have to be rough. But being rough isn\'t mentioned for Harry, but again, the question is about being big, so that\'s okay. There\'s no contradiction because the rules allow him to be rough as a consequence, but since there\'s no stated fact contradicting that, it\'s acceptable.\n\nSo, putting it