flicting with white.\n\nNow, looking at rule 18: If someone is white and not cold then they are not blue. Since Bob is cold (point2), even if he were white, the "not cold" part wouldn\'t apply, so that rule doesn\'t affect Bob\'s possible whiteness directly.\n\nWait, but the key is, does Bob have to be white? Let\'s see.\n\nWait, from rule 14, Bob is green. So if Bob is green, then according to rule 12, he\'s furry (which he is). But the problem is, can someone be both green and white? The problem doesn\'t state that colors are mutually exclusive. Unless there\'s a rule that says so, but I don\'t see any. So maybe Bob could be both green and white. However, the question is whether the statement "Bob is white" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nBut wait, let\'s check if there\'s any rule that would require or forbid Bob from being white. Let me see.\n\nLooking at rule 15 again: "If Bob is not green and Bob is not white then Bob is furry." But since we know Bob is green, the first part of the antecedent (not green) is false, so the implication holds regardless. So that rule doesn\'t affect it.\n\nAnother angle: Since Bob is smart (and thus green), and smart people are green, so Bob is green. Now, can a green person also be white? Unless there\'s a rule against it, but there\'s none mentioned. So Bob could be both, but is there any information that tells us Bob\'s color besides green?\n\nWait, the context states Bob is big, cold, furry, smart, but not mentioning his color except via being smart (so green). The only direct color statements about Bob are not given except through the smart rule. Wait, the problem says "Bob is not green" isn\'t stated. Wait, actually, according to the given context, the statements about Bob are: big, cold, furry, smart. The color attributes (green, white, etc.) come from the rules. Since he\'s smart, he must be green. So Bob is green. \n\nNow, the question is whether he\'s white. The problem says Fiona is white and not green, but that\'s Fiona