green and white, but the question is whether we can conclude he is white.\n\nWait, the problem states that "Bob is not green and Bob is not white then Bob is furry." But since Bob is green, the antecedent is false, so that rule doesn\'t apply. So that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is an implicit assumption that each person has only one color, but that\'s not stated. The problem doesn\'t say that colors are mutually exclusive. So unless there\'s a rule, we can\'t assume that.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, Bob is green (due to being smart), but there\'s no information that says he is white or not. Wait, but hold on. Wait, the context lists Fiona as white, but Bob\'s color is only mentioned through the smart rule (green). The only direct color attributes for Bob are not stated except through rules. So unless there\'s a rule that requires a green person to not be white, which there isn\'t, then Bob could be either white or not. Therefore, the answer would be unknown?\n\nWait, but let\'s check again. Let me see if there\'s any other rule that might require Bob to be white or not.\n\nWait, the fifth rule: Fiona is white, but that\'s separate. \n\nWait, another angle: The problem says "If Bob is not green and Bob is not white then Bob is furry." The contrapositive of this would be: if Bob is not furry, then Bob is green or white. But Bob is furry (given), so the antecedent of the contrapositive isn\'t triggered, so that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, since Bob is green, the "not green" part is false, so the implication in rule15 is trivially true, but it doesn\'t give any info on whether Bob is white or not.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that states Bob is white or not, except that he must be green, but green and white can coexist unless prohibited. Since there\'s no prohibition, it\'s possible he is white, but there\'s no evidence for it. So the answer would be "unknown" (option C)?\n\nWait, but wait another thought: The question is "is Bob white?" The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s a way to determine it\'s false. Suppose someone is green (Bob is), so maybe the color green is exclusive with white? Like, maybe in the context, people can only have one color, but that\'s an assumption not stated. The problem says Fiona is white and not green, but that doesn\'t mean green people can\'t be white. Unless "green" and "white" are mutually exclusive, but the problem doesn\'t say that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules imply that if someone is green, they can\'t be white. Let me check all the rules again:\n\nRule 12: All green are furry. Doesn\'t affect color.\n\nRule 14: Smart people are green. So smart → green.\n\nRule 15: If (¬green ∧ ¬white) → furry. But since Bob is green, that antecedent is false.\n\nRule 13: If Gary is furry and ¬smart → white. But Gary is smart, so no.\n\nRule 17: smart and furry → big. Bob is big.\n\nRule 16: big → cold. Bob is cold.\n\nRule 18: white ∧ ¬cold → ¬blue. Bob is cold, so doesn\'t apply.\n\nRule 19: cold → ¬blue. So Bob is not blue, but that\'s irrelevant to white.\n\nSo there\'s no rule that says a green person can\'t be white. Therefore, Bob could be both green and white, or just green. Since there\'s no information stating his color beyond green (from being smart), the fact that he is green doesn\'t preclude him from being white. But also, there\'s no information that says he is white. So the answer should be "unknown".\n\nWait, but let me check again. Wait, the problem says "All green people are furry." Since Bob is green and furry, that\'s okay. But Fiona is white and not green. So the fact that Fiona is white and not green doesn\'t mean that green people can\'t be white. So there\'s no rule against it.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that states Bob is white or not, except that he is green, but green doesn\'t conflict with white, the answer is that it\'s unknown. Therefore, the correct option is C.\n\nWait, but wait. Let me think again. Is there any other rule that could lead to a conclusion?\n\nWait, the rule 15: If Bob is not green and not white → furry. Since Bob is green, the antecedent is false, so it doesn\'t affect. So that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that Bob is furry is already stated, so even if he were not green and not white, he would still be furry. But since he is green, it\'s irrelevant.\n\nHmm, so I think the conclusion is that the information given doesn\'t state whether Bob is white or not, so the answer is unknown. Therefore, option C.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s a contradiction if Bob were white. Suppose Bob is white. Then, since he\'s green and white, that\'s allowed. There\'s no rule against that. So no problem. Alternatively, if Bob is not white, that\'s also okay. So since both possibilities are allowed by the given rules, the answer is unknown. So C is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe correct option is: C\n'] 11%|█▏        | 68/600 [1:51:47<16:08:33, 109.24s/it]
