vant here. \n\nAnother point: The squirrel likes the lion (statement12). That\'s given, but doesn\'t directly affect the cat\'s feelings towards the mouse. \n\nWhat about the lion being red (statement4)? Rule9 says if something is red and does not need the squirrel, then it visits the mouse. The lion is red (statement4). Does the lion need the squirrel? Statement7 says the lion needs the squirrel. So the lion does need the squirrel, so Rule9\'s condition (does not need the squirrel) is not met. So Rule9 doesn\'t apply here. \n\nTherefore, no conflict there. \n\nSo putting all together, the chain seems solid. Therefore, the cat must like the mouse, so the answer should be A) True. \n\nWait, but hold on a second. Let me check again the exact wording of the rules to ensure I didn\'t misinterpret anything. \n\nRule6: "If something visits the cat then the cat likes the mouse." So the antecedent is "something visits the cat", and the consequent is "the cat likes the mouse". So whenever any entity visits the cat, the cat\'s liking for the mouse is triggered. \n\nIn this case, the squirrel is visiting the cat, so the antecedent is satisfied. Therefore, the consequent must hold. Therefore, the cat must like the mouse. \n\nTherefore, the answer is A) True. \n\nHmm, but wait another thought: The problem says "If something visits the cat then the cat likes the mouse." Does that mean that whenever any visitor comes to the cat, the cat likes the mouse? So regardless of who visits, as long as someone is visiting, the cat must like the mouse? \n\nAlternatively, is it that for each visitor, the cat likes the mouse? But the way it\'s phrased is "If X visits the cat then the cat likes the mouse." So for any X, if X visits the cat, then the cat\'s liking is required. So as long as there is at least one visitor to the cat, then the cat must like the mouse. \n\nIn our case, the squirrel does visit the cat, so that\'s enough. Therefore, the cat must like the mouse. \n\nTherefore, the conc