t, maybe the problem is that the conditions are such that if someone is quiet and red, they are smart, but if being smart leads to a contradiction, then the only way to resolve this is that Gary cannot be quiet. Because if he were quiet, that would force him to be smart, which then forces him to be white, conflicting with his red color. Therefore, the conclusion is that Gary cannot be quiet. Therefore, the statement "Gary is quiet" is false. So the answer would be B) False.\n\nLet me retrace the steps to make sure:\n\n1. Gary is big → round (from statement 9).\n2. Gary is red and round → so by statement 8, he should be quiet.\n3. But if he is quiet and red → by statement 6, he must be smart.\n4. If he is smart and big (he is big), then by statement 5, he must be white.\n5. But Gary is red, so being white is impossible. Therefore, the assumption that he is quiet leads to a contradiction.\n6. Therefore, Gary cannot be quiet. Hence the statement "Gary is quiet" is false.\n\nSo the correct answer should be B) False.\n'] 12%|█▎        | 75/600 [2:01:47<12:33:55, 86.16s/it]
