, he isn\'t.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through being rough and smart? But rough things are smart (statement16), but that\'s the other way. So being rough implies smart, but not vice versa.\n\nHmm. Maybe the key is to see if Bob must be big or not. But that might not affect his whiteness.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there are any other implications involving white. The only statements involving white are 14, 18 (which doesn\'t involve white), and 19 (which involves Fiona).\n\nStatement 14: If Bob is white → furry. Since he is furry, that\'s okay.\n\nStatement 19: If Fiona is red and white → smart. Fiona is smart anyway, so that\'s okay.\n\nSo there\'s no rule that would make Bob\'s whiteness lead to a contradiction. Therefore, Bob could be white or not; the information doesn\'t specify. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not white" would be unknown, because there\'s no information that forces him to be white or not.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check again. Are there any other constraints?\n\nWait, statement 18: If Bob is round and big, then he\'s furry. Since Bob is furry, but unless he\'s round and big, this doesn\'t add anything. Since Bob\'s roundness isn\'t known, so this doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through Fiona\'s properties. Fiona is red (from statement17) and big (from statement8). So Fiona is big and red. Does that help with anything?\n\nHmm. Let me think if there\'s any way to force Bob to be not white. Suppose Bob were white. Then, is there a way that leads to a contradiction?\n\nAlternatively, maybe if Bob is white and red, but that\'s allowed. Unless there\'s a rule that says something can\'t be both red and white. The problem doesn\'t state that, so they can coexist.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if Bob is white and smart, but that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that all rough things are smart (statement16), but Bob\'s roughness isn\'t established.\n\nWait, another thought: Let\'s consider all the attributes that must be true fo