emises don\'t state those actions, but they also don\'t contradict them. Therefore, the conclusion holds.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s another angle. Let me think of the premises again. Is there any other premise that could affect the tiger\'s size?\n\nLooking at premise16: If something eats the lion, then it is not cold. The tiger does not eat the lion (premise3 says the lion does not eat the tiger, but that\'s different). The tiger eats the dog (premise9). So that\'s irrelevant here.\n\nPremise4: The lion is not big. So the lion\'s size is known. The tiger\'s size is derived from chasing the lion. So yes, the conclusion is that the tiger is big. Therefore, the answer is B) False, because the statement claims the tiger is not big, which is false.\n\nWait, but I need to make sure there\'s no other condition that could make the tiger not big. For instance, if the tiger doesn\'t chase the lion, but premise8 clearly states it does. So no, that\'s not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False.\n'] 14%|█▍        | 86/600 [2:19:25<14:40:58, 102.84s/it]
