ead end unless we can find another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe if she\'s smart and blue, then she\'s furry (statement 10). But again, we don\'t know if she\'s smart. \n\nAlternatively, if she were smart and white, then she\'d be furry (statement 11). But Erin isn\'t white, since Fiona is the one mentioned as white (statement 5). Wait, but the problem doesn\'t say Erin\'s color beyond blue. So Erin is blue but maybe not white. So that path doesn\'t apply.\n\nAnother thought: The statement 12 says young things are nice. So if Erin were young, then she would be nice. But we need to see if she can be young. Let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction if she were young.\n\nSuppose Erin is young. Then by statement 9, she would be smart. Then, since she\'s blue and smart (from being young and blue), by statement 10, she would be furry. Also, since she\'s young, she would be nice (statement 12), and since she\'s nice, by statement 16, she must be blue (which she is). \n\nBut does that cause any contradictions? Let\'s see. If she\'s furry, nice, then by statement 8, she must be white. Wait, statement 8 says "Furry, nice things are white." So if Erin were furry and nice, then she must be white. But Erin is blue (statement 2). So that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Erin cannot be both furry and nice. \n\nTherefore, if we suppose Erin is young (leading to being nice, smart, and thus furry via statements 9 and 10), that would require her to be white (from statement 8), but she\'s blue. Therefore, the assumption that Erin is young leads to a contradiction (since she would have to be both blue and white, but that\'s impossible unless overlapping, but maybe colors can be combined? The problem doesn\'t mention that. So likely, colors are exclusive here. Hence, the contradiction means that Erin cannot be young. \n\nTherefore, Erin is not young. Since she\'s not young, statement 12 doesn\'t apply, so we can\'t say she\'s nice based on that. \n\nNow, is there another way Erin could be nice? The only way to be nice is via being young (from statement 12) or maybe another rule? Let\'s check all statements again. \n\nLooking at the rules, the only other mention of "nice" is in statement 8 ("Furry, nice things are white") and statement 16 ("If something is nice, then it is blue"). \n\nSo, if something is nice, it must be blue. Erin is blue, but that doesn\'t automatically make her nice. The converse isn\'t necessarily true. \n\nAlternatively, maybe Erin could be nice through some other condition? The problem\'s given statements don\'t mention any other way to be nice except through being young. \n\nTherefore, since Erin isn\'t young, she can\'t be nice via statement 12. \n\nThus, there\'s no way to conclude that Erin is nice. But wait, is there any other path?\n\nWait, perhaps through being furry and nice? But that would require her to be white (from statement 8). But she\'s blue, so that\'s not possible. \n\nAlternatively, if she is smart and white, then she is furry (statement 11), but again, she\'s blue not white. \n\nAlternatively, being blue and smart leads to furry (statement 10), but being smart requires being young and blue (statement 9). But since she can\'t be young (due to the contradiction), she can\'t be smart. \n\nTherefore, Erin\'s status as nice is not supported by any of the statements. \n\nBut wait, let me check statement 16 again: If something is nice, then it is blue. The contrapositive is if not blue, then not nice. Since Erin is blue, that doesn\'t help. \n\nAlternatively, could there be a case where being blue and something else makes her nice? Not that I can see. \n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information leading to Erin being young (which is the only way to be nice), and no other way to be nice, we can\'t confirm that she is nice. \n\nBut wait, maybe there\'s another angle. Let me check all entities again. \n\nLooking at Fiona: Fiona is blue and white (statements 4 and5). That might be important. \n\nThe statement 13 says: If Fiona is smart and young, then she\'s not furry. But Fiona is white and blue. Let\'s see if Fiona is smart. To be smart, Fiona would have to be young and blue (statement9). But we don\'t know if Fiona is young. If Fiona is young, then she would be smart, but then Fiona would have to be young and blue, leading to smart, then via statement10 (blue and smart → furry), so furry. But statement13 says if Fiona is smart and young, then she is not furry. So that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Fiona cannot be both smart and young. \n\nTherefore, Fiona is either not smart, not young, or both. Since Fiona is white and blue, maybe she can be smart another way? Let\'s see. \n\nAlternatively, Fiona could be smart via another route? The only way to be smart is via being young and blue (statement9). So if Fiona isn\'t young, she can\'t be smart. \n\nSo maybe Fiona is not young. But we don\'t know. \n\nThis might not affect Erin\'s case directly. \n\nBack to Erin: Since there\'s no way to establish she\'s young, and being young is the only path to being nice, then Erin cannot be nice. Wait, but maybe there\'s a different path?\n\nWait, the problem says "nice" is mentioned in statement 8 and 12 and 16. Statement 12: young → nice. So unless there\'s another way to be nice, then no. So since Erin isn\'t young, she can\'t be nice. Therefore the answer would be B) False? Because the question is whether "Erin is nice" is true. Since she can\'t be, then it\'s false. Wait, but hold on. \n\nWait, maybe there\'s a mistake in my reasoning. Let me retrace. \n\nThe conclusion was that if Erin were young, then she would have to be white, but she\'s blue, so she can\'t be young. Therefore, Erin is not young. Since being young is the only way to be nice (from statement12), then Erin cannot be nice. Therefore, the statement "Erin is nice" must be false. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer would be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check again. The problem says "nice things are white" (statement8). Wait, statement8 says "Furry, nice things are white." So if something is both furry and nice, then it is white. But if something is nice but not furry, maybe it doesn\'t have to be white? Hmm, that complicates things. \n\nWait, so statement8 is a conditional: If something is furry AND nice, then it is white. It doesn\'t say that all nice things are white, only those that are both furry and nice. \n\nSo, Erin could be nice without being furry, and thus not needing to be white. So my previous conclusion that being young leads to a contradiction might not hold. Let me reassess that part. \n\nIf Erin is young, then she is nice (statement12). If she\'s young and blue (since she\'s blue), then she\'s smart (statement9). Then, since she\'s blue and smart (from being young and blue), she must be furry (statement10). \n\nNow, she is furry and nice (because she\'s young → nice). Therefore, by statement8, she must be white. But she\'s blue, which is a contradiction. Therefore, the assumption that she\'s young leads to a contradiction (being both blue and white). Therefore, she cannot be young. \n\nTherefore, Erin is not young, so not nice via statement12. \n\nHowever, could there be another way for her to be nice? The problem doesn\'t mention any other way. So the only way to be nice is being young. Therefore, she can\'t be nice. \n\nTherefore, "Erin is nice" is false. So the answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check the contrapositive. Since the only way to be nice is to be young, and she isn\'t, then she can\'t be nice. Therefore the statement is false. \n\nAlternatively, maybe "nice" can be achieved through another means not mentioned? The problem states "Young things are nice" (statement12), but doesn\'t say that only young things are nice. So maybe there are other ways to be nice? The wording is important here. \n\nStatement12 is "Young things are nice." This is a universal statement: All young things are nice, but it doesn\'t exclude the possibility that non-young things can also be nice. Therefore, maybe there are other ways to be nice. \n\nOh, this is a crucial point! I made a mistake here. The statement "Young things are nice" means that all young things are nice, but it doesn\'t mean that only young things are nice. So, there might be other things that are nice even if they aren\'t young. \n\nTherefore, my earlier conclusion was incorrect. So I need to re-examine this. \n\nSo, to determine if Erin is nice, we can\'t just assume that only young things are nice. There might be other ways. \n\nHmm, so what are the other statements involving "nice"? \n\nStatement8: Furry and nice → white. \n\nStatement16: If nice → blue. \n\nSo, if Erin is nice, then she must be blue (statement16), which she is. But that doesn\'t help us confirm she\'s nice. \n\nThe only other way to be nice is via being young, but we can\'t confirm that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe being furry and nice requires being white (statement8), but that\'s a different condition. \n\nWait, perhaps there\'s a way for Erin to be nice without being young? Let\'s think. \n\nSuppose Erin is nice. Then by statement16, she must be blue (which she is). Also, if she were furry and nice, she would have to be white (statement8), but she\'s blue. Therefore, if she is nice, she cannot be both furry and nice. So she can be nice but not furry. \n\nIs there any other constraints? Let\'s see. \n\nAlternatively, if she is smart and white, then she is furry (statement11). But she\'s blue, so not white. \n\nAlternatively, if she\'s blue and smart (statement10), then she is furry. \n\nBut to be smart, she needs to be young and blue (statement9). So unless she\'s young, she can\'t be smart. \n\nSo if she\'s not young, she\'s not smart. \n\nSo, if she is nice but not young, then she must be nice through some other way not mentioned. Since there\'s no other rule given, we can\'t assume that. \n\nTherefore, the only way we know of to be nice is being young. Since Erin isn\'t young (as we