ry is kind.\n\nThus, Gary is quiet, kind, red, round, young.\n\nNow, Rule 5: All quiet and kind things are white. Since Gary is quiet and kind, he must be white. But he is given as red, so that's a contradiction. So the system is inconsistent.\n\nIn an inconsistent system, any statement can be proven. So in this case, can we prove that Gary is not quiet? Well, since the system is inconsistent, we can derive both \"Gary is quiet\" (given) and \"Gary is not quiet\" (if we follow that contradiction leading to explosion). But in such cases, the answer would be that it's unknown, because the premises are inconsistent? Or perhaps the problem expects us to ignore the contradiction and proceed?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in assuming that all the rules must hold. Let me check again the rules and see if there's a different way to interpret them.\n\nWait, Rule 5 says \"All quiet, kind things are white.\" So if something is both quiet and kind, it must be white. But Gary is both, so he must be white. But he's red. So that's a problem. Therefore, the given data for Gary is conflicting with the rules. Since the problem states all these as given, perhaps we have to consider that the premises are inconsistent, so the question's statement can't be determined? Or maybe the answer is that it's false because the given fact says Gary is quiet, so the statement \"Gary is not quiet\" is false. But the contradiction suggests that perhaps the given fact is wrong? But in logic puzzles, typically the given facts are taken as true unless they conflict with the rules. Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is an error in my reasoning. Let me check again step by step.\n\nLet me re-express all the rules as implications:\n\n1. White \u2192 Round\n2. (Kind \u2227 White) \u2192 Green\n3. Young \u2192 Kind\n4. Round \u2192 Young\n5. (Quiet \u2227 Kind) \u2192 White\n6. (Quiet \u2227 Green) \u2192 Red\n7. (Young \u2227 Quiet) \u2192 Kind\n8. (Quiet \u2227 Red) \u2192 Kin