t 6). So Harry\'s quietness isn\'t given. Therefore, we can\'t assume he\'s quiet. \n\nTherefore, from point 10, only if Harry were both quiet and furry would he have to be white. But since we don\'t know if he\'s quiet, we can\'t conclude he\'s white. So Harry\'s whiteness is also unknown here. \n\nBut the question is about being round. None of the premises mention roundness at all. The attributes given are nice, quiet, white, young, furry, cold, etc., but "round" isn\'t part of any of the statements or the implications. \n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information provided about roundness for any of the individuals or through any of the rules, the answer must be that it\'s unknown. \n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any possible way that another attribute could imply roundness? For example, maybe being furry implies a shape? But the premises don\'t state anything like that. All furry things are cold, but that\'s about temperature, not shape. The other rules don\'t connect any attribute to roundness. So no, there\'s no connection. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be C) Unknown.\n']
