e cat then it likes the mouse." So, the \'it\' is the chaser, so the mouse (since mouse chases cat) must like the mouse. Wait, so the mouse likes itself? That seems possible. But maybe that\'s not the issue here. The important part here is that since the mouse chases the cat, then the mouse must like the mouse. Hmm, but maybe that\'s okay. Let\'s note that.\n\nSo from 16, since mouse chases cat, then mouse likes mouse. Not sure yet, but let\'s keep going.\n\nNext, statement 18: If something likes the mouse, then the mouse is big. Since the mouse likes itself (from above), that would mean the mouse is big. So if the mouse likes the mouse, then mouse is big. So that would make mouse big. Therefore, mouse is big?\n\nWait, but if mouse is big, then by statement 11 (All big things are blue), so the mouse would be blue. Then by statement 14 (All blue things are young), so mouse would be young. So that would make the mouse young, which would make the original statement "mouse is not young" false. But let\'s check if this is valid.\n\nWait, but let me retrace:\n\n1. Mouse chases cat → by 16 → mouse likes mouse.\n\n2. Since mouse likes mouse → by 18 → mouse is big.\n\n3. mouse is big → by 11 → mouse is blue.\n\n4. mouse is blue → by 14 → mouse is young.\n\nTherefore, the mouse is young. Therefore the statement "mouse is not young" is false. So the answer would be B) False?\n\nBut wait, there\'s another part. Let\'s check statement 19: "If something sees the mouse and the mouse is not blue then the mouse likes the cat." The cat sees the mouse (from 3). So the \'something\' here could be the cat. So if (cat sees mouse AND mouse is not blue), then mouse likes cat. But the mouse does not like the cat (from 6). Therefore, the antecedent must be false. Because if the antecedent were true, then the consequent (mouse likes cat) would have to be true, which contradicts statement 6. Therefore, the antecedent must be false. \n\nThe antecedent is: (cat sees mouse AND mouse is not blue).