at.\n\nThe question is about whether the cat likes the lion. Let\'s see if any of these statements directly or indirectly relate to the cat\'s liking towards the lion.\n\nLooking at the given facts:\n\nFrom fact 3, the cat likes the cow. But nothing here directly mentions the cat liking or not liking the lion. However, maybe there are implications through the other statements.\n\nLet me look at the implications step by step. Let\'s check each conditional statement to see if they can lead us to the conclusion.\n\nStarting with the cat\'s relationships:\n\n- The cat visits the lion (fact 4). Now, looking at the conditionals:\n\nFact 14 says: If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat. Wait, but that\'s about visiting the cat, not the lion. So maybe not directly applicable here.\n\nWait, the cat is visiting the lion. So the cat is the one doing the visiting here. Let me see if any conditionals are triggered by the cat\'s actions.\n\nLooking at fact 17: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel. Hmm, but that\'s if an entity likes the cat, then that entity chases the squirrel. Not sure how that helps yet.\n\nFact 19: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. Let\'s see, the lion itself chases the cat (fact 8), but does anything chase the lion? The squirrel likes the cow (fact 12), but not sure about chasing. Wait, maybe the cow or another animal?\n\nFact 21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat. The cow\'s roughness? The lion is rough (fact 10), but the cow\'s attributes? The cow\'s color is blue (fact5), but not mentioned as rough. So unless the cow is rough, which isn\'t stated. Wait, fact10 says the lion is rough. So the cow isn\'t necessarily rough, so fact21 might not apply here.\n\nWait, maybe the cow is not rough. Since the lion is rough, but the cow\'s roughness isn\'t mentioned. So fact21 is a conditional that only applies if the cow is rough, which we don\'t know. So that\'s maybe not helpful.\n\nLet me go ba