ly direct info is that the cat likes the cow (fact3). The cow likes the lion (fact6). The squirrel likes the cow (fact12). The lion chases the cat (fact8). The cat is big and cold. The lion is rough.\n\nWait, maybe there\'s a chain here. The cat likes the cow (fact3), and the cow likes the lion (fact6). Does that imply the cat likes the lion? Not necessarily, unless there\'s a transitive relationship, which isn\'t stated here. So that\'s an assumption we can\'t make.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps through some conditional that requires the cat to like the lion. Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at all the conditionals again:\n\nFact15: If something likes the squirrel and is big, then the squirrel likes the lion. The cat is big (fact1), so if the cat likes the squirrel, then the squirrel would like the lion. But the problem doesn\'t say the cat likes the squirrel, so this is a maybe.\n\nFact17: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel. Suppose the lion likes the cat (maybe from fact14, since the lion visits the cat, so the lion must like the cat). Then the lion would have to chase the squirrel (fact17). But the lion already chases the cat (fact8), but that\'s okay. However, this doesn\'t give us info about the cat\'s feelings towards the lion.\n\nFact19: If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow. But since we don\'t know if the lion is cold or not, and who chases the lion, this might not help.\n\nFact21: If the cow is rough, then it chases the cat. Since the lion is rough (fact10), but the cow\'s roughness isn\'t mentioned. Unless the cow being rough is inferred, but no. So not helpful.\n\nHmm, maybe I need to check if there\'s any contradiction if we assume the cat does like the lion, or if it doesn\'t.\n\nSuppose the cat likes the lion. Is that possible without contradicting any given facts?\n\nWell, there\'s no fact that says the cat cannot like the lion. The only explicit likes are the cat likes the cow, the cow likes the lion, 