ion. \n\nTherefore, Charlie cannot be smart. \n\nTherefore, Charlie is not smart. \n\nSo that gives us a new fact: Charlie is not smart. \n\nNow, how does that affect our scenarios?\n\nIn Scenario 1 where Charlie is big (and not blue):\n\n- From premise 14, he would not be smart (which aligns with our new conclusion that he\'s not smart). \n\n- So that\'s okay. \n\nIn Scenario 2 where he is not big:\n\n- His smartness isn\'t determined by premise 14, but we know he can\'t be smart (from the above), so he must not be smart. \n\nWait, but how do we know he can\'t be smart? Because if he were smart, that would require him to be quiet (premise 9), which requires big and blue, leading to contradiction. Therefore, he can\'t be smart. So regardless of being big or not, he\'s not smart. \n\nSo that doesn\'t add new info about his bigness. \n\nTherefore, the key point is that Charlie could be big (as long as not blue) or not big. \n\nIs there any other constraint? Let me think.\n\nWait, perhaps we can consider Charlie\'s roundness. If he is big, he could be round or not, but if he is round, then he must be big (from premise 15). \n\nBut since roundness isn\'t mentioned, maybe there\'s a way to link it. \n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s any other rule about being big. \n\nPremise 15: Round things are big. So if Charlie is round, then he must be big. But if he is not round, he could be big or not. \n\nTherefore, unless we know if he is round, we can\'t determine. \n\nBut since we don\'t have info about Charlie\'s roundness, that doesn\'t help. \n\nHmm. \n\nAlternatively, let me think of all possibilities for Charlie\'s attributes and see if any lead to contradictions:\n\nCase 1: Charlie is big and not blue.\n\n- Then, he is quiet? No, since not blue. So not quiet. \n\n- Therefore, smartness: He is not smart (from premise 14: big and young → not smart). Which is okay. \n\n- Roundness: If he is round, then he must be big (which he is), so that\'s okay. But roundness isn\