fore, Erin is rough.\n\nNow, fact 13 states "Rough, nice things are round." Since Erin is both rough and nice, this would mean she must be round. Wait, so does that mean Erin is round?\n\nHold on, but there\'s another thing. Fact 16 says "All round things are green." If Erin is round, then she must be green. But do we have any information about Erin\'s color besides red?\n\nLooking back, the only things mentioned about Erin are that she\'s red (fact 3) and from the deductions, nice, cold, rough. But there\'s no direct statement about her being green. However, if she is round, then according to fact 16, she must be green. But Erin is already red. Wait, can something be both red and green? Let me check if there\'s any conflict here.\n\nLooking at Gary: Gary is green, red, and rough, etc. So Gary is both green and red. The problem didn\'t say that an object can\'t have multiple colors. So maybe that\'s okay. So if Erin is round, then she must be green. But Erin is already red. The problem doesn\'t state that being red precludes being green, so that\'s possible. But the question is just about whether Erin is round. The fact that she would have to be green if round might not contradict anything else unless there\'s another rule.\n\nWait, but the problem is asking if the statement "Erin is round" is true, false, or unknown. Let me retrace the steps again to make sure I didn\'t make a mistake.\n\nStarting over:\n\n1. Erin is red (given).\n2. Red things are nice (fact 12) → Erin is nice.\n3. Since Erin is red and nice, by fact 15, she must be cold.\n4. Cold things are rough (fact14) → Erin is rough.\n5. Now, Erin is rough and nice (from steps 2 and 4). Fact13 says rough and nice things are round → so Erin must be round.\n\nTherefore, according to these deductions, Erin must be round. But wait, then fact16 says all round things are green. So Erin would also have to be green. However, the original data doesn\'t mention Erin being green. But the problem doesn\'t say that being