the rabbit. Not sure yet.\n\n16. If something is big then it visits the cow. (All big things visit the cow. The bald eagle is big (1), cow is big (4), dog is big (6), so they must all visit the cow. Wait, but does that mean they visit the cow? Let me note that.)\n\n17. If something sees the rabbit then the rabbit visits the bald eagle. Wait, the bald eagle sees the rabbit (3), so that would trigger this condition, so the rabbit must visit the bald eagle. Which matches statement 10. So that's consistent.\n\n18. If something is blue then it likes the rabbit. (From the previous, the dog is blue (from 12), so the dog must like the rabbit?)\n\n19. If something likes the cow and the cow is young then it is blue. Hmm. The bald eagle likes the cow (statement 2). If the cow were young, then the bald eagle would have to be blue. But we know the cow is big (4), but is it young? Not sure yet.\n\nOkay, let's start breaking this down step by step.\n\nFirst, from the bald eagle seeing the rabbit (statement 3), according to conditional 11, that means the rabbit must be big. So the rabbit is big. That's a fact we can add.\n\nNext, the dog likes the bald eagle (statement 8). So conditional 12 says that if something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue. Therefore, the dog must be blue. So the dog is blue. So that's another fact.\n\nNow, conditional 18 says if something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. Since the dog is blue (from above), the dog must like the rabbit. So now we know the dog likes the rabbit. But that's not directly related to the question about the bald eagle liking the rabbit. Let's note that.\n\nNow, conditional 16: If something is big, it visits the cow. All big things (bald eagle, cow, dog) must visit the cow. Therefore:\n\n- Bald eagle visits the cow.\n\n- Cow visits itself? Wait, but \"visits the cow\" would mean another entity visits the cow. The cow visiting itself isn't specified, but the condition is about \"something\" (any entity) being big implies it vis