t 2). If the cow were young, then the bald eagle would have to be blue. But we know the cow is big (4), but is it young? Not sure yet.\n\nOkay, let\'s start breaking this down step by step.\n\nFirst, from the bald eagle seeing the rabbit (statement 3), according to conditional 11, that means the rabbit must be big. So the rabbit is big. That\'s a fact we can add.\n\nNext, the dog likes the bald eagle (statement 8). So conditional 12 says that if something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue. Therefore, the dog must be blue. So the dog is blue. So that\'s another fact.\n\nNow, conditional 18 says if something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. Since the dog is blue (from above), the dog must like the rabbit. So now we know the dog likes the rabbit. But that\'s not directly related to the question about the bald eagle liking the rabbit. Let\'s note that.\n\nNow, conditional 16: If something is big, it visits the cow. All big things (bald eagle, cow, dog) must visit the cow. Therefore:\n\n- Bald eagle visits the cow.\n\n- Cow visits itself? Wait, but "visits the cow" would mean another entity visits the cow. The cow visiting itself isn\'t specified, but the condition is about "something" (any entity) being big implies it visits the cow. So the cow, being big, must visit the cow? That seems a bit odd, but maybe it\'s okay. The problem might not care about self-visits, but the logic would require that the cow does visit the cow. Hmm, but maybe that\'s acceptable. Similarly, the dog being big must visit the cow, so dog visits cow. And the bald eagle visits the cow.\n\nSo now, we can say:\n\n- Bald eagle visits cow.\n\n- Dog visits cow.\n\n- Cow visits cow (though perhaps that\'s irrelevant).\n\nNow, let\'s look at conditional 13: If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young. \n\nFirst, do we know if anything likes the rabbit? The dog likes the rabbit (from conditional 18, because dog is blue, so that\'s a fact). So the dog likes the rabbit. Also, does the rabbit visit the cow? The rabbit visits the bald eagle (statement 10), but does it visit the cow? From conditional 16, the rabbit is big (from earlier), so if the rabbit is big, then it must visit the cow. Wait, the rabbit is big (from conditional 11 and statement 3), so the rabbit must visit the cow. Therefore, the rabbit does visit the cow. \n\nTherefore, the conditions for conditional 13 are met: something (the dog) likes the rabbit, and the rabbit does visit the cow (because it\'s big). Therefore, the cow must be young. \n\nWait, that\'s a big conclusion. Let me confirm again:\n\n- The rabbit is big (from 11 and 3), so it must visit the cow (from 16). So rabbit visits cow. \n\n- The dog likes the rabbit (from 18 because dog is blue). \n\nTherefore, conditional 13\'s antecedent is true (something likes the rabbit (the dog) and the rabbit visits the cow (yes)), so the consequent must hold: the cow is young. \n\nTherefore, the cow is young. Now, that\'s a new fact. So the cow is young. \n\nNow, looking at conditional 19: If something likes the cow and the cow is young, then it is blue. \n\nWe know the cow is young (from above), and the bald eagle likes the cow (statement 2). Therefore, since the bald eagle likes the cow and the cow is young, then the bald eagle must be blue. \n\nSo the bald eagle is blue. \n\nNow, going back to conditional 18 again: if something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. The bald eagle is blue (from above), so the bald eagle must like the rabbit. \n\nWait! That\'s the question. The question is whether the bald eagle likes the rabbit. \n\nBecause the bald eagle is blue (from conditional 19), then according to conditional 18, it must like the rabbit. \n\nTherefore, the bald eagle does like the rabbit. So the statement "The bald eagle likes the rabbit" is true. Therefore, the answer would be A) True. \n\nBut let me check again to make sure I didn\'t make any mistakes in the chain.\n\nLet me retrace:\n\n1. Bald eagle se