nThen, from rule 7 (Quiet \u2192 Blue), since Harry is quiet, he must be blue. So Harry is blue. \n\nNow, since Harry is blue, from rule 1 (All blue things are rough), Harry must be rough. So Harry is rough. \n\nNow, from rule 8 (Rough \u2192 Kind), since Harry is rough, he must be kind. So Harry is kind. \n\nWait, so now Harry has attributes: Big, Quiet, Blue, Rough, Kind. \n\nNow, let's check other rules. Since Harry is kind, from rule 5 (All kind things are furry), he must be furry. \n\nWait, that's a key point. Rule 5 says that if something is kind, then it is furry. So since Harry is kind, he must be furry. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"Harry is not furry\" would be false. Because according to the rules, Harry is kind, so he must be furry, making the statement false. \n\nWait, but let me check again to make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me retrace each step.\n\nStarting with Harry's given attribute: Big. \n\n1. Big \u2192 Quiet (rule 6). So Harry is Quiet. \n\n2. Quiet \u2192 Blue (rule 7). So Harry is Blue. \n\n3. Blue \u2192 Rough (rule 1). So Harry is Rough. \n\n4. Rough \u2192 Kind (rule 8). So Harry is Kind. \n\n5. Kind \u2192 Furry (rule 5). Therefore, Harry must be Furry. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"Harry is not furry\" is false. So the answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possibility that any of these steps could be contradicted or have exceptions. For example, maybe some rules have conditions that aren't met here. Let me see:\n\n- The rules are all universal statements, so they should apply unless there's a contradiction. \n\nIs there any rule that would prevent Harry from being furry? Let's see. The only way Harry wouldn't be furry is if he's not kind. But since he's rough, which requires him to be kind (rule 8), he must be kind, so he must be furry. \n\nWait a second, let me check the rules again to ensure there's no conflicting information. \n\nAlso, let's check other attributes. For example, does being big and