nd.\n4. Gary is cold. Gary is kind. Gary is young.\n5. If someone is kind then they are cold. (So, Kind → Cold)\n6. All green people are quiet. (Green → Quiet)\n7. If Erin is green and Erin is cold then Erin is round. (Erin is G ∧ C → R)\n8. All cold, smart people are round. Wait, the problem didn\'t mention "smart" anywhere else. Hmm, maybe "smart" is a typo? Let me check the original problem again. The user wrote: "All cold, smart people are round." But in the context given, the attributes listed are cold, kind, quiet, green, round, young. Maybe "smart" is a mistake? Or perhaps it\'s a new attribute not mentioned elsewhere. Since it\'s part of the given premises, I have to consider it as is. So maybe "cold and smart" implies two attributes. But since "smart" isn\'t mentioned in the people\'s attributes, maybe this premise is irrelevant? Or maybe "smart" is a typo for another attribute? Hmm, this could be a problem. Let me note that and see if it affects anything later.\n\n9. All quiet, kind people are green. (Quiet ∧ Kind → Green)\n10. Quiet people are kind. (Quiet → Kind)\n\nThe question is about Gary being quiet. Let\'s see what we know about Gary from the context:\n\nFrom point 4: Gary is cold, kind, and young. \n\nNow, the rules. Let\'s see if we can derive anything about Gary\'s quietness.\n\nFirst, let\'s look at the rules that might link to being quiet. \n\nRule 10 says Quiet → Kind. So if someone is quiet, they must be kind. But the converse isn\'t necessarily true. However, rule 9 says that if someone is both quiet and kind, then they are green. So Quiet ∧ Kind → Green.\n\nAlso, rule 6 says Green → Quiet. So being green makes you quiet, but not necessarily the other way around.\n\nLooking at Gary\'s attributes: he is kind (from point 4) and cold (from point 4). \n\nSince Gary is kind, according to rule 5 (If someone is kind then they are cold), that\'s satisfied because he is cold. So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, the question is whether Gary is quiet. Let\