e any other conditions that might affect this. Let's see:\n\nStatement 3: Smart, cold people are white. Erin is smart and cold (given), so she must be white. So Erin is white. Then looking at statement 1: If Erin is white, then she is blue. Since she is white, this implies she must be blue. Therefore, Erin is also blue.\n\nNow, statement 7 says if someone is blue, they are smart. Since Erin is blue, that's consistent because she's already smart. No problem there.\n\nNow, what about statement 2: If someone is round, they are blue. Since we concluded Erin is round, then she must be blue. Which she already is, so that's okay.\n\nAdditionally, statement 5: Furry people are cold. Erin is furry and cold, so that's consistent.\n\nNow, is there any rule that would prevent Erin from being round? Let me think. The only way Erin wouldn't be round is if one of the premises in the statements leading to her being round is negated. But since she is smart and furry (from being smart), the conclusion must hold unless there's some exception.\n\nWait, the statement 6 is \"Smart, furry people are round\". So all smart and furry people are round. Since Erin is both, she must be round. There's no exception mentioned here. Therefore, Erin must be round, so the statement \"Erin is not round\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check another angle. Let's look at the other attributes. For instance, Fiona is blue, cold, furry, white. Since Fiona is blue, by statement 7, she must be smart. But Fiona's attributes don't mention being smart, but according to the rules, if she is blue, she must be smart. So maybe Fiona is smart but that wasn't stated. But the problem says \"Fiona is blue. Fiona is cold. Fiona is furry. Fiona is white.\" So maybe that's an oversight? Wait, but the rules might require that. Since Fiona is blue, she must be smart (from statement 7). So Fiona is actually smart, even if it wasn't listed. But the given info might not mention all attributes, just so