ere's a rule that says something can't be two colors? The given rules don't mention that. So perhaps that's allowed. But then, if he's both blue and white, then according to point 9 (all white things are kind), he would also be kind. Wait, but Charlie's kindness isn't stated, but if he's white, then he must be kind. So that would make Charlie kind as well.\n\nBut does that affect anything? Let's see. If Charlie is green, blue, white, rough, big (from blue), and kind (from white). Then, does that cause any conflict with other rules?\n\nLooking at point 10: If something is green and kind, then it is round. Charlie would be both green and kind (since he's white, so kind via point 9), so that would require him to be round. But the original info doesn't say anything about Charlie's roundness. But that's okay, unless there's a contradiction elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that being both blue and white might conflict with another rule? Let's check. The rules don't say that something can't be both blue and white. So maybe that's okay. So in that case, Charlie being rough would lead to being white, which is acceptable, but then he's also blue. So that's allowed unless there's a rule against that.\n\nWait, but point 14 says \"Blue things are big.\" Since Charlie is blue, he must be big. Which is okay because being big doesn't conflict with anything here.\n\nSo putting this all together: Charlie is green \u2192 blue (point 13) \u2192 rough (point8) \u2192 white (point16). So Charlie is rough. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is not rough\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait a second, but let me check again if there's any way Charlie could not be rough. The only way would be if he's not blue, but since he's green, he must be blue. So he must be blue, which makes him rough. So there's no escape from that conclusion. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nHmm, but let me think again. Maybe there's a loop here. Suppose Charlie is green, so he