and the lion chases the rabbit, then the rabbit chases the mouse.\n\nWe know that the mouse chases the lion (premise 2). But does the lion chase the rabbit? There\'s no information given that the lion chases the rabbit. The lion\'s actions are not mentioned except being kind and seeing the mouse.\n\nTherefore, the antecedent of premise 14 requires both "something chases the lion" and "lion chases the rabbit". The first part is true (mouse chases lion), but the second part (lion chases rabbit) is unknown. Therefore, premise 14 doesn\'t activate, so we can\'t conclude the rabbit chases the mouse.\n\nHmm, so that\'s not helpful here.\n\nLooking for other premises. Let\'s see if any of the conditionals involve the mouse seeing the rabbit.\n\nPremise 12 says that kind things see the mouse. Since the rabbit is kind, it sees the mouse. But that\'s the rabbit\'s perspective.\n\nIs there any rule that would make the mouse see the rabbit? Let\'s see:\n\nLooking at premise 15: If something is kind and sees the mouse, then it sees the lion.\n\nThe mouse itself is not kind, so that doesn\'t apply. The rabbit is kind and sees the mouse, so it must see the lion (which we already derived).\n\nThe tiger is now kind and sees the mouse (since it\'s kind and premise 12 requires it to see the mouse), so the tiger must see the lion (by premise 15).\n\nWait, tiger is kind (from premise 9, since mouse saw it), and the tiger sees the mouse (premise 12), so by premise 15, the tiger must see the lion.\n\nSo tiger sees lion.\n\nBut that\'s another point.\n\nNow, is there any rule that would require the mouse to see the rabbit?\n\nLooking at premise 8: If the mouse sees the rabbit, then the rabbit must be cold. We already know the rabbit is cold, so that\'s okay. But that doesn\'t force the mouse to see the rabbit.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse doesn\'t see the rabbit, that\'s allowed as long as the rabbit remains cold (which it is). So the mouse could or could not see the rabbit without violating any rules.\n\nWait, but is there any other premise that could require the mouse to see the rabbit?\n\nLooking at premise 13: If something is blue and kind, it sees the rabbit. The rabbit is blue and kind, so the rabbit sees the rabbit. The tiger is blue and kind, so the tiger sees the rabbit. But the mouse isn\'t blue and kind, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nPremise 14: Not applicable here.\n\nPremise 15: As above, applies to tiger and rabbit, but not the mouse.\n\nHmm. Let me see if there\'s any other angle.\n\nSuppose the mouse does see the rabbit. Is that possible?\n\nIf the mouse sees the rabbit, then according to premise 8, the rabbit must be cold. Which it is. So that\'s okay. So that\'s allowed.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse does not see the rabbit, that\'s also possible, as long as other conditions are met.\n\nWait, but is there any rule that would make it necessary for the mouse to see the rabbit?\n\nLooking through all premises, I don\'t see any. The only connection is through premise 11, which requires the mouse to see the tiger. But that\'s all.\n\nTherefore, whether the mouse sees the rabbit or not is not determined by the given premises. So the statement "the mouse does not see the rabbit" could be true or false, but there\'s no information to decide. Hence, the answer would be unknown (C).\n\nWait, but wait! Let me check again. Are there any other premises that might imply it?\n\nWait, the rabbit sees the mouse (from premise 12). So the rabbit is seeing the mouse. But does that mean the mouse is being seen by the rabbit, but the mouse\'s perspective on the rabbit isn\'t necessarily reciprocal?\n\nIn other words, just because the rabbit sees the mouse doesn\'t mean the mouse sees the rabbit. So that\'s not an issue.\n\nAlternatively, is there any transitivity? Like if X sees Y and Y sees Z, then X sees Z? Not that I can see in the premises.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, perhaps there\'s an implicit assumption that seeing is mutual? But the problem doesn\'t 