ald eagle, then the rabbit must visit the bald eagle. \n\nNow, looking at the bald eagle: the bald eagle does visit the rabbit (statement 4). Does the bald eagle need the bald eagle? No, the bald eagle needs the lion (statement 3). Wait, so the "it needs the bald eagle" part would be about X needing the bald eagle. So for the bald eagle (X is bald eagle), the bald eagle does visit the rabbit, but does it need the bald eagle? No, the need is for the lion. Therefore, the condition (X visits rabbit AND X needs bald eagle) is not satisfied for X being the bald eagle. Therefore, this condition doesn\'t trigger anything here. \n\nBut wait, maybe there\'s another X? For example, the lion? Let\'s see: does the lion visit the rabbit? The lion visits the bald eagle and the dog (statements 10 and 11), but does the lion visit the rabbit? Not according to the given statements. The rabbit visits the lion (statement 14), but the lion doesn\'t visit the rabbit. So maybe no other X satisfies this condition. Therefore, this conditional might not be triggered here. \n\nThird conditional: If something is rough → it visits the lion. Since the rabbit is rough (from first conditional), then rabbit must visit the lion. Which is already stated in statement 14. So that\'s consistent. \n\nFourth conditional: If (dog needs the rabbit AND rabbit sees the bald eagle) → then bald eagle sees the dog. \n\nWe need to check if the antecedent is true. The dog needs the rabbit? The lion needs the dog and the rabbit (statements 8 and 9), but does the dog need the rabbit? The context doesn\'t say that. The dog\'s needs aren\'t mentioned except in statement 8 (lion needs dog). So "dog needs the rabbit" is not stated, so the first part of the antecedent is false. Therefore, the implication holds (since implication is only false when antecedent is true and consequent is false). So this conditional doesn\'t give us anything. \n\nFifth conditional: If (rabbit visits the lion AND rabbit needs the dog) → then t