\'t know. So the fact that we don\'t know B (the cat chases the squirrel) doesn\'t let us infer A is false (the cat likes itself). Therefore, we can\'t be sure. So perhaps the answer is unknown (C).\n\nHmm, this is confusing. Let me try to structure it:\n\nThe question is: Is "Cat does not like cat" true?\n\nWe need to see if any of the given info leads to that conclusion.\n\n1. The cat\'s likes are only stated as liking the cow. No mention of self-liking.\n\n2. The fifth condition: If something (including the cat) likes the cat, then it must chase the squirrel.\n\nIf the cat does like itself, then it must chase the squirrel. But there\'s no information given about the cat chasing the squirrel. So, either:\n\n- The cat does like itself, and therefore chases the squirrel (but this isn\'t mentioned, so maybe it\'s possible but not confirmed), or\n\n- The cat does not like itself, so the condition is irrelevant.\n\nSince the problem asks if the statement "the cat does not like the cat" is true, we can\'t confirm it\'s true because there\'s no direct contradiction. The condition only imposes a consequence if the cat does like itself, but that consequence isn\'t violated (since we don\'t know if the cat chases the squirrel). Therefore, the cat could either like itself (with the consequence of chasing the squirrel, which is unknown) or not like itself. Since both possibilities are open, the answer is unknown (C).\n\nWait, but maybe the fact that there\'s no information about the cat chasing the squirrel allows us to assume it doesn\'t, thus making the antecedent (the cat liking itself) false? Because if it were true, then the consequence would have to be true. Since the consequence isn\'t stated, perhaps we can infer the antecedent is false? \n\nIn logic, if we have "A implies B", and we know that B is false, then we can conclude A is false. But here, B (the cat chases the squirrel) is not stated to be false. It\'s just not stated. So we can\'t assume B is false. Therefor