(statement 14). Then, since it\'s blue, by statement 12, it must chase the squirrel. Which it does (statement 1). So that\'s consistent. \n\nHowever, the bald eagle being round also requires that it eats the squirrel (statement 8). Wait, the bald eagle is round → it must eat the squirrel. But the bald eagle already eats the bear (statement 2). So that\'s possible. \n\nWait, but if the bald eagle eats the squirrel, then by statement 10, the squirrel must be green. But the squirrel is red. So again, that creates a contradiction. Because the squirrel is red, so it can\'t be green. \n\nTherefore, if the bald eagle is round, then the squirrel must be green (contradiction), so the bald eagle cannot be round. Therefore, the bald eagle is not round. \n\nAh, this is a crucial point. Let me go through this again:\n\nAssume the bald eagle is round. \n\nThen by statement 11, it chases the lion. \n\nBy statement 14, since it chases the lion, it must be blue. \n\nBy statement 12, since it\'s blue, it must chase the squirrel (which it does, per statement 1). \n\nAlso, being round, by statement 8, the bald eagle must eat the squirrel. \n\nBut if the bald eagle eats the squirrel, then by statement 10, the squirrel must be green. \n\nBut the squirrel is red (statement 6). So that\'s a contradiction. Therefore, our assumption that the bald eagle is round must be false. Therefore, the bald eagle is not round. \n\nThat\'s an important conclusion. So the bald eagle is not round. \n\nNow, going back to statement 16: "If something visits the bald eagle and it is not round then it visits the lion."\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 7). The bald eagle is not round (as we just deduced). So the squirrel visits the bald eagle and the bald eagle is not round. Therefore, by statement 16, the squirrel must visit the lion. \n\nBut the squirrel\'s visits: it already visits the bald eagle, and now also must visit the lion. \n\nNow, the bear visits the lion (statement 4). The squirrel also now must visit the lion. \n\nNow, what about the lion\'s roundness? Let\'s see.\n\nSuppose the lion is round. Then by statement 8: If something is round, it eats the squirrel. So the lion (being round) would have to eat the squirrel. \n\nBut does the lion eat the squirrel? The problem doesn\'t state that directly, but let\'s see. \n\nThe bald eagle eats the bear (statement 2). The lion\'s eating habits are only mentioned in statement 5: the lion does not eat the bald eagle. \n\nIf the lion is round, then it must eat the squirrel (statement 8). So that\'s a possibility. \n\nBut if the lion does eat the squirrel, then by statement 10, the squirrel must be green. However, the squirrel is red (statement 6), so that would be a contradiction again. \n\nTherefore, if the lion is round, then the squirrel must be green (contradiction). So the lion cannot be round. Therefore, the statement "The lion is not round" would be true. \n\nWait, let me verify that again:\n\nAssume the lion is round. Then by statement 8, the lion eats the squirrel. \n\nBy statement 10, if something eats the squirrel (the lion in this case), then the squirrel must be green. But the squirrel is red. Contradiction. Therefore, the assumption that the lion is round must be false. So the lion cannot be round. Therefore, the statement "The lion is not round" is true. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True. \n\nWait, but let me check if there are other possibilities. \n\nAlternatively, could the lion be round without eating the squirrel? But if it\'s round, then by statement 8, it must eat the squirrel. So there\'s no way around that. Therefore, the conclusion holds. \n\nSo the answer should be A) True.\n'] 29%|██▉       | 176/600 [4:46:29<14:39:22, 124.44s/it]
