the tiger's color is unknown unless other info.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something is red, then it eats the bald eagle. The bald eagle is already eaten by the dog. But maybe the tiger is red? Let's see.\n\nWait, let's try to see if the tiger is red. Suppose the tiger is red. Then by statement 10, it must eat the tiger. Again, that's impossible. So the tiger can't be red. Therefore, the tiger is not red, so its color must be something else, maybe green? But we don't have info on that yet.\n\nAlternatively, the tiger could be another color, but the problem doesn't mention colors other than green and red? Wait, the statements mention green and red in the conditionals, but maybe there are other colors. Hmm, but maybe not. Let me check the statements again.\n\nLooking at the conditionals:\n\n- If something is green and eats the cow, then it doesn't see the bald eagle (statement 8).\n\n- If something is green and needs the dog, then it needs the bald eagle (statement 12).\n\n- If something is red, then it eats the tiger (statement 10).\n\n- If something is red, it eats the bald eagle (statement 14).\n\n- If something is young, it's red (statement 15).\n\nSo the colors mentioned are green and red. So perhaps all entities are either green, red, or another color not specified. But maybe we can infer.\n\nWait, the bald eagle is red (as established). The cow's color isn't mentioned. The dog's color? Let's see.\n\nThe dog eats the bald eagle. Suppose the dog is green. Let's see what that implies.\n\nIf the dog is green, then maybe check if there are any conditions involving green. Let's see:\n\nStatement 8: If green and eats cow, then doesn't see bald eagle. The dog doesn't eat the cow, the dog eats the bald eagle. So that's okay.\n\nStatement 12: If something is green and needs the dog, then it needs the bald eagle. Not sure yet.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog's color isn't determined yet. Let's see if we can find out.\n\nAnother angle: The question is about whether the