d cold, then it is furry. But Erin is cold, but is she rough? From the context, Gary is rough, but Erin\'s roughness isn\'t directly stated. However, rule 13 says "Cold things are rough." Wait, rule 13 is a general statement. So all cold things are rough. Since Erin is cold, that means Erin must be rough. Because if something is cold, then it is rough. So Erin is rough. \n\nWait, that\'s important. So Erin is cold, so by rule 13, she\'s rough. So now Erin has blue, cold, round, and rough. \n\nNow, using rule 5: If something is rough and cold, then it is furry. Since Erin is both rough and cold, then according to rule 5, she must be furry. So that would mean Erin is furry, so the answer would be A) True?\n\nBut wait, let me check again. Because maybe there\'s a contradiction or exception. Let me go step by step.\n\nFirst, confirm Erin\'s traits:\n\n- Blue (given)\n- Cold (given)\n- Round (given)\n- From rule 13 (Cold things are rough), so Erin is rough.\n\nNow, rule 5: If (rough ∧ cold) → furry. Since Erin is both, that implies she must be furry. So that would make the statement "Erin is furry" true. But let me check all other rules to see if there\'s any conflicting information.\n\nWait, but let me check if there are any other constraints that might negate that. For instance, maybe another rule says that blue, round things can\'t be furry? Let me look again.\n\nLooking at the rules:\n\nRule 12: All blue, round things are not quiet. Erin is blue and round, so she\'s not quiet. But that\'s okay, since quietness isn\'t related to fur here.\n\nRule 9: If Fiona is furry then Fiona is blue. Fiona is quiet, but that\'s separate.\n\nRule 7: If Dave is blue then he\'s furry. But Dave is already stated to be furry, and his blueness isn\'t given, so that\'s maybe a conditional but not directly relevant here.\n\nRule 6: Quiet and big things are not round. Fiona is quiet, but unless she\'s big, it doesn\'t apply. Fiona\'s size isn\'t mentioned.\n\nRule 8: All quiet, blue things are big. Fiona is quiet but not necessarily blue, so again, not directly relevant here.\n\nRule 10: If something is quiet, then it is cold. Fiona is quiet, so Fiona must be cold. But that doesn\'t affect Erin.\n\nRule 11: All big things are cold. Dave is big, so he\'s cold. But Dave\'s fur is already stated, so that\'s okay.\n\nNow, going back to Erin. Since she is rough and cold, by rule 5, she must be furry. So that would make her furry. But wait, the problem says "Erin is blue. Erin is cold. Erin is round." There\'s no mention of her being furry, but according to the rules, she must be. Therefore, the answer would be A) True?\n\nWait, but let me check again. Is there a possibility that rule 5\'s condition is met, but another rule contradicts that conclusion?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in applying rule 13. Rule 13 says "Cold things are rough." So all cold things must be rough. Since Erin is cold, she must be rough. So that\'s correct.\n\nTherefore, Erin is rough and cold, so by rule 5, she must be furry. So the answer should be True (A).\n\nBut wait, let me check if there\'s a rule that says blue things can\'t be furry or something. Let me see:\n\nLooking through the rules again, the only rules that mention fur are:\n\n- Dave is furry (given).\n- Rule 5: rough and cold → furry.\n- Rule 7: If Dave is blue, then he\'s furry (but Dave is already furry, so that\'s okay).\n- Rule 9: If Fiona is furry, then she\'s blue. But Fiona\'s furry status isn\'t given.\n\nSo no rule prohibits Erin from being furry. So according to the given rules, Erin must be furry because of rule 5. Therefore, the statement "Erin is furry" is true. So the answer is A) True?\n\nWait a second, but the options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The problem says "If something is rough and cold then it is furry." So that\'s a conditional. So whenever something is rough AND cold, it must be furry. Since Erin is both, she must be furry. Therefore, the conclusion is that Erin must be furry, so the statement is true. So the answer is A.\n\nHmm, but maybe I missed something. Let me see the original statements again.\n\nWait, the problem says "All blue, round things are not quiet." Erin is blue and round, so she\'s not quiet. That\'s okay, but maybe another rule?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules are structured such that some conditions override others? Like, maybe being blue and round affects fur somehow?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that says "If something is blue and round, then it\'s not furry", but I don\'t see that. Let me check all rules again:\n\nThe given statements:\n\n1. Dave is big. Dave is furry.\n\n2. Erin is blue. Erin is cold. Erin is round.\n\n3. Fiona is quiet.\n\n4. Gary is rough.\n\n5. If something is rough and cold then it is furry.\n\n6. Quiet, big things are not round.\n\n7. If Dave is blue then Dave is furry.\n\n8. All quiet, blue things are big.\n\n9. If Fiona is furry then Fiona is blue.\n\n10. If something is quiet then it is cold.\n\n11. All big things are cold.\n\n12. All blue, round things are not quiet.\n\n13. Cold things are rough.\n\nSo, looking through all of these, none of them say anything that would negate Erin being furry. The only thing that would stop her from being furry is if there was a rule that says "If something is blue and round, it can\'t be furry," but that\'s not present. So according to rule 5, she must be furry because she is rough and cold, and that\'s enforced by the rules. So the answer should be A.\n\nWait, but the problem says "Erin is cold" which via rule 13 makes her rough, so that\'s correct. So then the conclusion is that Erin is furry. So the statement is true, so answer A.\n\nBut wait, maybe there\'s a mistake in assuming that rule 5 applies here. Let me check again. The rule is "If something is rough and cold then it is furry." So the antecedent is met for Erin (she is both), so the consequent must hold. Therefore, she must be furry. So yes, that