      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this problem step by step. Hmm, the question is whether the dog is young. The options are True, False, or Unknown. Alright, let me go through all the given information first and see what I can deduce.\n\nFirst, let's list out all the facts provided:\n\n1. The bald eagle is cold.\n2. The bald eagle is nice.\n3. The bald eagle likes the cow.\n4. The bald eagle likes the tiger.\n5. The bald eagle sees the cow.\n6. The bald eagle sees the dog.\n7. The bald eagle sees the tiger.\n8. The bald eagle visits the cow.\n9. The cow is young.\n10. The cow visits the bald eagle.\n11. The dog sees the bald eagle.\n12. The dog visits the tiger.\n13. The tiger is blue.\n14. The tiger is cold.\n15. The tiger is young.\n16. The tiger visits the cow.\n\nNow, the conditional statements:\n\n- If something is big and it visits the bald eagle, then it visits the cow. (Let me note this as Rule 1)\n- If something likes the tiger and the tiger is big, then it is big. (Rule 2)\n- If something visits the cow and the cow is young, then it is big. (Rule 3)\n- If something visits the tiger, then it is young. (Rule 4)\n- If the tiger likes the dog, then the tiger visits the bald eagle. (Rule 5)\n- If something is big, then it visits the dog. (Rule 6)\n- If something visits the dog, then it is cold. (Rule 7)\n- If something is young, then it likes the tiger. (Rule 8)\n\nThe question is about whether the dog is young. Let's see what we can find.\n\nFirst, looking at the direct statements, the dog's age isn't directly mentioned. The cow is young (fact 9), the tiger is young (fact 15), but the dog's age isn't given. So maybe we need to use the rules to infer.\n\nLet me check the rules that could relate to the dog's youth. Rule 4 says that if something visits the tiger, then it is young. Now, looking at the facts, the dog visits the tiger (fact 12). So according to Rule 4, since the dog visits the tiger, that should mean the dog is young. Wait, that would make the ans