ions:\n\nFirst, Rule 2: Round ∧ Cold → Red. Since Bob has both Round and Cold, this implies he must be Red. Therefore, according to this rule, Bob must be Red. So that would make the statement "Bob is red" true. But wait, let\'s check if there are any other rules that might contradict this or affect it.\n\nRule 6 says: If something is round, then it is red. (Round → Red). So even if he wasn\'t cold, just being round would make him red. Wait, but Rule 2 is a more specific case (round and cold → red), but Rule 6 is a general case. Wait, but actually, Rule 6 is a more general statement. Wait, if Rule 6 says "If something is round, then it is red", then that would mean that all round things are red. Therefore, since Bob is round, he must be red regardless of other attributes. So that would make the conclusion even stronger. Therefore, according to Rule 6, Bob must be red. So that would make the answer A) True.\n\nWait, but why is Rule 2 there then? Let me check if there\'s a conflict between the rules. Let me read Rule 2 again: "If something is round and cold then it is red." But Rule 6 says "If something is round then it is red." So Rule 6 is a more general statement. So Rule 6 would override Rule 2 in the sense that any round thing must be red, so even if it\'s not cold, it\'s still red. Therefore, Rule 2 is a special case that\'s actually redundant because the more general rule already covers it. Therefore, regardless of whether Bob is cold or not, being round makes him red. So Bob must be red. Therefore, the answer should be A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check again the problem\'s given attributes for Bob. The problem lists Bob\'s attributes as big, blue, cold, furry, green, round. There\'s no mention of red. But according to the rules, he must be red. So the problem\'s context includes all these statements, so the fact that Bob is round (and cold) means he must be red, even if it\'s not listed. Therefore, the statement "Bob is red" is true. So the answer is A.\n\nWa