blem. Because the question is whether Bob is not white. If according to the rules, being rough and cold makes him white, and we've established that Bob is both rough and cold, then Bob must be white. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is not white\" would be false. \n\nWait, but let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't skip anything. \n\nStarting over with Bob:\n\n1. Bob is nice (given). \n2. From Nice \u2192 Big (rule 10), so Bob is big. \n3. From Big \u2192 Red (rule 12), so Bob is red. \n4. Since Bob is nice and big, rule 13 (Nice \u2227 Big \u2192 Rough) applies, so Bob is rough. \n5. Since Bob is rough, rule 8 (Rough \u2192 Furry) applies, so Bob is furry. \n6. Now, Bob is furry and big (from step 2). Rule 14 (Furry \u2227 Big \u2192 Cold) applies, so Bob is cold. \n\nNow, Bob is rough and cold. Rule 9 (Rough \u2227 Cold \u2192 White) requires that Bob is white. \n\nTherefore, Bob must be white. The question is asking if \"Bob is not white\" is true. Since according to the rules, Bob must be white, then the statement \"Bob is not white\" is false. So the answer would be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possibility that Bob could be an exception. The rules are all universal, so there's no exceptions. Since all the premises lead to Bob being white, the statement that he isn't must be false. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Given Facts About Bob:**\n   - Bob is nice (Premise 1).\n   - From \"If someone is nice then they are big\" (Premise 10), Bob must be **big**.\n   - From \"All big people are red\" (Premise 12), Bob is **red** (though irrelevant to the question).\n\n2. **Deriving Bob's Additional Traits:**\n   - Since Bob is **nice and big**, \"All nice, big people are rough\" (Premise 13) applies. Thus, Bob is **rough**.\n   - From \"All rough people are furry\" (Premise 8), Bob is **furry**.\n   - Since Bob is **furry and big**, \"All furry, big people are cold\" (Premise 14) applies. Thus, Bob is **cold**.\n\n3. **Final Inference:**\n   - Bob is now **rough and cold**.\n   - \"If someone is rough and cold then they are white\" (Premise 9) forces Bob to be **white**.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The statement \"Bob is not white\" contradicts the derived conclusion that Bob **must be white**. Hence, the statement is **False** (Option B).",
