act 1, Bob is nice. Using rule 10 (Nice → Big), that means Bob must be big. So Bob is nice → big. \n\nNow, since Bob is big, rule 12 says big people are red. So Bob is red. But the question is about white, so maybe that\'s a side note. \n\nLooking further, since Bob is nice and big (from 1 and 10), rule 13 says that nice and big people are rough. So Bob must also be rough. \n\nNow, Bob is rough. Rule 8 says all rough people are furry, so Bob is furry. \n\nSo Bob has the attributes: nice, big, red, rough, furry. \n\nNow, let\'s see if any of these lead to being white or not. \n\nSince Bob is rough and furry, and big. Let\'s check other rules. \n\nRule 14: All furry and big people are cold. Since Bob is furry and big, he must be cold. \n\nSo Bob is cold. \n\nNow, Bob is rough (from 13) and cold (from 14). Rule 9 says if someone is rough and cold, they must be white. So Bob would have to be white. \n\nWait, that\'s a problem. Because the question is whether Bob is not white. If according to the rules, being rough and cold makes him white, and we\'ve established that Bob is both rough and cold, then Bob must be white. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not white" would be false. \n\nWait, but let me check again step by step to make sure I didn\'t skip anything. \n\nStarting over with Bob:\n\n1. Bob is nice (given). \n2. From Nice → Big (rule 10), so Bob is big. \n3. From Big → Red (rule 12), so Bob is red. \n4. Since Bob is nice and big, rule 13 (Nice ∧ Big → Rough) applies, so Bob is rough. \n5. Since Bob is rough, rule 8 (Rough → Furry) applies, so Bob is furry. \n6. Now, Bob is furry and big (from step 2). Rule 14 (Furry ∧ Big → Cold) applies, so Bob is cold. \n\nNow, Bob is rough and cold. Rule 9 (Rough ∧ Cold → White) requires that Bob is white. \n\nTherefore, Bob must be white. The question is asking if "Bob is not white" is true. Since according to the rules, Bob must be white, then the statement "Bob is not white" is false. So the answer would be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any possibility that Bob could be an exception. The rules are all universal, so there\'s no exceptions. Since all the premises lead to Bob being white, the statement that he isn\'t must be false. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False.\n'] 34%|███▍      | 204/600 [5:36:13<7:36:25, 69.16s/it] 34%|███▍      | 205/600 [5:36:48<6:28:02, 58.94s/it]
