ondition of red and nice.\n\nNow, if Erin is cold, then fact 14 says cold things are rough. Therefore, Erin must also be rough. So Erin is now red, nice, cold, and rough.\n\nNext, looking at fact 13: "Rough, nice things are round." Since Erin is both rough and nice, she must be round. So Erin is round as well.\n\nNow, fact 16 states that all round things are green. Since Erin is round, she must be green. Therefore, Erin is green. That would make the statement "Erin is green" true, so the answer should be A) True.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Let me go through each step again.\n\n1. Erin is red (fact 3) → red things are nice (fact 12) → Erin is nice. That\'s solid.\n2. Since Erin is red and nice, fact 15 applies → must be cold. So cold.\n3. Cold implies rough (fact 14) → rough.\n4. Rough and nice → must be round (fact 13). So round.\n5. Round things are green (fact 16) → green.\n\nAll these steps seem to chain correctly without contradiction. There\'s no information conflicting with these conclusions. The only thing is that maybe there\'s an exception, but the given rules don\'t mention any exceptions. So yes, Erin must be green. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n']
