r. So all young individuals see the bear. But how does that relate to the bear seeing the bald eagle?\n\nHmm. Maybe the bear\'s age? Let\'s see. Point 9: If someone chases the bear, then the bear is young. Who chases the bear? From point 14, the bald eagle does, as per the deduction above. So if the bald eagle chases the bear, then according to point 9, the bear must be young. So the bear is young. \n\nNow, since the bear is young, then according to point 13, being young, the bear would see the bald eagle. Wait, point 13 says "If someone is young then they see the bald eagle." So if the bear is young, then the bear would see the bald eagle. That would directly answer the question as true.\n\nWait, but let me check again. Let\'s make sure that the bear is indeed young. \n\nThe bear is young because someone (the bald eagle) chases the bear. Since the bald eagle chases the bear (from point 14\'s deduction), then by point 9: if someone chases the bear, then the bear is young. So yes, the bear is young. Therefore, applying point 13, since the bear is young, then the bear sees the bald eagle. Therefore the statement is true. So the answer would be A?\n\nBut wait, let me verify each step again to be sure I didn\'t make a mistake.\n\nStep 1: Bald eagle chases cow (point 1). Applying point 14: If someone chases the cow, then they chase the bear. Therefore, the bald eagle chases the bear. So that\'s a valid deduction.\n\nStep 2: Since the bald eagle chases the bear, then per point 9: If someone (here, the bald eagle) chases the bear, then the bear is young. So the bear is young.\n\nStep 3: Since the bear is young, per point 13: If someone (the bear) is young, they see the bald eagle. Therefore, the bear sees the bald eagle. Hence the statement is true. So the answer should be A.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check point 13 again. The exact wording is "If someone is young then they see the bald eagle." The subject here is the young individual. So if the bear is young, then the 