final_output:  ['\n\nThe squirrel is red, so by "All red things are blue," it is blue. Since the squirrel is both blue and red, "Blue, red things are round" makes it round. By "If something is round then it eats the squirrel," the squirrel must eat itself, requiring it to be green (via "If something eats the squirrel then the squirrel is green"). Thus, the squirrel is blue, red, and green. \n\nUsing "If something is blue and green then it chases the bald eagle," the squirrel (being blue and green) must chase the bald eagle. The squirrel’s visit to the bald eagle doesn’t conflict with this. \n\nThe correct option is: A', 'Okay, let\'s see. I need to figure out if the statement "The squirrel chases the bald eagle" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Hmm, let\'s start by going through each of the provided statements one by one and see what relationships we can establish.\n\nFirst, the context says: "The bald eagle chases the squirrel." So that\'s a direct statement. Bald Eagle → chases → Squirrel. Got that. So the bald eagle is doing the chasing here towards the squirrel. \n\nNext, "The bald eagle eats the bear." So Bald Eagle → eats → Bear. Interesting. Then, "The bear does not eat the lion." So Bear doesn\'t eat Lion. But "The bear visits the lion." So Bear visits Lion. \n\n"The lion does not eat the bald eagle." So Lion doesn\'t eat Bald Eagle. \n\n"The squirrel is red." So Squirrel\'s color is red. And "The squirrel visits the bald eagle." So Squirrel → visits → Bald Eagle. That\'s important because the question is about the squirrel chasing the bald eagle, but here it\'s stated that the squirrel visits the bald eagle. Wait, but visiting and chasing are different actions. Need to note that.\n\nNow the conditional statements:\n\n1. "If something is round then it eats the squirrel." So Round → eats Squirrel.\n2. "If something is blue and green then it chases the bald eagle." Blue ∧ Green → chases Bald Eagle.\n3. "If something eats the squirrel then