t, but Fiona is white. Let's see if Fiona's whiteness can lead to any conclusions about her youth. Let's check other rules.\n\nRule 6 says smart things are white. Fiona isn't stated to be smart. However, Anne is smart. Since Anne is smart, by rule 6, she must be white. Wait, but the context says Anne is green. Wait, that's a conflict. Wait, hold on. The context says Anne is green, and she is smart. Rule 6 states that smart things are white, so if Anne is smart, she must be white. But the context says Anne is green. That's a contradiction. Wait, that can't be right. Did I misread?\n\nWait, let me check again. The context says:\n\n\"Anne is green. Anne is nice. Anne is not rough. Anne is smart.\"\n\n\"Smart things are white\" (rule 6). So Anne being smart would require her to be white, but the context says she is green. That's a contradiction. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here. Wait, perhaps I misread the rules. Let me check again.\n\nWait, rule 6: \"Smart things are white.\" So if something is smart, then it is white. So Anne is smart, so she must be white. But the context says Anne is green. That's a contradiction. Therefore, that would mean that either the context has an error, or perhaps I misapplied the rules. Wait, maybe I need to check again.\n\nWait, let me re-express the context's given facts again carefully:\n\nAnne's properties: green, nice, not rough, smart.\n\nBob's properties: green, nice.\n\nFiona: white.\n\nGary: rough, round, white.\n\nHmm, so Anne's smartness plus rule 6 (smart \u2192 white) would require her to be white, but she's stated as green. That's a problem. So maybe that's a mistake in the problem setup, but perhaps I need to proceed assuming that the given data is correct and perhaps I made an error in reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps the context is presented as a set of premises, and we have to accept all as true, even if they seem contradictory? Wait, but in logic, if there's a contradiction in the premises, then any conclusion can be drawn, bu