l statements:\n\n- If someone is cold, then they see the rabbit.\n- If someone sees the tiger and the tiger is kind, then they see the bald eagle.\n- If the rabbit is red and the rabbit is rough, then the rabbit likes the bear.\n- If someone sees the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the bald eagle.\n- If someone eats the bald eagle, then they are cold.\n- If someone sees the bald eagle and they eat the bear, then they like the bald eagle.\n\nThe question is about whether the bald eagle sees itself. Let\'s denote the bald eagle as BE for simplicity. So, we need to determine if BE sees BE.\n\nFirst, let\'s check if there\'s any direct statement about BE seeing anything. Looking through the facts, there\'s no explicit mention of BE seeing anything. The "sees" relation is only mentioned in the conditional statements. So, we have to see if any of the conditions can lead us to conclude that BE sees BE.\n\nLet me go through each conditional statement and see if they can apply to BE.\n\nStarting with the first conditional: If someone is cold, then they see the rabbit. So, if BE is cold, then BE would see the rabbit. But does BE being cold lead to seeing the rabbit? However, the question is about BE seeing itself, so maybe this isn\'t directly helpful yet.\n\nThe second conditional: If someone sees the tiger and the tiger is kind, then they see the bald eagle. Since the tiger is kind (fact 9), if someone sees the tiger, then they must see the bald eagle. But does BE see the tiger? Let\'s see. Wait, does BE see the tiger? The first fact says BE eats the tiger. But "eats" is a different relationship from "sees". The problem doesn\'t state that eating implies seeing, so we can\'t assume that. So unless there\'s another condition that links eating to seeing, we can\'t say BE sees the tiger. Therefore, unless BE sees the tiger, this condition doesn\'t apply to BE.\n\nThird conditional: If the rabbit is red and the rabbit is rough, then the rabbit likes the bear. But the rabbit\'s col