e. The squirrel\'s perspective on seeing the bear isn\'t directly stated. \n\nWe need to see if there\'s any conditional statement that can lead us to that.\n\nLet me check the conditional statements again. Let\'s see:\n\nStatement 17: If something sees the lion and does not see the bear, then it sees the squirrel. Hmm. So if an entity sees the lion but doesn\'t see the bear, then they must see the squirrel. \n\nNow, we know the squirrel visits the lion (statement 11). But does the squirrel see the lion? Earlier conclusion was that the squirrel cannot see the lion because that would require it to be big (statement 19), and since the squirrel isn\'t big (from statement 12\'s contrapositive), the squirrel can\'t see the lion. Wait, but maybe the squirrel could see the lion without visiting? Wait, no, the squirrel visits the lion (statement 11), but visiting doesn\'t imply seeing. Wait, but maybe visiting requires seeing? Hmm, this is a bit ambiguous. Wait, let\'s clarify.\n\nWait, the problem uses "visits" and "sees" as separate actions. So unless a statement links them, we can\'t assume. So the squirrel visiting the lion doesn\'t necessarily mean it sees the lion. However, if the squirrel were to see the lion, then it must be big (statement 19). Since we already determined the squirrel is not big (because if it were, the lion would have to visit the rabbit, which it doesn\'t), then the squirrel cannot see the lion. Therefore, the squirrel does not see the lion. So the squirrel\'s seeing of the lion is false.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel does not see the lion. \n\nNow, going back to statement 17: If an entity sees the lion and does not see the bear, then it must see the squirrel. Since the squirrel does not see the lion, the antecedent of this implication (sees the lion AND doesn\'t see the bear) is false. Because the first part (sees the lion) is false. Therefore, the implication holds regardless of the squirrel\'s seeing the bear. So that doesn\'t give us info.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see other statements.\n\nStatement 15: If something sees the squirrel and visits the squirrel, then the squirrel does not see the lion. Well, the squirrel visits the lion (statement 11), but that\'s the squirrel visiting the lion, not visiting the squirrel. So that\'s not applicable here. Unless the squirrel is visiting itself? Probably not. So maybe this isn\'t relevant.\n\nWhat about statement 14? We already considered that since the rabbit likes the squirrel, the squirrel must visit the rabbit. So squirrel visits rabbit. That\'s an inferred fact.\n\nNow, if the squirrel visits the rabbit, then from statement 18: If something visits the rabbit, then it sees the lion. Wait, so the squirrel visits the rabbit (from statement 14), so according to statement 18, that would mean the squirrel must see the lion. But earlier we concluded that the squirrel cannot see the lion because that would require it to be big, which it isn\'t. That\'s a contradiction. Wait, this is a problem!\n\nHold on, this is important. Let me retrace that.\n\nFrom statement 14: If something likes the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the rabbit. The rabbit likes the squirrel (statement 8), so the squirrel must visit the rabbit. Therefore, squirrel visits rabbit. \n\nThen statement 18 says: If something visits the rabbit, then it sees the lion. So if the squirrel visits the rabbit, then the squirrel must see the lion. \n\nBut earlier, we thought that the squirrel cannot see the lion because that would require it to be big (statement 19: seeing lion implies being big). Since the squirrel is not big (from statement 12\'s contrapositive), seeing the lion would be impossible. Therefore, there\'s a contradiction here. \n\nHmm, so this suggests that my earlier conclusion that the squirrel is not big might be wrong? Or maybe I made a mistake in reasoning.\n\nLet me re-examine that part. \n\nStatement 12: If something is big and visits the lion, then the lion visits the rabbit. \n\nWe know the lion does not visit the rabbit (since the lion visits the bear, statement 5, and there\'s no mention of visiting the rabbit). Therefore, the antecedent of the contrapositive of statement 12 must be false. The contrapositive is: If the lion does not visit the rabbit, then it is not the case that (something is big and visits the lion). \n\nSince the lion does not visit the rabbit, the consequent must hold: Not (X is big AND X visits the lion). So for any X that visits the lion, X cannot be big. \n\nThe squirrel visits the lion (statement 11), so X = squirrel. Therefore, the squirrel cannot be big. So that part holds. \n\nNow, the squirrel visiting the rabbit (from statement 14) implies via statement 18 that the squirrel must see the lion. \n\nBut if the squirrel sees the lion, then by statement 19, the squirrel must be big. \n\nBut we just concluded that the squirrel cannot be big. \n\nThis is a contradiction. Therefore, there must be an error in my reasoning. \n\nWait, so this suggests that one of the premises might be conflicting, but since all the given statements are facts, perhaps my assumption that the squirrel must visit the rabbit is wrong?\n\nWait, let\'s check statement 14 again: If something likes the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the rabbit. The rabbit likes the squirrel (statement 8), so substituting that, the squirrel must visit the rabbit. \n\nTherefore, that\'s a necessary conclusion. So if the squirrel must visit the rabbit, then via statement 18, it must see the lion. \n\nBut that would require the squirrel to see the lion, which in turn requires it to be big (statement 19). However, the squirrel cannot be big because visiting the lion (statement 11) would require it not to be big (from statement 12\'s contrapositive). \n\nThis is a contradiction. That means there must be a mistake in my reasoning somewhere. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the squirrel visits the lion (statement 11) and visits the rabbit (from 14). \n\nVisiting the rabbit (per stateme