. That might be a conflict.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check again. The context says:\n\n"Charlie is blue. Charlie is quiet." There\'s no mention of Charlie being white. But according to rule 11, if Charlie is furry (which he is, because quiet) and blue, then he must be white. Therefore, Charlie must be white. But the context didn\'t mention that. So that would mean Charlie is white, even though it\'s not stated. So, Charlie\'s attributes are blue, quiet, furry, and white. Wait, but being white has implications. Because of rule 7: White things are cold. So if Charlie is white, then he must be cold.\n\nAh, here\'s a chain: Charlie is blue and quiet (so furry). Therefore, by rule 11 (furry and blue → white), Charlie must be white. Then, since white, by rule 7 (white → cold), Charlie must be cold. Therefore, Charlie must be cold. So that would make the statement "Charlie is cold" true. But wait, the original context didn\'t say Charlie is white or cold, but through the rules, we can derive it?\n\nWait, but let me check each step again carefully to make sure I didn\'t make a mistake.\n\nStarting over:\n\nCharlie\'s explicit attributes from context:\n\n- Blue.\n\n- Quiet.\n\nFrom the general rule 5: All quiet things are furry. Therefore, Charlie is furry.\n\nNow, Charlie is furry and blue. So applying rule 11: If something is furry AND blue, then it is white. Therefore, Charlie must be white. So Charlie is white, even though it\'s not stated in the context. Therefore, Charlie\'s attributes now include white.\n\nNow, since Charlie is white, by rule 7: White things are cold. Therefore, Charlie must be cold. Therefore, Charlie is cold. So the statement "Charlie is cold" is true. So the answer would be A) True.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if there\'s any contradictions here. The problem is that in the context, Charlie is only stated as blue and quiet, but through the rules, we derive that he must be white and cold. But does this lead to any contradictions?\n\nWait, if Charlie is