te. So Harry would be both white and blue. But is that possible? The problem didn\'t say that colors can\'t overlap. Hmm. But then, in the context, Anne is green, not blue. Charlie is white. Gary is green, not white. So maybe colors can be combined unless stated otherwise. So Harry can be both white and blue. But then, let\'s see if that causes any contradictions.\n\nWait, the problem also mentions "Blue, quiet people are round." Since Harry is blue (from quiet) and quiet, he must be round. So Harry is round. Also, looking at statement 6: If someone is quiet and round, then they are kind. Harry is quiet and round, so he must be kind. Which matches his given attribute of being kind. So that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, but the problem states that Harry is white, so he must be green (from statement 1). So Harry\'s attributes now are: white, blue, quiet, green, kind, and round. Wait, but the problem didn\'t mention Harry being blue or green, but according to the rules, those are inferred. \n\nNow, the question is whether Harry is rough. The only person mentioned as rough is Gary. The context says Gary is rough, but there\'s no direct info about Harry being rough. Let\'s check if any of the rules can lead to Harry being rough or not.\n\nFirst, let\'s see if Harry has any attributes that could imply roughness. The problem states that Gary is rough, but there\'s no general statement that links roughness to any particular attributes. The rules given don\'t mention anything about roughness except that Gary has it. The rules are about colors, being kind, quiet, round, etc. The only mention of roughness is in Gary\'s attributes. \n\nSo unless there\'s a rule that connects being rough to some other attribute, we can\'t infer Harry\'s roughness. Since the question is about whether Harry is rough, and the only info is that Gary is rough, but no rules link roughness to other attributes, we can\'t conclude anything about Harry being rough. \n\nWait, but let me check all the statements 