      "text": "\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out if the statement \"Dave is not blue\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information. Let me start by going through each of the premises one by one and see how they connect.\n\nFirst, the context gives several statements about Dave, Erin, Fiona, and Gary. Let me list them out again to make sure I don't miss anything:\n\n1. Dave is big.\n2. Dave is furry.\n3. Erin is blue.\n4. Erin is cold.\n5. Erin is round.\n6. Fiona is quiet.\n7. Gary is rough.\n8. If something is rough and cold, then it is furry.\n9. Quiet, big things are not round.\n10. If Dave is blue then Dave is furry.\n11. All quiet, blue things are big.\n12. If Fiona is furry then Fiona is blue.\n13. If something is quiet then it is cold.\n14. All big things are cold.\n15. All blue, round things are not quiet.\n16. Cold things are rough.\n\nThe question is whether Dave is not blue. So, I need to check if the premises lead us to conclude that Dave is not blue (so the answer would be A: True), that Dave must be blue (so B: False), or if it's unknown (C).\n\nLet me start by looking at the statements related to Dave. Dave is big (1), furry (2), and we need to see if he can be blue. The key here might be the implications given in statements 10, which says \"If Dave is blue then Dave is furry.\" Since Dave is already furry (2), that statement doesn't give us new information. But maybe other statements can help.\n\nAnother point: All big things are cold (14). Since Dave is big (1), he must be cold. So Dave is cold. Also, from statement 16, cold things are rough, so Dave must be rough too. Wait, but let me check that again. If something is cold, it's rough. So since Dave is cold, he must be rough. So Dave is rough.\n\nNow, looking at the implication in statement 8: \"If something is rough and cold then it is furry.\" Since Dave is rough and cold (as established), this would mean he must be furry. Which he is, so that's consistent.\n\nNow, the question is about Dave being blue. Let's see if there's any rule that connects blue to Dave. The first thing is statement 10: If Dave is blue, then he's furry. Since he is furry, that doesn't contradict, but it's a conditional. The contrapositive would be if Dave isn't furry, then he isn't blue, but since he is furry, that doesn't help.\n\nAre there other statements that might link Dave to blue? Let's look at other premises. All blue, round things are not quiet (15). But Erin is blue, round, and quiet? Wait, no. Erin is blue (3), round (5), but Fiona is quiet (6). Wait, Erin isn't mentioned as quiet, but Fiona is quiet. Wait, let me check again.\n\nErin's properties: blue, cold, round. Fiona is quiet. So Erin isn't necessarily quiet, but Fiona is. So the statement 15 says that blue and round things can't be quiet. Since Erin is blue and round, she can't be quiet. But the context says Erin is cold, blue, round. So that's okay because Fiona is the one who is quiet. So that's separate.\n\nNow, looking at statement 12: If Fiona is furry, then Fiona is blue. Fiona is quiet (6), and from statement 13, quiet things are cold. So Fiona is cold. Since Fiona is quiet, she must be cold (from 13). Also, from statement 16, cold things are rough, so Fiona must also be rough. But Fiona's properties are only given as quiet. So Fiona is quiet, cold, rough. Now, Fiona is quiet, so if she were blue, then from statement 11 (All quiet, blue things are big), Fiona would have to be big. But there's no statement that says Fiona is big. However, the problem is that we don't know Fiona's other properties except being quiet. But maybe that's okay.\n\nBack to Dave. Let's see if there's a contradiction if we assume Dave is blue. Let's suppose Dave is blue. Then, from statement 10: If Dave is blue then he's furry, which he is, so that's okay. Also, since he's blue and big (from 1), but is there any rule that would conflict with that? Let's see.\n\nAnother thing: All quiet, blue things are big (11). So if something is quiet and blue, it must be big. But Dave being blue and big would not conflict unless he's also quiet. But Dave's quietness isn't mentioned. However, since all big things are cold (14), and Dave is big, he is cold. From statement 16, cold things are rough, so he is rough. So Dave is rough and cold, so by statement 8, he must be furry, which he is. So that's okay.\n\nWait, but if Dave were blue, then since he is big (so he's big and blue), does that interact with any other rules? Let's see. Statement 15: All blue, round things are not quiet. Since Dave isn't necessarily round, that's not an issue. So maybe being blue is possible for Dave?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another angle. Let's look at the contrapositive of some statements. For example, statement 10: If Dave is blue \u2192 furry. Since he is furry, the implication is true regardless. So that doesn't help.\n\nIs there any rule that would require Dave not to be blue? Let's see. Suppose Dave is blue. Then, since he's big (1), he's also cold (from 14). So blue, big, cold, furry, rough. Is there any contradiction here?\n\nAnother point: All big things are cold (14), so Dave is cold. Since he's cold, he's rough (from 16). So rough and cold, so must be furry (8), which he is. So no problem there.\n\nWait, maybe the key is in statement 9: \"Quiet, big things are not round.\" But Dave is big, but not necessarily quiet. So that doesn't affect him unless he's quiet, which he isn't stated to be.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if being blue would require him to be something that conflicts. Let me check if any statements about blue things. The statements about blue are:\n\n- Erin is blue (3), and she's round and cold. From statement 15, blue and round things can't be quiet. Since Erin is blue and round, she can't be quiet. Which is okay because Fiona is quiet. So that's okay.\n\n- All quiet, blue things must be big (statement 11). So if something is blue and quiet, it must be big. But if Dave were blue and