 the rabbit.**\n    - Lion → visits → rabbit.\n\n13. **The rabbit chases the bald eagle.**\n    - Rabbit → chases → bald eagle.\n\n14. **The rabbit visits the bald eagle.**\n    - Rabbit → visits → bald eagle.\n\nNow, the conditional statements:\n\n15. **If someone needs the rabbit then the rabbit chases the lion.**\n    - If X needs rabbit → then rabbit chases lion. \n\n16. **If someone is rough and they do not chase the rabbit then they visit the bald eagle.**\n    - If (X is rough AND X does not chase rabbit) → X visits bald eagle.\n\n17. **If someone chases the lion then they do not need the cow.**\n    - If X chases lion → X does not need cow.\n\n18. **If someone needs the bald eagle and the bald eagle chases the cow then the bald eagle is not round.**\n    - If (X needs bald eagle AND bald eagle chases cow) → bald eagle is not round.\n\n19. **If someone chases the bald eagle and they do not need the cow then they visit the cow.**\n    - If (X chases bald eagle AND X does not need cow) → X visits cow.\n\n20. **If someone needs the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow then the rabbit is big.**\n    - If (X needs rabbit AND rabbit visits cow) → rabbit is big.\n\n21. **If someone is big then they do not visit the lion.**\n    - If X is big → X does not visit lion.\n\nThe question is about whether "The bald eagle visits the bald eagle" is true, false, or unknown. Let\'s denote this as B visits B, where B is the bald eagle.\n\nFirst, we need to see if there\'s any direct information about the bald eagle visiting itself. Looking at the given statements, there\'s no direct statement that says "The bald eagle visits the bald eagle". The only mentions of visits involving the bald eagle are in statements 14 and 12, but those are from the rabbit and lion visiting the bald eagle. \n\nSo, the bald eagle\'s own actions regarding visiting aren\'t directly stated. Therefore, we need to check if any of the conditional statements can lead us to conclude whether B visits B or not