ound (which we don\'t know). Since we don\'t know if Harry is round, the implication doesn\'t force anything here. So this statement might not be applicable yet.\n\n5. If someone is young and smart then they are blue. So any young and smart person must be blue. Let\'s see who is young and smart. From above, Erin is red, so she is young (from point 3). She is also smart (given) and white. Since she\'s young and smart, according to point 5, she must be blue. But wait, the problem states Erin is red. That\'s a conflict! Because according to the rules, if she\'s young and smart (she is both), she must be blue. But the given data says she\'s red. That\'s a contradiction. Hmm, this is a problem.\n\nWait, that\'s a contradiction. Let me check again. Erin is red (given), so by rule 3, she must be young. She\'s also smart (given). Therefore, by rule 5, since she\'s young and smart, she must be blue. But the problem says she is red. So this is a contradiction. That might mean that the given data is inconsistent, but maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me verify:\n\nErin\'s attributes as per given:\n\n- Erin is red.\n\n- Erin is round.\n\n- Erin is smart.\n\n- Erin is white.\n\nSo Erin is smart and young (because red implies young). Therefore, by rule 5, young and smart → blue. But Erin is red. So that\'s a direct contradiction. That suggests that either there\'s an error in the problem\'s given data, or perhaps I\'m misunderstanding the rules. But since this is a problem to solve, maybe I need to proceed despite the contradiction? Or perhaps the contradiction invalidates some of the statements?\n\nHmm, this is a problem. Maybe I need to check if there\'s another angle. Alternatively, perhaps the problem expects us to ignore the contradiction and proceed, but that\'s unclear. Let\'s see if there\'s another way to resolve this.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the statement "White people are smart" (rule 6) is important here. Since Erin is white, she must be smart, which she is. That\'s okay. But the conflict between her being red (so young) and thus needing to be blue is still there. Since she\'s stated as red, that\'s a contradiction. Maybe the problem expects us to treat the given statements as facts, even if they conflict with the rules? But that might not make sense. Or perhaps the rules take precedence over the given attributes?\n\nWait, the problem says "Based on the above information", so perhaps all the given statements (both the direct attributes and the conditional rules) must hold. Therefore, if there\'s a contradiction, then the conclusion might be that some statements can\'t be true, but the problem is asking about Dave\'s color. Since Dave is directly stated as blue, unless there\'s a rule that overrides that, he must be blue, so the answer would be B) False (since the question is "Dave is red"). However, the contradiction with Erin complicates things. But maybe we are to assume that all the given data is consistent, so perhaps I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let me check again. The problem says "All smart, round people are blue." Erin is smart and round, so she must be blue. But she\'s stated as red. That\'s a contradiction. Therefore, perhaps the problem is designed so that even with that contradiction, we can proceed? Or maybe the contradiction means that some of the premises can\'t hold, but since we have to take all given facts as true, perhaps the answer is that Dave is blue, so the statement that he is red is false. Maybe the contradiction in Erin\'s case is just an inconsistency in the problem\'s setup, but we proceed with the rest.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I missed something. Let me re-express all the given facts and see.\n\nGiven facts (direct attributes):\n\n- Dave: blue\n\n- Erin: red, round, smart, white\n\n- Fiona: rough, round\n\n- Harry: red, rough, smart\n\nConditional rules:\n\n1. All smart ∧ round → blue\n\n2. Fiona is round → Fiona is red\n\n3. red → young\n\n4. Harry is young ∧ round → rough\