its the mouse. Wait, the subject here is "someone" (X). The mouse is the one who is red and eats the tiger. So substituting X as the mouse, since the mouse is red and eats the tiger, then the tiger must visit the mouse. So this gives us that the tiger visits the mouse. So that\'s a new fact: tiger visits mouse. Because the condition is satisfied by the mouse\'s attributes (red and eats tiger), so the consequent must be true: tiger visits mouse.\n\nWait, but the original fact 11 says "the tiger eats the mouse". So the tiger eats the mouse and also visits the mouse? That\'s possible. So that\'s an additional fact we can infer.\n\nEighth conditional: "If someone visits the bear then they are kind." If someone visits the bear, they must be kind. But again, unless we know someone visits the bear, this may not help here.\n\nNow, back to the question: does the mouse visit the squirrel?\n\nLooking at the second conditional again: "If someone visits the mouse then they like the squirrel." So if someone visits the mouse, then that someone must like the squirrel. The question is about the mouse visiting the squirrel. Wait, but the conditional is about visiting the mouse, not visiting the squirrel. So maybe that\'s not directly related.\n\nAlternatively, could the mouse be the one visiting the mouse? That would be visiting oneself, which seems odd, but perhaps logically possible. If the mouse did visit the mouse, then according to the second conditional, the mouse must like the squirrel. But does the mouse like the squirrel? The facts given don\'t mention that. The mouse likes the bear (fact8) and doesn\'t like the tiger (fact9). There\'s no info about the mouse\'s feelings towards the squirrel. So if the mouse were to visit itself, then that would require the mouse to like the squirrel, which we don\'t know. But since the mouse visiting itself isn\'t mentioned, maybe that\'s not the path.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse visits the squirrel. If that were the case, would that 