ue. But Fiona's smartness is what we're trying to determine. \n\nSo, if Fiona were smart, then according to rule 9, she would have to be blue. But there's no information given that Fiona is blue or not. The problem doesn't state Fiona's color. So, if Fiona is both quiet and smart, she must be blue. But since we don't know her color, can we infer anything?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another angle. Let's check other rules. Rule 11 says that kind things are quiet. So, if something is kind, then it's quiet. Gary is both kind and quiet (points 4 and 5), which fits. But that doesn't directly relate to Fiona.\n\nRule 12 says that all blue and green things are smart. But Fiona's color isn't mentioned, so unless we can link her to being blue or green, that might not help.\n\nNow, let's think about the contrapositive of rule 9. The original is: If (quiet ∧ smart) → blue. The contrapositive would be: If not blue → not (quiet ∧ smart), which is equivalent to not quiet ∨ not smart. But Fiona's not known to be blue or not. So if Fiona is not blue, then she can't be both quiet and smart. But since we don't know her color, that doesn't give us a definitive answer.\n\nWait, but maybe we can look for contradictions. Suppose Fiona is smart. Then she must be blue (from rule 9). But if she's blue, then according to rule 12 (all blue and green things are smart), but wait, rule 12 requires both blue and green. Fiona's color is only blue (if we suppose she is blue from being smart and quiet). Since she isn't necessarily green, that rule doesn't apply here. Hmm, maybe that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, if Fiona isn't smart, then she can be quiet without being blue. That would be allowed. Since the only requirement is that if she were both quiet and smart, then she must be blue. But without knowing her color, we can't confirm either way.\n\nIs there any other rule that might affect this? Let me check again.\n\nRule 10 says if something is green and smart, then it's not blue. But Fion