ment 10 says all young, blue things are not green. Since Fiona is young and blue, she cannot be green. But that doesn't relate to coldness directly. \n\nNow, to see if Fiona is cold. The premises that mention coldness are:\n\n- Charlie is cold (so Charlie is cold)\n- Dave is cold (so Dave is cold)\n- Statement 15: If something is cold and green, then it is young. \n\nBut Fiona is not green (from statement 10, since she's young and blue, so not green). Therefore, Fiona cannot be both cold and green because she's not green. So statement 15 doesn't apply to her. \n\nIs there any rule that connects being nice, round, blue, young, white (Fiona's attributes) to being cold or not?\n\nLooking at the rules again, maybe the contrapositive of some statements?\n\nAlternatively, let's see if Fiona's attributes lead to being round and blue, which might have other implications. \n\nWait, since Fiona is blue (from round via statements 12 and 16), then statement 14 says blue things are young, which she is. Also, statement 16 says round things are blue, so that loops back. \n\nIs there any statement that links being blue or round to being cold or not cold?\n\nLooking at the premises again: \n\nStatement 15: If something is cold and green \u2192 young. But Fiona isn't green. \n\nThe other cold mentions are Charlie and Dave being cold. The question is whether Fiona can be inferred to be cold or not. \n\nThe problem is, there's no direct statement about Fiona's coldness. The premises don't say anything like \"All young and blue things are cold\" or anything that would link her attributes to coldness. \n\nWait, maybe there's a chain of reasoning. Let's see:\n\nFiona is round (from being nice, via 12), so round things are blue (16). Therefore, she's blue. \n\nNow, since she's blue, statement 14 says blue things are young, which is already given. \n\nIs there any rule that says if something is blue, then it's not cold? Let me check all the statements again. \n\nLooking at all statements:\n\nThe only ones that might relate are the ones about coldness:\n\n- Charlie and Dave are explicitly cold. \n\n- Statement 15: If cold and green \u2192 young. But Fiona isn't green, so that's irrelevant. \n\nThere's also the contrapositive of any implications. Let's see:\n\nSuppose someone is cold. Are there any constraints?\n\nFor instance, if something is cold and green, then it must be young. But Fiona isn't green. \n\nAlternatively, maybe if someone is round (which Fiona is), then they are blue (statement 16). But blue things are young (statement 14). But none of these link to coldness. \n\nTherefore, there's no direct or indirect statement that says Fiona must be cold or must not be cold. \n\nWait, but let's check the last part again. The question is whether Fiona is NOT cold. To prove that, we need to find a contradiction if she were cold, or find a statement that says she can't be cold. \n\nSuppose Fiona is cold. Would that lead to any contradictions?\n\nIf Fiona is cold, then she is cold. But the premises don't state any rules that would conflict with her being cold. The only rules about coldness are the explicit mentions of Charlie and Dave, and the conditional in 15. Since Fiona isn't green, being cold wouldn't trigger any issues. \n\nAlternatively, maybe if Fiona is round (she is), and round things are blue (she is), but being blue doesn't exclude her from being cold unless there's a rule. \n\nWait, looking again at statement 13: \"If Charlie is round then Charlie is white.\" But Charlie is already young and cold, but that's a separate condition. \n\nHmm. I don't see any rule that would prevent Fiona from being cold. Since the premises only state that Charlie and Dave are cold, but Fiona's attributes don't conflict with being cold. \n\nTherefore, the information given doesn't allow us to conclude Fiona is cold or not. Therefore, the statement \"Fiona is not cold\" would be unknown, because we can't confirm it's true or false based on the premises. \n\nWait