te doesn't necessarily mean being nice, but being nice requires being white. So if Gary is white, he could be nice or not. But if he is nice, then he must be white, which is consistent.\n\nAlternatively, if Gary is white and rough, then from point8, he must be quiet, which he is. So that's okay. So if he is white and rough, that's possible. But is there a reason he must be rough?\n\nAlternatively, if he is white but not rough, then point8 doesn't apply, so his quietness isn't explained by that rule. But quietness could be from another reason. The problem doesn't say that all quiet things are rough and white, only that rough and white things are quiet. So being quiet could have other causes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Gary is white and not rough, but then how is he cold? Let's see. If he's cold, maybe because he's a rough and nice thing (point12). But if he's nice, then he must be white (point11), which would be okay. But if he's nice and rough, then he must be cold (point12). So that could be a possibility. Let's see:\n\nSuppose Gary is nice. Then he must be white (point11). Then being nice and rough would make him cold (point12). But does he have to be rough? Because if he's nice and rough, then he is cold. But the problem doesn't state that he's nice or rough. So that's a possibility but not required.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Gary is not nice. So he could be white for another reason? The problem doesn't state that only nice things are white, just that nice things are white. So there might be other white things that aren't nice.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's another angle. Let's see if we can find any rules that would require Gary to be white or not.\n\nWait, the question is whether Gary is *not* white. So to confirm that, we need to find a rule that would make him not white, or find that it's impossible for him to be white, leading to the conclusion that he must not be. Alternatively, if there's no way to determine, then it's unknown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules force him to be white, making the statement false, or maybe not.\n\nLet me try another approach. Let's list all the properties of Gary:\n\n- Gary is cold (point6)\n- Gary is quiet (point7)\n- The other info about others (Bob, Charlie, Dave) might not directly affect Gary, but maybe through some general rules.\n\nLooking at point12: Rough and nice things are cold. Since Gary is cold, perhaps he is a rough and nice thing. If so, then he must be nice (so white), and rough. But does that mean he must be? Or is that just one possibility?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to be cold. The problem doesn't state that only rough and nice things are cold. So Gary could be cold for another reason, so that path isn't necessary.\n\nAnother angle: point8 says rough and white things are quiet. Gary is quiet. So if Gary is white and rough, that explains his quietness. But since he is quiet, maybe he must be either rough and white, or something else. Since quietness can come from other sources, we can't be sure.\n\nWait, but if Gary is not white, then to be quiet, he would have to be quiet for a different reason. The problem doesn't state any other rules that make things quiet, so maybe that's allowed. So quietness doesn't require being rough and white, just that being rough and white would make you quiet.\n\nHmm. Let's see if there are any other constraints.\n\nLooking at point14: If something is rough, then it is young. So any rough thing must be young. But we don't know if Gary is young or not.\n\nPoint13: If something is round and rough, then it is nice. But again, not sure how that connects to Gary.\n\nPoint10: All young things are round. So if something is young, it must be round. But again, unless Gary is young, which we don't know.\n\nCharlie has multiple properties: he's nice (so white by point11, which matches point4), rough (point3), and white (point4). So Charlie is a rough, nice, white thing. So according to point12, he must be cold. Wait, but the problem 