))\n20. If something is nice then it is blue. (N(x) → Bl(x))\n\nThe question is whether "The squirrel is cold" is true, false, or unknown. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nLooking at the given statements, point 10 directly says "The squirrel is not cold." So that would mean ¬C(squirrel) is true, which would make the statement "The squirrel is cold" false. But wait, before jumping to conclusions, maybe there\'s some other information that could contradict this or provide more context?\n\nWait, let me check again. Statement 10 is explicitly "The squirrel is not cold." So that\'s a direct fact. Therefore, the squirrel is not cold, so the statement "The squirrel is cold" must be false. So the answer would be B) False. But let me make sure there\'s no trick here. Maybe there\'s a conditional that could override this?\n\nLet me see if any of the conditionals could affect this. For example, does any of the statements introduce a scenario where the squirrel might become cold or where the fact that it\'s not cold is somehow conditional?\n\nLooking at statement 13: If something visits the bear and it is green then it is nice. But that\'s about being nice, not cold. Statement 14 is about green and blue leading to visiting the bear. Statement 15 is about liking the lion and being blue leading to the lion being nice. Statement 20 says if something is nice, then it\'s blue. The squirrel is nice (statement 11), so by statement 20, the squirrel must be blue. But that doesn\'t relate to coldness.\n\nWait, the squirrel\'s coldness is directly addressed in statement 10. So unless there\'s a contradiction in the given statements, which there isn\'t, the squirrel is not cold. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any possibility that the squirrel could be cold despite statement 10. For example, maybe there\'s a condition that if something is nice, then it\'s not cold? Let me see. The squirrel is nice (statement 11). If being nice implies not cold, then that would reinforce the conclusion. But the problem is that the given statements don\'t say that. The only direct statement about coldness is that the squirrel is not cold. The other conditionals don\'t link niceness to coldness. Statement 20 says if something is nice, it\'s blue, but blue and coldness are separate.\n\nTherefore, the only direct fact is that the squirrel is not cold. Therefore, the statement "The squirrel is cold" is false. So the correct answer should be B) False. I don\'t see any conflicting information here. The other statements might influence other attributes but not coldness. So I think the answer is B.\n'] 54%|█████▍    | 326/600 [9:02:52<6:31:10, 85.66s/it] 
