ule \"If someone is nice then they are smart,\" Charlie must be smart. \n3. Since Charlie is smart, by \"All smart people are blue,\" he must be blue. \n4. Now, Charlie is blue and smart. The rule \"Blue, smart people are furry\" applies here, so Charlie must be furry. \n5. However, the context says \"Fiona is furry. Fiona is young.\" There's no mention of Charlie being furry. But in logical terms, unless stated otherwise, can we assume Charlie isn't furry? Wait, in logic, if the context doesn't state that Charlie is furry, then we can't assume it's true. So the statement \"Charlie is furry\" would be unknown, but the rules require it if he's blue and smart. \n\nThis creates a contradiction because the rules force Charlie to be furry, but the given data doesn't say he is. However, maybe the problem is that we have to work strictly with the given facts and the rules. The fact that Charlie isn't listed as furry means that the implication from the rules must hold, so either the rules are wrong (which they aren't in the context) or Charlie's status as smart must be false. \n\nWait, but the first rule is an \"if\" statement. The contrapositive would be if someone isn't smart, then they aren't nice. But Charlie is nice, so he must be smart. Therefore, the only way out is if the other rules don't apply because of some exception, but there's no such exception stated. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the rules are structured in a way that being cold and not furry could affect something else. Let's see another rule: \"Furry, cold people are smart.\" Charlie is cold but not furry, so that rule doesn't apply to him. \n\nWait, but the problem arises from the chain of rules. The conclusion that Charlie must be furry because he's blue and smart contradicts the given data (he's not furry). Therefore, the only way this can hold is if one of the premises is wrong. Since all the rules are given as true, the only possibility is that our initial assumption (Charlie is smart)