selves. But the problem's context includes statements like \"The dog sees the tiger\" so maybe seeing is only mentioned when it's true, but absence doesn't mean false. \n\nIn formal logic, absence of a statement doesn't mean the negation is true. So unless there's a contradiction if it does, we can't assume it's false. \n\nWait, but perhaps there's a contradiction if the tiger does see itself? Let's see. \n\nSuppose the tiger does see itself. Then, according to conditional20, that's okay because the tiger is big. \n\nIs there any other condition that would be triggered? \n\nIf the tiger sees the tiger (itself), then perhaps any conditionals that involve seeing the tiger would apply. But I don't think that introduces a contradiction. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the tiger is green and big (so young via point21). But that's okay. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the tiger is cold (because it's big) and chases the cat, so per conditional19, it sees the cat. Which we already have. \n\nTherefore, there's no contradiction if the tiger sees itself. \n\nTherefore, the question is whether the tiger sees itself. Since there's no information given about that, the answer would be unknown (C). \n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check the conditional statements again to see if any could imply that the tiger can't see itself. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the tiger is big (from point20 and dog seeing it) and big things are cold (point22). \n\nThe conditional19 says that if something is cold and chases the cat, then it sees the cat. The tiger is cold (since it's big), and it chases the cat, so it sees the cat. \n\nBut that's already inferred. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a way to see if the tiger's seeing itself would require something else. \n\nWait, maybe if the tiger sees itself, then according to conditional20, the tiger must be big, which is true. So that's okay. \n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that says an animal can't see itself? The problem doesn't state that. \n\nTherefore, since there's no information, the answer is unknown. \n\nWait, but wait another angle. Let me think of the question again: \"The tiger does not see the tiger.\" So, if we can prove that the tiger does see itself, then the statement is false. If we can prove it doesn't, then it's true. Otherwise, unknown. \n\nIs there any way to prove that the tiger does see itself? \n\nNo, unless there's a rule that requires it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger's seeing the cat (from conditional19) and the dog (from conditional18) but not itself. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the squirrel sees the dog (point10) and the squirrel is big (so cold via point22). \n\nWait, but that's not relevant here. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that the tiger likes the squirrel (point14) and squirrel is green (point8). \n\nHmm, not directly related. \n\nAlternatively, let's see if there's any other conditionals that might apply. \n\nThe conditional15: since the tiger likes the dog, it must chase the tiger (itself). Wait, that's an important point. \n\nWait, going back to conditional15: If something likes the dog, then it chases the tiger. \n\nThe tiger likes the dog (point13), so applying 15: the tiger must chase the tiger. \n\nSo the tiger chases the tiger. \n\nNow, does that lead to any other conclusions? \n\nIf the tiger chases itself, does that imply anything about seeing? \n\nThe problem's context doesn't say that chasing implies seeing. Unless there's a conditional that says so. \n\nLooking through conditionals, is there any that links chasing to seeing? \n\nConditional19 links chasing the cat (if cold) to seeing the cat. \n\nBut for chasing the tiger (itself), maybe there's no such rule. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps if the tiger chases itself, then maybe it must see itself? But there's no such rule. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the act of chasing requires seeing, but that's an assumption not stated. \n\nTherefore, the fact that the tiger chases it