ung). \n\nBut not sure how that helps. \n\nAlternatively, the squirrel sees the dog (point10). \n\nWait, but the question is about the tiger\'s self-seeing. \n\nHmm. Since there\'s no statement or conditional that directly requires the tiger to see itself or forbids it, except maybe through some chain. \n\nWait, perhaps the only way the tiger could see itself is if there\'s some rule that if an animal chases itself, it must see itself? But the problem doesn\'t state that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the tiger chases the cat (point11) and the conditionals don\'t require seeing itself. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tiger could see itself, but there\'s no information to confirm or deny that. So the answer would be unknown. \n\nWait, but let\'s think again. \n\nThe problem states "If something sees the tiger then the tiger is big." Since the tiger is big (as we derived from the dog seeing it), then that\'s okay. But if the tiger were to see itself, then the condition would still hold because the tiger is big. \n\nBut there\'s no rule that requires an animal to see itself unless some condition is met. \n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information in the premises that says the tiger sees itself, and no conditional that would require it to, then the statement "The tiger does not see the tiger" would be unknown? Or is there a way to infer that it doesn\'t?\n\nAlternatively, maybe by default, unless stated, animals don\'t see themselves. But the problem\'s context includes statements like "The dog sees the tiger" so maybe seeing is only mentioned when it\'s true, but absence doesn\'t mean false. \n\nIn formal logic, absence of a statement doesn\'t mean the negation is true. So unless there\'s a contradiction if it does, we can\'t assume it\'s false. \n\nWait, but perhaps there\'s a contradiction if the tiger does see itself? Let\'s see. \n\nSuppose the tiger does see itself. Then, according to conditional20, that\'s okay because the tiger is big. \n\nIs there any 