he only cold thing mentioned is the cat. So the lion\'s temperature is unknown. Therefore, the condition in statement 18 requires that "the lion is not cold". Since we don\'t know if the lion is cold or not, this might not be applicable yet. Let\'s keep that in mind.\n\nStatement 19: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. So if someone chases the lion, then the lion must chase the cow. Do we have any chaser of the lion? Not yet, unless someone else is doing that. Since the lion is chasing the cat, but nothing about chasing the lion. Unless the squirrel or cow does. Let\'s see later.\n\nStatement 20: If something chases the squirrel then the squirrel visits the cat. So if, say, the lion chases the squirrel (from statement 17, since lion likes cat, so lion chases squirrel), then the squirrel would have to visit the cat. Let\'s see. Let\'s track that.\n\nPutting some of these together:\n\nFrom statement 14: Lion visits cat → lion likes cat.\n\nFrom statement 17: lion likes cat → lion chases squirrel.\n\nIf that\'s the case, then from statement 20, since the lion is chasing the squirrel (from above), then the squirrel must visit the cat. So squirrel visits the cat. Now, does that affect anything?\n\nAlso, statement 13 says: If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat. But if the squirrel is being visited by someone, but do we know who? Let\'s see.\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel visits the cat (from statement 20\'s consequence), then maybe there\'s a reverse relation? Wait, the squirrel visiting the cat would be the squirrel\'s action. But statement 13 is about someone visiting the squirrel leading to that visitor liking the cat. Since the squirrel is visiting the cat, that\'s different.\n\nWait, if the squirrel visits the cat, then maybe the cat is visited by the squirrel, but the condition in statement 14 is about something visiting the cat leading to liking the cat. So the squirrel visiting the cat would mean that the squirrel likes