sits the lion (statement16), not the cow. So the rabbit can't be X here either. \n\nTherefore, there is no X that both visits the cow and eats the rabbit. So statement 18's condition isn't met. Therefore, we can't conclude the rabbit is cold from this.\n\nNext, looking at other conditionals that might involve the rabbit's coldness. \n\nStatement 23: All cold people are blue. But this doesn't directly affect the rabbit unless we can link the rabbit to being cold through another condition.\n\nStatement 25: If someone eats the lion then they are cold. Wait, this is about the eater of the lion being cold, not the lion itself. Let me parse that. \n\nStatement 25 says: If someone eats the lion, then they (the eater) are cold. \n\nSo who eats the lion? From the given facts, the cow eats the lion (statement1), and the mouse eats the lion (statement11). \n\nSo according to statement25, both the cow and the mouse would be cold. But the question is about the rabbit being cold. However, the rabbit itself isn't the eater here. Unless there's some chain that connects this to the rabbit's coldness. \n\nWait, but the rabbit's coldness isn't directly addressed here. Unless perhaps the eater being cold has some other effect. Let me think. \n\nWait, statement25 says that if someone eats the lion, then they (the eater) are cold. So the cow and mouse are cold. But does that affect the rabbit's status? Maybe through another conditional?\n\nLooking at statement23: All cold people are blue. So if the cow or mouse are cold (from statement25), then they would be blue. But how does that connect to the rabbit?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a chain via another condition. Let me check other statements.\n\nStatement17: If someone eats the lion and the lion is green, then they are blue. \n\nThe lion is green (statement9), so if someone eats the lion, then they are blue (because lion is green). \n\nWait, the cow and mouse both eat the lion. So for the cow: since it eats the lion and the lion is g