ar then the bear is green." The mouse is big (fact14) and does the mouse visit the bear? The cat visits the mouse (fact9) and the cat visits the bear (fact8). The mouse visiting the bear isn\'t stated. The bear is green anyway (fact1), so this condition might not be necessary, but maybe it\'s a way to confirm that if the mouse did visit the bear, then the bear\'s greenness is ensured, but since the bear is already green, maybe it\'s irrelevant here.\n\nNow, back to the main question: does the mouse visit the dog?\n\nLooking at the facts again, the dog visits the mouse (fact13). The question is whether the mouse visits the dog. The reverse isn\'t stated, so we need to see if any conditionals can lead us to that conclusion.\n\nLet me think about the first conditional again: "If something visits the mouse and the mouse visits the dog then it is cold." Let\'s parse this again. Suppose that there is some entity X such that X visits the mouse AND the mouse visits the dog. Then, X must be cold. \n\nBut we need to see if this can be used in reverse. Suppose that the mouse does visit the dog. Then, if there exists an X that visits the mouse, then X must be cold. But is there such an X?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse itself is X? Wait, the antecedent is about an entity X visiting the mouse and the mouse visiting the dog. So if the mouse visits the dog (the part we\'re trying to find out), then for any X that also visits the mouse, X must be cold. But does that help us?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to find that if the mouse is cold, then according to the third conditional, it must like the cat. Since the mouse is cold (fact15), it must like the cat. But how does that connect to visiting the dog?\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the mouse visiting the dog can be inferred via other conditionals. Let\'s see.\n\nSuppose the mouse does visit the dog. Then, is there a contradiction or a confirmation? Let\'s check if that would hold.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there 