d also need to be smart. Is there any information conflicting with that?\n\nLooking at the given facts, Harry is smart (7). But Gary\'s smartness isn\'t mentioned directly except through these implications.\n\nWait, but let\'s see if there\'s any rule that would prevent Gary from being smart. For example, statement 14: If Charlie is big and young, then Charlie is not smart. But that\'s about Charlie, not Gary. So that doesn\'t affect Gary.\n\nTherefore, if Gary is quiet, he must be smart, but there\'s no information that contradicts that. Since all the premises lead to Gary being quiet, then the statement "Gary is quiet" should be true. But wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nLet me list out all the conclusions about Gary:\n\n1. Blue → young (statement 10)\n2. White + young → round (statement 12)\n3. Round → big (statement 15)\n4. Big + blue → quiet (statement 11)\n\nTherefore, the chain is:\n\nGary is blue and white → young (from blue) and white → round → big → big and blue → quiet. So all these steps are straightforward. Therefore, Gary is quiet. So the answer would be True (Option A).\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check if there\'s any possibility that Gary isn\'t big. Because statement 15 says round things are big. Since Gary is round (from step 2), he must be big. So that\'s a must. So yes, he\'s big. Therefore, big and blue → quiet. So yes, quiet.\n\nAlternatively, could there be a case where even though he\'s big and blue, the statement 11 says "big, blue things are quiet"—so that\'s a universal statement. So all big blue things are quiet. So Gary must be quiet.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be A) True. But let me check again for any oversight.\n\nWait, another angle: the problem mentions Fiona is blue (statement 2). The statement 13 says: If Fiona is big and not white, then Fiona is blue. But since Fiona is already blue, maybe that\'s just a conditional that doesn\'t affect us. Since Fiona\'s color is given, maybe that\'s just extra.\n\nAlso, Charlie 