rule that could prevent Dave from being young? The only path to being young is through being kind. Since he's kind (from being nice), he must be young. So all steps hold. \n\nAlternatively, is there a possibility that being big and something else could affect it? For instance, Fiona is big and not furry, but she's not nice? Wait, Fiona is big (statement 4), but statement 9 says big implies nice, but Fiona is not nice (statement 4 says Fiona is not furry, but wait the context says \"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry.\" Wait the original context says \"Fiona is not nice\"? Let me check again. \n\nLooking back at the context:\n\n4. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. (Wait, the original context says \"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry.\" So the \"not nice\" part for Fiona isn't mentioned here. Wait the user's context says:\n\n\"Bob is green. Bob is not nice. Dave is furry. Erin is furry. Erin is nice. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. If something is young then it is green. If something is big and rough then it is not nice. If something is big then it is nice. If Bob is furry then Bob is young. If something is furry then it is big. If something is nice then it is kind.\"\n\nWait, so Fiona is big, and not furry, but there's no mention of Fiona being nice or not. But according to statement 9, if something is big, it is nice. So Fiona, being big, must be nice. However, the context does not state Fiona is not nice. Wait, maybe I misread earlier. Let me check again.\n\nLooking at the context again: The user wrote:\n\n\"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. ...\"\n\nAh, so Fiona's \"not nice\" is not mentioned. Wait, the original context says: \n\nWait the user's context lists Fiona as:\n\n\"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry.\"\n\nThere's no mention of Fiona's niceness. However, according to statement 9, being big implies nice. Therefore Fiona should be nice. But the problem is, the context didn't state that Fiona is not nice. Wait, the user might have made a mistake, but according to the given context, Fiona is big, so she must be nice. But in the problem's context, maybe I misread. Let me check again.\n\nWait the user's context says:\n\n\"Bob is green. Bob is not nice. Dave is furry. Erin is furry. Erin is nice. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. If something is young then it is green. If something is big and rough then it is not nice. If something is big then it is nice. If Bob is furry then Bob is young. If something is furry then it is big. If something is nice then it is kind.\"\n\nAh, okay, so Fiona's not nice is not stated. Wait the user might have made a mistake, but according to the given info, Fiona is big, so according to statement 9, she must be nice. But maybe there's an exception? Because statement 8 says if big and rough, then not nice. But Fiona is not furry, but does that imply anything else? Not necessarily. Unless \"rough\" is related to something else. Since Fiona is big, unless she's rough, she should be nice. But since we don't know if Fiona is rough, we can't say for sure. Wait, but the problem is about Dave, so maybe that's a side issue. \n\nBut back to Dave. Since Dave is furry \u2192 big (statement 11), big \u2192 nice (9), so he must be nice. Then nice \u2192 kind (12), so he's kind, which makes him young (5). Then young \u2192 green (7). So all steps are there. Therefore Dave must be green, so the answer should be A) True. \n\nWait but hold on. Let me check the statements again. \n\nStatement 6 says \"Young, big things are green.\" So if something is both young and big, it is green. Dave is young (from being kind) and big (from being furry), so that also applies. So that's another way to confirm. \n\nIs there any way that Dave could not be green? Suppose there's a rule that says something else. But according to the given info, all paths lead to green. \n\nTherefor