tement 9 says if something is big, then it\'s nice. Wait, but does that apply here? Statement 9 says "If something is big then it is nice." So Dave being big would mean he must be nice? But let\'s check if there\'s any conflicting info about Dave\'s niceness. The context doesn\'t mention anything about Dave being nice or not, except that Erin is nice. However, statement 9\'s implication might come into play here. So if Dave is big (from being furry), then according to statement 9, he must be nice. But wait, the problem is, does being big necessarily make him nice unless there\'s another condition? Let me check statement 8: If something is big and rough then it is not nice. But we don\'t know if Dave is rough. So unless there\'s a reason to think he\'s rough, the default would be that being big makes him nice via statement 9. \n\nBut let\'s see. Since Dave is furry and big (from 11), and big implies nice (from 9), so Dave must be nice. Then, statement 12 says if something is nice, then it is kind. So if Dave is nice, he must be kind. Then statement 5 says kind things are young, so Dave would be young. \n\nWait a second, so if Dave is kind (from being nice), then he\'s young. And statement 7 says if something is young, it\'s green. So putting this all together: Dave is furry → big (from 11), then big → nice (from 9), nice → kind (from 12), kind → young (from 5), young → green (from 7). Therefore, Dave would be green. \n\nBut hold on, let me check each step again to make sure there\'s no missing conditions. \n\nStarting over:\n\n1. Dave is furry (given).\n2. Furry → big (statement 11). So Dave is big.\n3. Big → nice (statement 9). So Dave is nice.\n4. Nice → kind (statement 12). So Dave is kind.\n5. Kind → young (statement 5). So Dave is young.\n6. Young → green (statement 7). Therefore, Dave must be green.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Dave is green" should be true, right? But wait, let\'s check if any of these steps have exceptions or other conditions that might invalidate this chain. \n\nFor example, statement 8 says that if something is big and rough, then it\'s not nice. But we don\'t know if Dave is rough. However, the fact that he is big and nice (from step 3) would mean that he can\'t be both big and rough, because if he were rough, then being big and rough would make him not nice. But since he is nice, he must not be rough. So that\'s okay. So the only way for Dave to be big and nice is that he is not rough, so that condition doesn\'t interfere. \n\nAnother check: statement 6 says that young and big things are green. Since Dave is young and big, that also would make him green (from statement 6). So both paths (young → green, and young and big → green) lead to the same conclusion. \n\nTherefore, according to all the given rules, Dave must be green. So the answer should be A) True. \n\nWait, but let me see if there\'s any other constraints. For instance, is there any rule that could prevent Dave from being young? The only path to being young is through being kind. Since he\'s kind (from being nice), he must be young. So all steps hold. \n\nAlternatively, is there a possibility that being big and something else could affect it? For instance, Fiona is big and not furry, but she\'s not nice? Wait, Fiona is big (statement 4), but statement 9 says big implies nice, but Fiona is not nice (statement 4 says Fiona is not furry, but wait the context says "Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry." Wait the original context says "Fiona is not nice"? Let me check again. \n\nLooking back at the context:\n\n4. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. (Wait, the original context says "Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry." So the "not nice" part for Fiona isn\'t mentioned here. Wait the user\'s context says:\n\n"Bob is green. Bob is not nice. Dave is furry. Erin is furry. Erin is nice. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. If something is young then it is green. If something is big and rough then it is not nice. If something is big then it is nice. If Bob is furry then Bob is young. If something is furry then it is big. If something is nice then it is kind."\n\nWait, so Fiona is big, and not furry, but there\'s no mention of Fiona being nice or not. But according to statement 9, if something is big, it is nice. So Fiona, being big, must be nice. However, the context does not state Fiona is not nice. Wait, maybe I misread earlier. Let me check again.\n\nLooking at the context again: The user wrote:\n\n"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. ..."\n\nAh, so Fiona\'s "not nice" is not mentioned. Wait, the original context says: \n\nWait the user\'s context lists Fiona as:\n\n"Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry."\n\nThere\'s no mention of Fiona\'s niceness. However, according to statement 9, being big implies nice. Therefore Fiona should be nice. But the problem is, the context didn\'t state that Fiona is not nice. Wait, the user might have made a mistake, but according to the given context, Fiona is big, so she must be nice. But in the problem\'s context, maybe I misread. Let me check again.\n\nWait the user\'s context says:\n\n"Bob is green. Bob is not nice. Dave is furry. Erin is furry. Erin is nice. Fiona is big. Fiona is not furry. Kind things are young. Young, big things are green. If something is young then it is green. If something is big and rough then it is not nice. If something is big then it is nice. If Bob is furry then Bob is young. If something is furry then it is big. If something is nice then it is kind."\n\nAh, okay, so Fiona\'s not nice is not stated. Wait the user might have made a mistake, but according to the given info, Fiona is big, so according to statement 9, she must be nice. But maybe there\'s an exception? Because statement 8 says if big and rough, then not nice. But Fiona is not furry, but does that imply anything else? Not necessarily. Unless "rough" is related to something else. Since Fiona is big, 