), big → young (rule9), so big and young → nice (rule8). Also, big and nice → cold (rule5). Then cold and kind → smart (rule2), smart → white (rule1). Young and white → cold (rule6), which matches. \n\nTherefore, Fiona\'s attributes would be: kind, big, young, nice, cold, smart, white. \n\nThe question is whether "Fiona is not big" is true, false, or unknown. Since we deduced Fiona must be big, the statement "Fiona is not big" is false. So the correct answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any possibility that Fiona could not be big. Suppose someone says, "Wait, maybe Fiona isn\'t big because of some other rule?" But according to rule 3, all kind people are big. Since Fiona is kind, that directly makes her big. The only way she wouldn\'t be big is if being kind doesn\'t apply, but she is kind. So I don\'t see any exceptions here. \n\nAnother point to check: The problem might have a trick where Fiona can\'t be big because of some contradiction. Let me see if any of the rules conflict when we assume Fiona is big. \n\nSuppose Fiona is big (from rule3), then she must be young (rule9). Then, being big and young makes her nice (rule8). Then being nice and big makes her cold (rule5). Then being cold and kind makes her smart (rule2). Then being smart makes her white (rule1). And being young and white makes her cold (rule6). All these are consistent. So no contradictions. Therefore, Fiona must be big. \n\nTherefore the statement "Fiona is not big" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n'] 61%|██████    | 366/600 [10:14:22<8:15:05, 126.95s/it]
