 the rabbit does or doesn\'t visit the lion.\n\nLooking at the conditionals:\n\n17. If something visits the cat, then the cat sees the lion.\n\nWe know the rabbit visits the cat (16), so substituting "rabbit" into this: If rabbit visits the cat, then the cat sees the lion. Since the rabbit does visit the cat, then the cat must see the lion. But does the cat actually see the lion? The premises don\'t mention the cat seeing the lion directly, but according to this rule, if the rabbit visits the cat, then the cat must see the lion. Wait, but does that mean the cat does see the lion? Let me check the premises again. The original premises don\'t state that the cat sees the lion. So according to this rule, since the rabbit does visit the cat (premise 16), then the conclusion must be that the cat sees the lion. So that would be an inferred fact. So the cat sees the lion. But that\'s a separate point.\n\nNow, are there any other rules that might involve the rabbit visiting the lion?\n\nLooking at premise 18: If something visits the lion and the lion visits the rabbit, then it needs the lion. Let\'s see if the rabbit could be that "something". Suppose the rabbit visits the lion. Then, the first condition (something visits the lion) would be satisfied. The second part is "the lion visits the rabbit", which is true (premise 12). So if the rabbit visits the lion, then the rabbit would need the lion. Does the rabbit need the lion? The premises don\'t say that the rabbit needs the lion. Let\'s check the premises. The only "needs" mentioned are the cat needs the bear (4), and the lion does not need the bear (8). There\'s no statement about the rabbit needing the lion or not. So if the rabbit were to visit the lion, then according to premise 18, it would need the lion, but since there\'s no information that the rabbit needs the lion, maybe this is a contradiction? Or perhaps it\'s allowed unless stated otherwise?\n\nWait, but the problem is asking whether the rabbit does not visit 