nd)\n\nNow, back to Dave. Let's see if Dave could be white.\n\nAssume Dave is white. Then by rule 3, he must be big. Also, since he is white, by rule 2, he must be kind. But to be kind, he could be either because he's furry or for some other reason. Wait, but the only way to be kind is via being furry (since the first rule is furry \u2192 kind, but is there another way? The problem says \"Furry people are kind\", which might mean that all kind people must be furry? Wait no, the first rule is \"Furry people are kind\" which is furry \u2192 kind, but it doesn't say that only furry people are kind. So maybe someone could be kind without being furry? Hmm, but the next rule says if you're kind, you're white, etc. But let's see.\n\nWait, the first rule: \"Furry people are kind\" means all furry people are kind, but there could be non-furry people who are kind? The problem doesn't say that only furry people are kind. So, maybe someone else could be kind without being furry, but then how?\n\nWait, but the problem's question is about Dave. Let's see Dave's attributes. The only given is that Dave is green. So, if Dave is white, then he must be big (from 3). Also, since he's kind (from 2), then to be kind, perhaps he must be furry? Because if he's kind, he must be white, but how else would he be kind? Unless there's another way. Hmm, but maybe the only way to be kind is through being furry. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules don't restrict that. So, Dave could be kind without being furry, but then he would have to be white and big. However, there's another rule: \"Furry, white people are rough.\" But if Dave is white and kind, he must be big. Now, if Dave is kind and green, then from rule 7: Green and kind \u2192 round. So Dave would have to be round. However, the problem didn't state that Dave is round, only that he is green. So, if Dave is kind, then he must be round. But since the problem only says he is green, not round, that would mean that if Dave were kind,