ry), so she must be round, which is given. Dave is green. If Dave is kind, then he would need to be round? But is Dave kind?\n\nWait, the question is about Dave\'s color. Let\'s see Dave\'s attributes. The context says Dave is green. Are there any other attributes given? No, just green. So we need to see if we can derive his color based on the rules.\n\nFirst, let\'s see if Dave can be white. Let\'s assume Dave is white. Let\'s check the implications.\n\nIf Dave is white, then by the rule "If someone is white then they are big," so Dave must be big. Also, since he\'s white, he must be kind (because all white people are kind via the chain: white implies big? Wait, no. Wait the chain is: kind → white? Wait no, the second rule says "If someone is kind then they are white." Wait that\'s the converse. Wait let me re-express the rules:\n\nRules summary:\n\n1. Furry → Kind (from "Furry people are kind")\n2. Kind → White (from "If someone is kind then they are white")\n3. White → Big (from "If someone is white then they are big")\n4. Rough → Green (if someone is rough, then they are green)\n5. Kind ∧ ¬Big → ¬Rough (but since Kind → White → Big, so this is redundant because if someone is kind, they must be big, so the antecedent is impossible)\n6. Furry ∧ White → Rough (Furry, white people are rough)\n7. Green ∧ Kind → Round (Green and kind people are round)\n8. White ∧ ¬Green → Round (If someone is white and not green, they are round)\n\nNow, back to Dave. Let\'s see if Dave could be white.\n\nAssume Dave is white. Then by rule 3, he must be big. Also, since he is white, by rule 2, he must be kind. But to be kind, he could be either because he\'s furry or for some other reason. Wait, but the only way to be kind is via being furry (since the first rule is furry → kind, but is there another way? The problem says "Furry people are kind", which might mean that all kind people must be furry? Wait no, the first rule is "Furry people are kind" which is furry → kind, but it doesn\'