is nice. So Erin is nice.\n4. Nice people are smart (given). Therefore, Erin is smart.\n5. All smart people are young (given). So Erin must be young.\n6. All young people are furry (given). Since Erin is young, she must be furry.\n\nTherefore, Erin is furry. The question asks if \"Erin is not furry\" is true. Since we just concluded she is furry, the statement must be false. So the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Given Facts:**  \n   - Erin is red.  \n   - All red people are not round \u21d2 Erin is not round.  \n   - The conditional statement: *If Erin is red and not round, then Erin is nice*. Since both conditions (red and not round) are true, Erin must be nice.  \n\n2. **Inferences from Traits:**  \n   - Nice people are smart \u21d2 Erin is smart.  \n   - All smart people are young \u21d2 Erin is young.  \n   - All young people are furry \u21d2 Erin must be furry.  \n\n3. **Conclusion:**  \n   Since Erin is furry, the statement \"Erin is not furry\" is **False**.  \n\nThe correct option is: B",
