, so she must be smart and therefore young. But wait, if she\'s young, then by the rule "all young people are furry," she must be furry. But the question is asking if Erin is not furry. So if she must be furry, then the statement "Erin is not furry" would be false. \n\nWait, let me make sure I didn\'t skip any steps. Let\'s go step by step again.\n\n1. Erin is red (given).\n2. Since red people are not round, Erin is not round (given by "All red people are not round").\n3. The statement "If Erin is red and Erin is not round then Erin is nice" applies here. Both premises (red and not round) are true, so the conclusion is Erin is nice. So Erin is nice.\n4. Nice people are smart (given). Therefore, Erin is smart.\n5. All smart people are young (given). So Erin must be young.\n6. All young people are furry (given). Since Erin is young, she must be furry.\n\nTherefore, Erin is furry. The question asks if "Erin is not furry" is true. Since we just concluded she is furry, the statement must be false. So the answer should be B) False.\n']
