.\n\nThen, from statement 18: Green things are young. Which is okay because the squirrel is young (statement 7). So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, what about the tiger? The tiger is cold (statement 11) and visits the bald eagle (statement 12). Therefore, since the tiger visits the bald eagle, by statement 13, the tiger must be big. So tiger is big.\n\nWait, that\'s important. The tiger visits the bald eagle (statement 12), so by statement 13, the tiger must be big. So tiger is big.\n\nNow, from statement 20: If the squirrel is big and the squirrel does not like the tiger, then the tiger is not big. But the squirrel does like the tiger (statement 8), so the antecedent is false. Therefore, the implication holds, and we don\'t get any info about the tiger\'s size. But we already established the tiger is big from visiting BE, so that\'s okay.\n\nNow, the tiger is big (from visiting BE), so by statement 14, the tiger must need the squirrel. So tiger needs squirrel.\n\nBut does the tiger need the squirrel? Not stated, but according to the rules, yes.\n\nNow, the squirrel is green (because it needs itself, per statement 16). So squirrel is green and young, which aligns with statement 18 (green things are young).\n\nNow, going back to the squirrel visiting the lion. Let\'s suppose that the squirrel does visit the lion. Then, as per statement 19, the lion needs the squirrel. But the lion\'s needs are not restricted except that the bald eagle needs the lion (statement 1). So that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel does NOT visit the lion, then statement 15\'s condition doesn\'t apply to the squirrel. So there\'s no contradiction either way.\n\nWait, but is there any information that would require the squirrel to visit the lion? Let me check all statements again.\n\nLooking at the given info:\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 10). The squirrel\'s other visits are not mentioned. The question is about whether it visits the lion. Since there\'s no direct statement saying it does or doesn\'t, perhaps the answer is unknown?\n\nBut maybe there\'s an indirect way to infer it. Let me think again.\n\nSuppose the squirrel does NOT visit the lion. Then, we can check for any contradictions.\n\nIf squirrel does not visit the lion, then the only visits involving the squirrel are to the bald eagle. Then, any implications based on that?\n\nWell, the squirrel is big (from visiting BE), so needs the squirrel (statement 14), so that\'s okay. The squirrel being green (from needing squirrel, so statement 16) and young (given) is consistent with statement 18.\n\nThe tiger is big (from visiting BE), so needs the squirrel (statement 14). That\'s okay.\n\nNow, statement 19 says that if something is big and visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel. Since the squirrel is big but (in this scenario) does NOT visit the lion, then the antecedent is not met. So that\'s okay.\n\nThe lion\'s needs: The bald eagle needs the lion (statement 1). The lion needing the squirrel is only triggered if the squirrel visits the lion, which in this scenario it doesn\'t. So the lion\'s needs are only as stated (from BE needing it).\n\nSo no contradictions here either.\n\nTherefore, whether the squirrel visits the lion or not doesn\'t lead to any contradiction. Since there\'s no direct information given about the squirrel visiting the lion, and no indirect rule that forces it to do so or not, the answer would be unknown?\n\nWait, but let me check all the rules again to make sure I didn\'t miss something.\n\nLooking at statement 15: If something visits the squirrel AND visits the lion → needs the squirrel.\n\nSuppose there exists an X that visits both squirrel and lion. Then X needs squirrel.\n\nBut if the squirrel doesn\'t visit the lion, then the squirrel itself can\'t be that X (since it only visits BE). The only other entities that visit the lion are the bald eagle (statement 2) and maybe the squirrel (if it does visit). The bald eagle visits the lion, but does it visit the squirrel? Not stated. So the bald eagle visiting lion and not squirrel, so the antecedent isn\'t met. The tiger visits the bald eagle (so is big) and visits the tiger (wait, no, the lion visits the tiger (statement 5), but the tiger\'s visits are to BE and maybe others? The tiger\'s visits are only stated as visiting BE. So the tiger doesn\'t visit the squirrel or the lion (except the lion visits the tiger, but that\'s the other way around).\n\nTherefore, the only possible candidate for X in statement 15 would be the squirrel if it visits both. Since there\'s no other entity mentioned to do so, unless the lion visits the squirrel (but that\'s not stated), so the only possible X is the squirrel itself. But if the squirrel doesn\'t visit the lion, then statement 15\'s condition isn\'t triggered. So there\'s no issue.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information forcing the squirrel to visit the lion or preventing it, the answer should be unknown, which is option C.\n\nWait a second, but let me think again. The question is asking if "The squirrel does not visit the lion" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a way to prove that the squirrel cannot visit the lion. Let me see.\n\nSuppose the squirrel does visit the lion. Then, as per statement 19, since the squirrel is big (because it visits BE), the lion needs the squirrel. Now, the lion is already needing the bald eagle? Wait no, the bald eagle needs the lion (statement 1), but the lion\'s needs are not specified except in the context of the implication in statement 19.\n\nBut is there any contradiction in the lion needing the squirrel?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel is green (as we established earlier because it needs itself). The squirrel being green is okay. The squirrel is young, so that\'s consistent with statement 18 (green things are young).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that the squirrel is young (statement 7) and via statement 17, it needs the tiger. So squirrel needs tiger. But the squirrel also needs the squirrel (because it\'s big). So the squirrel has two needs: itself and the tiger. That\'s okay.\n\nThe only possible issue could be if needing the squirrel (itself) creates a loop, but the rules don\'t prohibit that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that if something needs the squirrel, then it must be green, which the squirrel is, so that\'s okay.\n\nHmm. I can\'t find any contradictions in either case (whether the squirrel visits the lion or not). Therefore, the answer should be unknown, which is option C.\n\nWait, but wait another thought. Let me check statement 15 again. Suppose that the squirrel does visit the lion, then the squirrel would need the squirrel (from statement 14 because it\'s big, but also from statement 15 if it visits both the squirrel and the lion. Wait, actually, if the squirrel visits the squirrel (itself) and the lion, then the squirrel (as X) must need the squirrel. Which is already required by statement 14. So that\'s consistent. But does the squirrel visit itself?\n\nThe problem doesn\'t state that the squirrel visits itself, so we can\'t assume that. So the squirrel visiting the lion would only satisfy the "visits the lion" part, but not the "visits the squirrel" part of statement 15\'s condition. Therefore, the condition of statement 15 wouldn\'t be met unless the squirrel also visits itself, which isn\'t stated.\n\nTherefore, even if the squirrel visits the lion, unless it also visits itself, the implication in statement 15 wouldn\'t apply. So the only way statement 15 is triggered is if there\'s an entity that visits both the squirrel and the lion. Since we don\'t have any such entity explicitly mentioned (except maybe the squirrel if it visits both), but the squirrel visiting the squirrel isn\'t stated, so we can\'t assume that.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel visiting the lion doesn\'t necessarily trigger any new implications beyond what\'s already there (like the lion needing the squirrel via statement 19).\n\nSo, since there\'s no information to confirm or deny whether the squirrel visits the lion, the correct answer is unknown (C).\n\nBut wait, let me check the options again. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nWait, the question is whether "The squirrel does not visit the lion" is true. So if we can\'t determine whether it does or not, then the answer would be unknown. But perhaps there\'s a way to infer it must be true or false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a hidden implication. Let me think differently.\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 10). From statement 13, that makes the squirrel big. Then, from statement 14, the squirrel must need the squirrel. From statement 16, needing the squirrel means it\'s green. So squirrel is green and young (from statement 7 and 18, which is okay).\n\nNow, the squirrel is young, so by statement 17, it must need the tiger. So squirrel needs tiger (which is okay because it likes the tiger, but "needs" is a different relation).\n\nNow, the question is about visiting the lion. The only direct visit from the squirrel is to the bald eagle. There\'s no information about visits to the lion. So unless there\'s a rule that says if something is young and/or green, they can\'t visit the lion, but there isn\'t.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that would require the squirrel to visit the lion?\n\nLooking at all the rules again, I don\'t see any that force it to do so. Therefore, the answer is unknown (C).\n\nHmm. But let me check once more for any possible oversight.\n\nWait, the problem states "If something visits the squirrel and it visits the lion then it needs the squirrel." (statement 15). Suppose the squirrel does visit the lion. Then, if there is any entity that visits both the squirrel and the lion, then that entity must need the squirrel. But unless the squirrel visits itself, the squirrel isn\'t visiting the squirrel. So unless another entity visits both, there\'s no trigger