ry is rough, this rule doesn't apply to him. Since Harry is white, rule 7 says he's nice regardless. \n\nNow, let's get back to the question: Is Harry not blue? So we need to determine Harry's color. \n\nHarry's given attributes are white. From the rules, can we infer his color? Let's see. \n\nFirst, since Harry is white (given), he must be nice (rule 7). Therefore, by rule 4, all nice people are big, so Harry must be big. \n\nHarry is big. Then, by rule 1, all big people are rough. Therefore, Harry must be rough. \n\nWait, so Harry is big (from being nice) and therefore rough (from being big). So Harry is rough. \n\nNow, since Harry is rough (from the above), rule 3 says he must be green. \n\nSo Harry is green. \n\nAdditionally, rule 5 says green people are blue. Therefore, since Harry is green, he must be blue. \n\nTherefore, Harry is both green and blue. \n\nWait, but the question is asking if Harry is not blue. But according to this, he must be blue (since he's green, and green implies blue). Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not blue\" would be false. So the answer would be B) False. \n\nBut let me double-check all steps to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting with Harry's attributes: white. \n\nRule 7: white \u2192 nice. Therefore, Harry is nice. \n\nRule 4: nice \u2192 big. So Harry must be big. \n\nRule 1: big \u2192 rough. Therefore, Harry is rough. \n\nRule 3: rough \u2192 green. So Harry must be green. \n\nRule 5: green \u2192 blue. Therefore, Harry must be blue. \n\nSo Harry is blue. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not blue\" is false. Therefore, the correct answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other rule that might affect this. \n\nAlso, rule 6: Rough, white people are nice. Harry is rough and white, so that rule applies. But since he's already nice (from rule 7), that's consistent. \n\nRule 8: green people are nice. Since Harry is green, he must be nice. Which he is, so that's okay. \n\nSo all the inferences hol