ve one color. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps "green people are blue" (rule 5) means that all green people are blue, but blue people can be green or not. Wait, rule 5 is "Green people are blue," so that\'s a one-way implication: if someone is green, then they are blue. But blue people can be green or not. So Anne is blue and rough, so she must be green (from rule 3), so she must also be blue (which she is) and green. So she\'s both blue and green, which is allowed. That\'s okay. So no problem there.\n\nOkay, moving on. Fiona: big, blue, quiet. Since she\'s big (given), rule 1 requires her to be rough. Therefore Fiona must also be rough (inferred). \n\nFiona is rough, so by rule 3, she must be green. So Fiona is also green. \n\nGary: blue, quiet, rough, white. \n\nGary is rough, so by rule 3, he must be green. \n\nGary is white. \n\nHarry: white. \n\nNow, let\'s look at the other rules. \n\nRule 4: All nice people are big. So any nice person must be big. \n\nLooking at the given attributes:\n\nAnne is nice, so she must be big (already covered). \n\nGary is rough and white. Let\'s see if Gary is nice. Rule 7 says "White people are nice." So since Gary is white, he must be nice. \n\nWait, rule 7 says "White people are nice," so all white people are nice. Therefore, Gary is white, so he must be nice. But the context lists Gary as quiet, rough, blue, and white. It doesn\'t mention "nice," but according to rule 7, he must be nice. Therefore, Gary is also nice. \n\nTherefore, Gary is nice, so by rule 4, he must be big. But the context doesn\'t list Gary as big. Wait, but is there a conflict here?\n\nWait, Gary is rough and white. Also, if Gary is nice (from rule 7), then he must be big (from rule 4). Therefore, Gary must be big. But the context didn\'t mention that. So that\'s an inferred attribute. \n\nBut Gary is also rough. Since he\'s rough, he must be green (rule 3). So Gary is green. \n\nRule 5 says green people are blue, so Gary is already blue, so that\'s okay. \n\nHarr