e other statements.\n\nAlso, Charlie is blue, so from statement 12, blue → quiet, so Charlie is quiet. Additionally, from statement 15, quiet people are round, which matches because Charlie is round, so that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, but if Charlie is quiet, that\'s because he\'s blue. But the first condition (statement 8) says if Charlie is cold then he is quiet. But Charlie\'s quietness is already established via being blue. So whether Charlie is cold or not doesn\'t affect his quietness here. Hmm, but maybe there\'s a conflict if Charlie is cold. Let me note that.\n\nNow, Charlie is round (so green), blue (so quiet), and we don\'t know yet if he\'s big or not. Let\'s see. If Charlie is big, then from statement 9, he can\'t be green. But he is green because he\'s round, so that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Charlie cannot be big. Because if he were big, he can\'t be green, but he must be green because he\'s round. Therefore, Charlie must not be big. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 9 (if Charlie is big) is false, so the implication holds, but Charlie\'s bigness is negated. So Charlie is not big. So that\'s okay.\n\nNow, moving to Dave. Dave is blue (5). From statement 12, blue → quiet, so Dave is quiet. From statement 15, quiet → round. Therefore, Dave must be round. Wait, but the context says Charlie is round, but Dave\'s roundness is inferred here. So Dave is round. Now, from statement 14 (round → green), so Dave must be green. So Dave is green.\n\nWait, but Dave is blue and green. Is that allowed? The problem doesn\'t say colors can\'t overlap unless stated. So that\'s possible. \n\nNow, the question is about Dave\'s coldness. Let\'s see. The statement 13 says: If Dave is kind, then Dave is cold. So D kind → D cold. But we don\'t know if Dave is kind. Let\'s check if there\'s any info on Dave\'s kindness. The context only mentions Dave\'s color (blue) and that\'s it. So unless there\'s another rule that can determine Dave\'s kindness.\n\nWait, let\'s