not blue (he is white), then the contrapositive is: If \u00acblue \u2192 \u00ac(nice \u2227 quiet). Since Bob is not blue (he's white), then \u00ac(nice \u2227 quiet) must hold. Since Bob is nice, then \u00acquiet must be true. Therefore, Bob is not quiet. Therefore, the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, this seems conflicting with the earlier conclusion. Let me make sure. Let's use formal logic here.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet B be Bob.\n\nPremises:\n\n1. B is white (W(B))\n\n2. B is nice (N(B))\n\n3. W \u2192 Q (If someone is white, they are quiet)\n\n4. (N \u2227 Q) \u2192 B (If someone is nice and quiet, then they are blue)\n\n5. B is white, so W(B) \u2192 Q(B). Therefore, Q(B) must hold (Bob is quiet).\n\nBut from premises 2 and 5, we have N(B) and Q(B), so (N \u2227 Q)(B), which by rule 4 would imply Blue(B). But premise 1 says W(B), which contradicts Blue(B) unless W and Blue can coexist? Wait, no, the context says Bob is white, not blue. So Blue(B) is false. Therefore, the conclusion from 4 would require that (N \u2227 Q)(B) \u2192 Blue(B). Since Blue(B) is false, the antecedent (N \u2227 Q)(B) must also be false. Since N(B) is true (he is nice), then Q(B) must be false. Therefore, Bob is not quiet. Therefore, the answer would be B) False.\n\nAh, so that's the correct reasoning. The key is that the combination of nice and quiet would force him to be blue, which he isn't, so he can't be quiet. Therefore, even though being white would usually make him quiet, the other rule overrides that by requiring that if he were quiet, he would have to be blue, which he isn't, so he can't be quiet. Therefore, Bob is not quiet, so the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but how does that work exactly? Let me go through again step by step.\n\nGiven that Bob is white (W(B)), so by rule W\u2192Q, so Q(B). But if he is also nice (N(B)), then combining N and Q gives N(B) \u2227 Q(B). By the rule (N \u2227 Q) \u2192 B (blue), so that would require B is blue. But Bob is stated as white, s