at's two steps. Since there's no rule that negates that, it should be valid.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be True (A). Wait, but the options are A/B/C as per the question. Let me confirm once more.\n\nWait, but let me also check if the squirrel chasing BE would cause any other conditions. For example, if the squirrel chases BE, then by statement18, it must see the mouse. If the squirrel sees the mouse, does that lead to any other conclusions?\n\nIf S sees M, then since M needs D (point10), statement24 applies: If something (S) sees the mouse and the mouse needs the dog \u2192 the dog chases the bald eagle. So then D chases BE. So that's another consequence, but that's okay. The question is only about the squirrel's action.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that would prevent the squirrel from seeing the mouse? The only constraints are on the mouse's vision (mouse doesn't see squirrel) and BE's vision (doesn't see dog). The squirrel's vision isn't restricted except by statement25, which would only affect if something else sees the squirrel.\n\nTherefore, I think the conclusion holds. The squirrel must chase the bald eagle, so the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait a second. Let me check the exact wording of statement22 again: \"If something chases the dog then it chases the bald eagle.\" So \"it\" refers to the same something. So if X chases D, then X chases BE. So in this case, X is the squirrel, so yes, that applies.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel chases the dog (from 17), so it must chase BE. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nHmm. But I'm a bit confused why the answer might not be A. Let me see if there's any other angle. Wait, is there a rule that says the squirrel cannot chase BE because of another condition?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a rule that the squirrel can't chase BE because of needing something else?\n\nThe squirrel's needs aren't mentioned except that the dog needs the squirrel (point7). The squirrel's own needs aren't stated. So no