ow about Anne. From statements 1 and 2, Anne is kind and round. \n\nStarting with statement 14: "Kind people are green." Since Anne is kind (statement 1), this would mean Anne must be green. So, Anne is green. Then, looking at statement 12: "All green people are blue." Since Anne is green, she must also be blue. \n\nNow, if Anne is blue, let\'s see if any other rules apply. Statement 17 says, "All blue, kind people are quiet." Since Anne is both blue and kind, she must be quiet. So now, Anne is kind, round, green, blue, and quiet. \n\nNext, statement 13 says, "Quiet, round people are cold." Since Anne is both quiet and round, this would imply she is cold. Wait, but does that mean the statement is true? Let me check again. \n\nBreaking down statement 13: If someone is quiet AND round, then they are cold. Since Anne is both quiet and round, according to this rule, she must be cold. \n\nSo, following that logic, Anne would have to be cold. But wait, let me double-check if there\'s any conflicting information. Let me go through all statements again to make sure I didn\'t miss anything.\n\nStarting with Anne\'s attributes: kind, round, green (from 14), blue (from 12), quiet (from 17). Then, since she is quiet and round, by 13, she must be cold. That would make the statement "Anne is cold" true. \n\nBut hold on, let me check the other implications. Statement 16 says, "If someone is cold then they are furry." So if Anne is cold, she must be furry. But do we know if Anne is furry? The given information doesn\'t state that Anne is furry. The only mentions of being furry are Dave (statements 5 and 7?), wait: Dave is furry (statement 5), Fiona is furry (9), but Anne\'s furrieness isn\'t mentioned. \n\nHmm, so if Anne were cold, she must be furry. But since there\'s no information that Anne is furry, does that create a contradiction? Let\'s see.\n\nWait, the problem is asking whether the statement "Anne is cold" is true, false, or unknown. So according to the rules, if Anne is 