ig people are not cold.\n9. Round, big people are not cold.\n10. Furry people are round.\n11. White, round people are furry.\n12. If Dave is furry and Dave is round then Dave is rough.\n13. If someone is furry then they are rough.\n\nThe question is whether Dave is not white. So, we need to determine Dave\'s color based on these statements.\n\nStarting with Dave\'s attributes. From point 2, Dave is rough and not young. Let me note that Dave is rough (R) and not young (¬Y). \n\nNow, looking at the rules. Rule 5 says that round people are white. So if Dave is round, then he would be white. But is Dave round?\n\nLooking at other rules. Rule 10 states that furry people are round. But Dave\'s furrieness isn\'t directly stated. However, rule 13 says that if someone is furry, then they are rough. Since Dave is rough, does that mean he could be furry? Wait, the contrapositive of rule 13 is: if someone is not rough, then they are not furry. But Dave is rough, so that doesn\'t necessarily mean he is furry. So being rough is necessary for being furry, but not sufficient. So Dave might or might not be furry.\n\nWait, but there\'s another rule: rule 12 says "If Dave is furry and Dave is round then Dave is rough." Well, Dave is already rough, so that doesn\'t add much. Hmm. Let\'s see.\n\nWe need to check if Dave is round. Let\'s see if there\'s any information leading to that.\n\nRule 10: Furry people are round. So if Dave is furry, then he must be round. But we don\'t know if Dave is furry. Alternatively, maybe there\'s another way to determine if he\'s round.\n\nLooking at the other rules. Rule 7 says all round, rough people are big. If Dave were round and rough (he is rough), then he would be big. But that doesn\'t tell us if he is round yet.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s a contradiction if we assume Dave is round or not.\n\nFirst, suppose Dave is round. Then by rule 5, he would be white. So if he\'s round, he\'s white. But the question is whether he is *not* white, so if he is white, then the statement "Dave is not white" would be false. But we need to see if we can confirm whether he is round.\n\nAlternatively, if he is not round, then rule 5 doesn\'t apply, so his color isn\'t determined by that. But maybe other rules could affect it.\n\nWait, let\'s see other rules. Rule 6 says if someone is young, they are white. But Dave is not young (from point 2), so that doesn\'t apply to him. So Dave\'s whiteness is only determined if he is round (from rule 5) or maybe other rules.\n\nWait, rule 11 says "White, round people are furry." So if someone is white and round, they must be furry. But Dave, if he were white and round, would have to be furry. But does that affect anything else?\n\nAlso, rule 10 says furry implies round, so if he were furry, he is round, and then by rule 5, white. So being furry would require him to be round and white, but we don\'t know if he\'s furry.\n\nNow, let\'s see if there are any contradictions if we assume Dave is round or not.\n\nSuppose Dave is round. Then he\'s white (rule 5). Then, by rule 11, since he\'s white and round, he must be furry. But if he\'s furry, then by rule 13, he must be rough (which he already is). That doesn\'t contradict anything. Wait, but rule 12 says if Dave is furry and round, then he is rough. Since he is both, that holds. So that\'s okay. So if Dave is round, he is white and furry, and that\'s consistent.\n\nAlternatively, suppose Dave is not round. Then, is there any rule that would require him to be white? The only other way would be if he were young, but he\'s not. So if he\'s not round, then his color isn\'t determined by the given rules. So his color could be anything, but the question is whether it\'s not white. Since the rules don\'t state anything else about his color unless he is round, we can\'t confirm he\'s white or not. Wait, but maybe there\'s another angle.\n\nWait, let\'s think again. Since Dave is rough and not young, can we derive if he is round?\n\nRule 7 says that all round and rough people are big. So if Dave were round and rough (he is rough), then he would be big. But we don\'t have any info on his bigness. So that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain here. Let\'s consider that if Dave is round, then he\'s white and furry (from above). But then, being furry (from rule 10) would require him to be round, so that loops back. But there\'s no contradiction here. However, the problem is whether we can conclude he is round or not.\n\nIs there any rule that says if someone is rough and not young, then they must be round? Not that I can see. So Dave could be round or not round. Since the premises don\'t state that Dave is round or not, except possibly through inference.\n\nWait, looking back at the given statements, the initial data says:\n\nDave is rough. Dave is not young. (point 2). The other attributes for Dave aren\'t listed, so we have to see if we can infer his roundness.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that Harry is furry (point 4) and furry implies round (rule 10) so Harry is round. But that\'s separate.\n\nWait, perhaps the key is in rule 12: "If Dave is furry and Dave is round then Dave is rough." Well, Dave is already rough, so this is a conditional that doesn\'t add new info unless we know if he is furry and round. But since we don\'t know that, maybe that\'s a tautology here.\n\nHmm. Let me try to see if there\'s another path. Suppose we assume Dave is not round. Then, he can\'t be white because rule 5 only applies to round people. Wait no, rule 5 says round people are white, but it doesn\'t say that only round people are white. So someone could be white without being round, but the rules don\'t state that. Wait, actually, the rule is "Round people are white" which is a one-way implication. So all round people are white, but there might be white people who are not round. However, in the given rules, there\'s no other way to be white except being young (rule 6) or round. Since Dave isn\