en, being furry and big would make him nice (rule 3), which he is. Also, since he is white and nice, rule 5 says he must be rough, which he is. Additionally, since he's furry and nice, he must be young (rule 6), which he is. Also, since he's furry and young, rule 7 says he must be white, which he is. So if Harry is big, all his attributes are consistent with the rules. \n\nBut is there a way for Harry to have those attributes without being big? Let's see. Suppose Harry is not big. Then the first rule doesn't require him to be furry. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nIf Harry is not big, can he still be nice, rough, white, young?\n\nLet's check the rules again. To be nice, he could be because of rule 9? But he isn't blue. Or maybe because of rule 3, but that requires being furry and big. Since he's not big, that path is closed. Alternatively, maybe there's another way to get \"nice\" without being big and furry?\n\nLooking at the rules, the only paths to \"nice\" seem to be:\n\n- Rule 3: Furry and big \u2192 nice\n- Rule 9: Young and blue \u2192 nice\n\nHarry isn't blue, so rule 9 doesn't apply. So the only other way would be if there's another rule. Wait, let me check the list again. The rules are numbered 1-9 as listed above. \n\nLooking back, the only other way to get \"nice\" would be if there's a rule I missed. Let me recheck:\n\nRule 5: white and nice \u2192 rough. So that's a premise, not a way to get nice.\n\nRule 6: Furry and nice \u2192 young. Again, that's a conclusion, not a way to get nice.\n\nRule 9 is the only other, which requires blue. So if Harry isn't big, then the only way he can be nice is if there's another path, but I don't see one. Wait, perhaps the rules are structured such that \"nice\" can be an independent attribute? But in the problem's context, maybe all instances of \"nice\" must be derived through the rules. Wait, the problem says \"If something is big then it is furry\" etc., so perhaps the attributes listed for the individuals are the