er must be A) True. \n\nWait, but I need to confirm once more. Let me think of Harry as big and see if any contradictions arise. \n\nIf Harry is big, then he must be furry (rule 1). Then, being furry and big, he is nice (rule 3), which matches. His being white is explained by rule 7 (furry and young \u2192 white; since he's young and furry, he must be white). His roughness comes from rule 5 (white and nice \u2192 rough). All check out. \n\nIf he isn't big, then he can't be furry (unless the rules allow it?), but being not big doesn't require not being furry. Wait, actually, rule 1 says if big then furry, but it doesn't say anything about non-big things. So a non-big thing could be furry or not. However, if Harry isn't big, but is furry, then to be nice, he would need to have big, but he isn't, so that path is blocked. \n\nAlternatively, if he's not furry and not big, then he can't get nice via any rule. Since he is nice, that's impossible. Therefore, the only possibility is he is big and furry. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion is Harry must be big, so the answer is A) True. \n\nWait a second, but the problem says \"Harry is nice. Harry is rough. Harry is white. Harry is young.\" So those are given as facts. The rules must hold, so if Harry is not big, then the rules don't prevent him from having those attributes except for the requirement to be nice. But since he is nice, but the only way to be nice is via being big and furry or blue and young, and he's not blue, he must be big and furry. \n\nTherefore, the answer is A) True. \n\nBut wait, I'm confused because the problem's options include \"Unknown\". Maybe I made a mistake here. Let me try to see if there's another angle. \n\nSuppose Harry is not big. Then, he could be furry or not. Let's suppose he is not furry. Then, he can't get nice via rule 3. But he is nice, so that's a contradiction. Therefore, he must be furry. But if he is furry but not big, then can he be nice? The only other way would be rule 9 (young and 