ause there\'s no other way for him to be nice. \n\nWait, but the rules are conditional statements. They don\'t say that "if something is nice, then it must be either (furry and big) or (young and blue)", but rather, they say that if those antecedents hold, then the consequent does. However, the rules don\'t explicitly state that those are the only ways to be nice. So perhaps there could be other ways to be nice not covered by the rules, but the problem\'s context might treat the given rules as exhaustive?\n\nHmm, this is a bit of a problem. The question is whether the rules are to be considered as all the possible ways to derive the consequent, or if they are just additional constraints. In logic, typically, rules are treated as necessary conditions but not necessarily exhaustive unless stated. However, in some contexts, especially in these types of problems, the rules might be considered as the only ways to derive the consequent. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem expects us to use the rules to deduce necessary conditions. For instance, since Harry is nice, then according to the rules, the only possible antecedents that can lead to "nice" are the ones in the rules. So if he is nice, then either he is furry and big (rule 3) or young and blue (rule 9). Since he is not blue, the only possibility is that he is furry and big. Therefore, he must be furry and big. \n\nTherefore, since he must be big, the answer would be True (option A). But wait, let me check again because this is a critical point. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem allows for an object to have an attribute without it being justified by the rules, but the rules must not contradict the given facts. So if Harry is nice, that\'s a fact, but the rules don\'t require that the reason for being nice must be covered by the rules. In that case, maybe Harry could be nice for some other reason not mentioned, and thus his being big is unknown. \n\nThis is a crucial ambiguity. In logic puzzles like this, usually