cts. The rules must hold in addition to these facts, so we have to ensure that all the rules are satisfied. \n\nWait, another angle: Let\'s suppose Harry is not big. Then, according to rule 1, being not big doesn\'t impose anything on his fur. So he could be furry or not. But if he is not furry, then he can\'t satisfy the antecedent of rule 3 (furry and big), so he can\'t be nice via that. But he is nice, so that\'s a problem. Alternatively, if he is furry but not big, then rule 3\'s antecedent isn\'t met (since big is required), so he can\'t get nice from that. So without being big, he can\'t be nice via rule 3, and since he\'s not blue, he can\'t via rule 9. Hence, there\'s no way for him to be nice unless he is big and furry. Since he is nice, he must be big and furry. Therefore, Harry must be big. So the answer is A) True. \n\nHmm, that seems solid. Therefore, I think the correct answer is A. Wait, but let me check the initial statements again. \n\nWait, the problem says "Bob is big. Bob is furry. Bob is nice..." etc. So Bob is an example of someone who is big and furry, hence nice. Harry has similar attributes (nice, rough, white, young) except not big or furry (as per given). But according to the rules, the only way he can be nice is if he is big and furry. Therefore, the fact that he is nice necessitates that he is big. \n\nTherefore, the answer must be A) True. \n\nWait, but I need to confirm once more. Let me think of Harry as big and see if any contradictions arise. \n\nIf Harry is big, then he must be furry (rule 1). Then, being furry and big, he is nice (rule 3), which matches. His being white is explained by rule 7 (furry and young → white; since he\'s young and furry, he must be white). His roughness comes from rule 5 (white and nice → rough). All check out. \n\nIf he isn\'t big, then he can\'t be furry (unless the rules allow it?), but being not big doesn\'t require not being furry. Wait, actually, rule 1 says if big then furry, but it doesn\'t say an