ccording to the third rule, Charlie must be green because he's blue (and thus cold). Therefore, Charlie is green. \n\nWait, that's a big realization. So Charlie is blue, so cold, so blue + cold \u2192 green. Therefore, Charlie must be green. But the problem didn't state that Charlie is green, so that's an inferred fact. \n\nSo Charlie is blue, quiet, and green (from the rules). Now, since Charlie is green, the second rule says green \u2192 round. Therefore, Charlie must be round. \n\nAh! So that's the missing link. Because Charlie is green (due to being blue and cold), he must be round (from the second rule). Therefore, Charlie is round. \n\nNow, since Charlie is round, from the sixth rule (all round people are not furry), Charlie is not furry. \n\nNow, since Charlie is cold (as a blue person) and not furry (because round), then applying the seventh rule: If someone is cold and not furry, then they are white. \n\nTherefore, Charlie must be white. \n\nWait, that would make the answer A) True. But let me check again step by step to ensure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nLet's go through again:\n\n1. Charlie is blue. Therefore, by \"All blue people are cold\", Charlie is cold.\n\n2. Charlie is blue and cold, so by the third rule \"Blue, cold people are green\", Charlie must be green.\n\n3. Since Charlie is green, the second rule \"If someone is green then they are round\" applies. Therefore, Charlie is round.\n\n4. Being round means (from rule 6) Charlie is not furry.\n\n5. Charlie is cold (from step 1) and not furry (from step 4). The seventh rule says \"If someone is cold and not furry then they are white.\" So Charlie must be white.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Charlie is white\" is true. So the answer should be A) True. Wait, but let me check the options again. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nBut wait, the problem's context says \"Dave is not white\". But that's Dave, not Charlie. So that doesn't affect Charlie's status. \n\nWait, but let me check if