sit the lion.\" Here, the \"someone\" is the lion itself. So, if the lion needs the lion (which we just inferred), then by statement 10, the lion would visit the lion. That seems to lead directly to the conclusion that \"The lion visits the lion\" is true, so the answer might be A) True?\n\nBut wait, let me check for any contradictions or missing pieces here. Let me retrace:\n\n- The lion needs the bear (statement 6). \n- Because of that, by statement 9, the lion needs the lion.\n- Then, by statement 10, needing the lion implies visiting the lion. So, the lion (the one who needs the lion) visits the lion (themselves). \n\nSo that would mean the lion does visit itself. But is there any rule that might prevent this? Let me check other statements to see if there's something conflicting.\n\nLooking at other statements, for example, statement 3 says the bear visits the lion. Statement 14 says if someone is rough, they visit the lion. The cow is rough (statement 5), so the cow must visit the lion (from 14). But the question is about the lion visiting itself. \n\nAnother point to consider: statement 8 says if someone visits the lion, then they are round. So, if the lion visits itself, then according to statement 8, the lion must be round. But does the lion's roundness come from somewhere?\n\nLooking back, the bear is round (statement 1). The lion's roundness isn't directly stated. However, if the lion visits itself, then by statement 8, the lion must be round. But is there any information that the lion is round? The problem doesn't state that directly. Wait, unless the lion's roundness is inferred through another path. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion is round because of the visit? Wait, no, the statement is that if someone visits the lion, then that someone is round. So if the lion visits the lion, then the lion (the visitor) must be round. But we don't know if the lion is round unless another statement gives that. The bear is round, but the lion's roundness i