plying the rules step by step.\n\nFirst, rule 2: All green things are big. Since Bob is green, that means Bob must be big. Wait, but the initial facts only say Bob is green and young. So from rule 2, Bob being green implies Bob is big. So Bob is big, green, young. \n\nThen, rule 1: All big things are smart. Since Bob is big (from rule 2), Bob must also be smart. So Bob is smart. \n\nNow, looking at rule 5: If Bob is smart and green, then Bob is quiet. Since Bob is indeed smart and green, then according to this rule, Bob must be quiet. Wait, but the original context didn\'t mention Bob being quiet. So according to the rules, Bob is quiet? Or is that a conclusion we can draw?\n\nWait, the problem says "Given a context and a question", and the context includes both the statements about individuals and the general rules. So we have to use the rules to deduce new information. So yes, since Bob is smart and green, he must be quiet. Therefore, Bob is quiet. So that\'s an inference we can make.\n\nNow, moving on. Let\'s see if Bob can be blue. The question is whether Bob is not blue (so is he blue or not?).\n\nFirst, Bob is green. Now, rule 4 says if something is green and blue, then it is nice. So if Bob were blue, then he would have to be green and blue, so he would have to be nice. But does Bob being nice conflict with any other rules?\n\nWait, Bob is already young. Rule 3 states that if something is nice, then it is young. So if Bob were nice, that would require him to be young, which he already is. So that\'s okay. But is there any information about Bob being nice?\n\nThe context doesn\'t say Bob is nice. However, if Bob were blue, then by rule 4, he would have to be nice. But since the context doesn\'t state Bob is nice, can we assume he isn\'t? Or is there a contradiction?\n\nWait, let\'s think again. If Bob were blue, then he would have to satisfy the conclusion of rule 4 (being nice). But the problem is, the context does not state that Bob is nice. So if we suppose