isits the dog and it eats the lion then the lion is round.\n\nHmm. The direct statements about the lion's attributes are only its color (blue). The other statements are conditional or about relationships.\n\nNow, let's see if we can find any premises that connect the lion's roundness.\n\nLooking at statement 21: \"If something visits the dog and it eats the lion then the lion is round.\" Wait, the phrasing here might be a bit tricky. Let me parse the structure:\n\nThe statement is: If (something visits the dog) and (it eats the lion) \u2192 then the lion is round.\n\nWait, the \"it\" here might refer to the same \"something\" that visits the dog? Or is \"it\" referring to another subject? Let me check the exact wording again.\n\nThe original says: \"If something visits the dog and it eats the lion then the lion is round.\" \n\nHmm, the wording is a bit ambiguous. The \"it\" might refer back to the \"something\", so the antecedent is: (something visits the dog) AND (that same something eats the lion) \u2192 lion is round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"it\" refers to the lion? But that would not make sense. Probably, the \"it\" is the \"something\". So, if there exists an entity that both visits the dog and eats the lion, then the lion is round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second part is \"it eats the lion\", where \"it\" could be the lion itself? But that seems unlikely. Probably, the \"something\" that visits the dog also eats the lion, leading to the conclusion.\n\nNow, let's see if there's any entity that both visits the dog and eats the lion.\n\nLooking at the given premises, which entities visit the dog?\n\nLooking back:\n\n- The bald eagle visits the dog (statement 4).\n- The cat visits the dog (statement 10).\n- The dog's own actions: the dog eats the cat (statement 11), but not sure about visiting.\n\nSo, the bald eagle and the cat both visit the dog.\n\nNow, do any of them eat the lion?\n\nLooking at statements:\n\nStatement 16 says \"If something is big then it