So who could satisfy this? Let's see:\n\nWho needs the lion? The rabbit (statement 6). The lion itself? Not sure. Let's see if the rabbit needs the lion (statement 6), so X could be the rabbit. Does the rabbit visit the rabbit? The problem doesn't say that. The only visits mentioned are the tiger visiting the rabbit (statement 7). So the rabbit itself visiting the rabbit is not stated. So maybe that's not applicable here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the BaldEagle needs the lion? Not sure. The BaldEagle's needs aren't mentioned except that the rabbit needs the BaldEagle (statement 5). So maybe that's not the case.\n\nHmm, perhaps that's a side track. Let's get back to the main question.\n\nWe need to see if the bald eagle chases itself. The question is whether it does not chase itself. To find out, we need to see if any of the given statements can lead to a contradiction if we assume it does, or if there's a statement that directly prohibits it, or if there's insufficient info.\n\nFirst, let's suppose for contradiction that the bald eagle does chase itself. So BaldEagle \u2192 chases \u2192 BaldEagle. Would that lead to any contradiction?\n\nLet's see. Let's check the statements one by one to see if that assumption leads to a problem.\n\nFirst, if BaldEagle chases itself, then according to statement 8, \"If something chases the rabbit, then it is big.\" Wait, but in this case, the action is chasing itself, not the rabbit. So statement 8 would not apply here. So that's okay.\n\nHowever, statement 8 applies only when chasing the rabbit. So that's fine.\n\nNow, statement 2 says BaldEagle chases the tiger. That's already given, so that's okay.\n\nWhat about other statements. Let's see:\n\nIf BaldEagle chases itself, then maybe we can apply other conditionals. Let's see if any condition applies when something chases itself.\n\nLooking through the statements:\n\nStatement 8: chasing rabbit \u2192 big.\n\nStatement 12: chasing tiger \u2192 tiger needs rabbit (already establishe