tiger, then it is big. (If X needs Tiger → X is big)\n10. If something needs the lion and it visits the rabbit, then it chases the lion. (If (X needs Lion ∧ X visits Rabbit) → X chases Lion)\n11. If something is big, then it chases the lion. (If X is big → X chases Lion)\n12. If something chases the tiger, then the tiger needs the rabbit. (If X chases Tiger → Tiger needs Rabbit)\n13. If something visits the tiger, then the tiger is not green. (If X visits Tiger → Tiger ¬green)\n14. If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. (If X chases Lion → Lion chases Rabbit)\n15. If something chases the rabbit and it does not visit the lion, then the lion does not need the rabbit. (If (X chases Rabbit ∧ X ¬visits Lion) → Lion ¬needs Rabbit)\n\nThe question is about whether "The bald eagle does not chase the bald eagle" is true. So, we need to see if from the given statements, we can infer that BaldEagle does not chase itself, or if it\'s impossible, or if there\'s not enough info.\n\nFirst, let\'s note that the question is about the bald eagle not chasing itself. The problem doesn\'t explicitly mention any self-chasing, so maybe we can find a contradiction if we assume it does, or maybe there\'s no info.\n\nLet me see if any of the statements can lead to that conclusion.\n\nFirst, let\'s note the existing relationships:\n\nFrom statement 2, the bald eagle chases the tiger. So BaldEagle → chases → Tiger.\n\nNow, let\'s see if any of the conditional statements can apply here. For instance, statement 8: If something chases the rabbit, then it is big. Since BaldEagle chases the rabbit (statement 1), so BaldEagle must be big. So BaldEagle is big. \n\nThen, statement 11 says if something is big, then it chases the lion. So since BaldEagle is big, BaldEagle must chase the lion. Wait, so from statement 1 and 8, BaldEagle is big, so via statement 11, BaldEagle chases the lion. That\'s an inference we can make. Let\'s note that: BaldEagle → chases → Lion.\n\nNow, if BaldEagle chases the lion, then according to statement 14, if something (in this case BaldEagle) chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. But statement 3 says the lion needs the rabbit, but does it chase? The question\'s first statement says the bald eagle chases the rabbit, but there\'s no mention of the lion chasing the rabbit except through this implication.\n\nWait, statement 14 says if X chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. So since BaldEagle chases the lion (as we inferred), then the lion must chase the rabbit. But the original statements don\'t state that the lion chases the rabbit. However, the problem\'s first statement is that the bald eagle chases the rabbit, but the lion\'s actions? Let me check the given info again. The only direct statements about the lion\'s actions are that the lion needs the rabbit (statement 3), and that the rabbit does not chase the lion (statement 4). There\'s no explicit mention of the lion chasing the rabbit, but according to statement 14, since BaldEagle chases the lion, the lion must chase the rabbit. But does that create any problem?\n\nWait, but the problem\'s statements are all the given info. So if from our deductions, we have that the lion must chase the rabbit, then that\'s a fact. But the original context doesn\'t mention that. However, according to the rules, if something (like BaldEagle) chases the lion, then the lion must chase the rabbit. So that would mean the lion does chase the rabbit. But is that allowed? The problem\'s context doesn\'t say the lion can\'t do that. So perhaps that\'s acceptable. But maybe there\'s another constraint?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a contradiction here. Wait, let\'s see.\n\nWait, let\'s see if the rabbit needs the lion (statement 6). So Rabbit needs Lion. Then, statement 10 says if something needs the lion and visits the rabbit, then it chases the lion. But who needs the lion and visits the rabbit? The rabbit itself needs the lion (statement 6), but does it visit itself? Not sure. The tiger visits the rabbit (statement 7). The tiger needs the rabbit? Let me check. From statement 12: If something chases the tiger, then the tiger needs the rabbit. Since the bald eagle chases the tiger (statement 2), that means the tiger needs the rabbit. So Tiger needs Rabbit. So, Tiger → needs → Rabbit. \n\nWait, so from statement 12, because BaldEagle chases the tiger, then the tiger must need the rabbit. So that\'s an inference. So Tiger needs Rabbit. \n\nNow, going back to statement 10: If X needs the lion AND X visits the rabbit → then X chases the lion. So who could satisfy this? Let\'s see:\n\nWho needs the lion? The rabbit (statement 6). The lion itself? Not sure. Let\'s see if the rabbit needs the lion (statement 6), so X could be the rabbit. Does the rabbit visit the rabbit? The problem doesn\'t say that. The only visits mentioned are the tiger visiting the rabbit (statement 7). So the rabbit itself visiting the rabbit is not stated. So maybe that\'s not applicable here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the BaldEagle needs the lion? Not sure. The BaldEagle\'s needs aren\'t mentioned except that the rabbit needs the BaldEagle (statement 5). So maybe that\'s not the case.\n\nHmm, perhaps that\'s a side track. Let\'s get back to the main question.\n\nWe need to see if the bald eagle chases itself. The question is whether it does not chase itself. To find out, we need to see if any of the given statements can lead to a contradiction if we assume it does, or if there\'s a statement that directly prohibits it, or if there\'s insufficient info.\n\nFirst, let\'s suppose for contradiction that the bald eagle does chase itself. So BaldEagle → chases → BaldEagle. Would that lead to any contradiction?\n\nLet\'s see. Let\'s check the statements one by one to see if that assumption leads to a problem.\n\nFirst, if BaldEagle chases itself, then according to statement 8, "If something chases the rabbit, then it is big." Wait, but in this case, the action is chasing itself, not the rabbit. So statement 8 would not apply here. So that\'s okay.\n\nHowever, statement 8 applies only when chasing the rabbit. So that\'s fine.\n\nNow, statement 2 says BaldEagle chases the tiger. That\'s already given, so that\'s okay.\n\nWhat about other statements. Let\'s see:\n\nIf BaldEagle chases itself, then maybe we can apply other conditionals. Let\'s see if any condition applies when something chases itself.\n\nLooking through the statements:\n\nStatement 8: chasing rabbit → big.\n\nStatement 12: chasing tiger → tiger needs rabbit (already established because BaldEagle does chase the tiger, so tiger needs rabbit).\n\nStatement 15: If something chases the rabbit and does not visit the lion, then lion does not need rabbit. \n\nWait, the BaldEagle chases the rabbit (statement 1), so if we consider BaldEagle in this statement, then if BaldEagle chases rabbit and does not visit the lion, then the lion does not need the rabbit. But the lion does need the rabbit (statement 3), so that would create a contradiction if those conditions are met. Let\'s see: Does BaldEagle visit the lion?\n\nThe problem doesn\'t state that the BaldEagle visits the lion. The only visits mentioned are the tiger visiting the rabbit (statement 7). So if we assume that BaldEagle does not visit the lion (since it\'s not stated), then the antecedent of statement 15 would be true: BaldEagle chases rabbit AND ¬visits lion → therefore, the conclusion would be that the lion does not need the rabbit. But statement 3 says the lion does need the rabbit. That would be a contradiction. \n\nWait, so if BaldEagle chases the rabbit and does not visit the lion (which we can assume since it\'s not stated), then according to statement 15, the lion does not need the rabbit. But statement 3 says the lion does need the rabbit. That would be a contradiction. Therefore, our assumption that BaldEagle does not visit the lion must be wrong, or that BaldEagle does visit the lion?\n\nW