ion addresses the attribute \"rough,\" there's no basis in the provided data to determine the truth or falsity of that statement. \n\nHowever, I should make sure that there isn't any indirect implication. For example, maybe \"cold things are big\" and \"big things have some property,\" but \"rough\" isn't linked here. The term \"rough\" isn't connected through any of the given relationships. \n\nTherefore, since the context provides no information about the squirrel's texture or any attribute related to \"rough,\" the correct answer should be that it's unknown. The options given include C) Unknown, which would be the right choice here. \n\nWait, but let me double-check if \"rough\" could be a typo or maybe a term derived from another word. For instance, \"cold\" might sometimes be associated with roughness in some contexts, but the problem's statements don't make that connection. The only mention of \"cold\" is that cold things are big, which doesn't relate to roughness. So no, there's no link. \n\nHence, the answer is C) Unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe context provides information about various entities (bald eagle, cat, squirrel, tiger) and their relationships (likes, sees, chases, age, size), but it never mentions the term \"rough\" or any attribute related to texture. The question asks about the squirrel being \"rough,\" which is not addressed in any of the given statements. Since there is no logical pathway connecting the squirrel's properties to \"roughness\" within the provided premises, the statement's truth value cannot be determined from the information given.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
