ikes BE (as we inferred), then the mouse must eat the lion. But that\'s a conclusion about the mouse, not the bald eagle. \n\nThe question is about the bald eagle\'s action. So perhaps we need another angle.\n\nLet me see if there\'s a statement that if something is rough, then it eats the lion? Not directly. Wait, statement18\'s consequent is eating the lion, but only when the something likes BE.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle being rough (from earlier) can lead to something else. Since BE is rough (as per above), then from statement15, BE must like itself (BE likes BE). But that might not help.\n\nWait, perhaps there\'s another angle. Let me check the given statements again for any that connect BE eating the lion.\n\nLooking at statement19: "If something likes the mouse and the mouse does not eat the cat then the cat does not see the mouse." Hmm, not directly relevant here.\n\nWait, let me check the direct statements. The only direct statement about BE\'s eating is that it eats the mouse (statement1). The question is about eating the lion. So unless there\'s an implication that BE must eat the lion based on some conditions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that BE likes the lion (statement2) can be used with some other condition. Let me see:\n\nLooking at statement18 again: "If something likes the bald eagle then it eats the lion." Wait, so if an entity X likes the bald eagle (BE), then X eats the lion. But the bald eagle itself likes the lion (statement2), not BE. Wait, no, BE likes the lion, not itself. So the only entities that might like BE are the mouse (inferred) and maybe others. \n\nWait, the lion likes the cat (statement8), so the lion doesn\'t like BE. The cat\'s feelings towards BE aren\'t stated except in the cat not liking the lion. \n\nWait, so the only X that likes BE is the mouse (since mouse is rough → likes BE via statement15). Therefore, the mouse must eat the lion (from statement18). But that doesn\'t help with BE\'s actions.\n\nAlter