nother rule implies the bald eagle\'s coldness, we can\'t assume that.\n\nWait, there\'s another rule: Rule17. Let\'s check that.\n\nRule17: If the dog likes the bald eagle AND the bald eagle sees the dog → the bald eagle is cold.\n\nSo let\'s see if the dog likes the bald eagle. From the basic facts, the dog likes the squirrel (fact4), but not mentioned liking the bald eagle. So unless another rule makes the dog like the bald eagle, that part may not hold.\n\nLooking at rule12: If someone likes the dog and they see the squirrel → the dog likes the bald eagle.\n\nWait, the antecedent here is if someone (could be any entity) likes the dog and that someone sees the squirrel, then the dog likes the bald eagle.\n\nIs there any entity that likes the dog and sees the squirrel?\n\nLooking at the facts:\n\nThe rabbit likes the dog (fact8). Also, the rabbit sees the squirrel (fact9). So the rabbit is that someone. Therefore, the conditions of rule12 are met: the rabbit likes the dog and the rabbit sees the squirrel. Therefore, according to rule12, this should trigger that the dog likes the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but does that mean the dog now likes the bald eagle?\n\nYes. So from rule12\'s application, since the rabbit (the \'someone\') likes the dog and sees the squirrel, the conclusion is that the dog likes the bald eagle. Therefore, the dog does like the bald eagle.\n\nNow, going back to rule17: If the dog likes the bald eagle AND the bald eagle sees the dog → the bald eagle is cold.\n\nWe just established that the dog does like the bald eagle (from rule12). Also, the bald eagle does see the dog (fact2). Therefore, both conditions of rule17 are met. So that means the bald eagle is cold.\n\nSo now, the bald eagle is cold (from rule17\'s conclusion).\n\nNow, let\'s revisit rule13: If the bald eagle sees the dog AND the bald eagle is cold → the bald eagle likes the dog.\n\nWe know both parts are true (since the bald eagle is now established as cold). Therefore, the bald eagle likes the dog.\n\nSo, bald eagle likes the dog. \n\nNow, going back to the question: Does the bald eagle eat the rabbit?\n\nWe need to see if any rule leads to that.\n\nLooking at the existing facts, the bald eagle eats the dog (fact1), but nothing directly about the rabbit. Let\'s check the conditional rules.\n\nRule11: If someone is cold and they like the bald eagle → they eat the rabbit.\n\nWait, but here the subject is "someone". Let\'s see who could be that someone. The bald eagle is cold (from above), but does the bald eagle like the bald eagle? Not sure. Wait, the "someone" here is the subject. So, the conditions are:\n\nSomeone (X) is cold AND X likes the bald eagle → X eats the rabbit.\n\nSo, if the bald eagle (X) is cold (which it is) and the bald eagle likes the bald eagle (does it?), then the bald eagle would eat the rabbit. But does the bald eagle like itself? The problem doesn\'t mention that. The only likes mentioned are:\n\nFact10: squirrel likes the bald eagle.\n\nFact4: dog likes the squirrel.\n\nFact8: rabbit likes the dog.\n\nFact (from rule12 conclusion) dog likes bald eagle.\n\nAnd from rule13 conclusion, bald eagle likes the dog.\n\nSo the bald eagle likes the dog, but there\'s no mention of the bald eagle liking itself. So the first part of rule11\'s antecedent (X likes the bald eagle) for X being the bald eagle would require the bald eagle to like itself, which isn\'t stated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another X?\n\nSuppose someone else is cold and likes the bald eagle. Let\'s see:\n\nThe dog is cold (fact3). Does the dog like the bald eagle? Yes, from rule12\'s conclusion (dog likes bald eagle). So the dog is cold and likes the bald eagle. Then according to rule11, that would mean the dog eats the rabbit.\n\nWait, that\'s an important point. Let\'s check this:\n\nThe dog is cold (fact3). The dog likes the bald eagle (from rule12\'s conclusion). Therefore, applying rule11 to the dog (X is the dog):\n\nX (dog) is cold and X (dog) likes the bald eagle → dog eats the rabbit.\n\nSo that would mean the dog eats the rabbit. But the problem states in fact6 that the rabbit eats the dog. Wait, but can two animals eat each other? The problem doesn\'t say that\'s impossible, but perhaps that\'s a contradiction? Or maybe it\'s allowed?\n\nWait, but the original fact6 says "The rabbit eats the dog." So according to the given data, the rabbit eats the dog, but if the dog also eats the rabbit, that\'s possible unless there\'s a rule against it. The problem doesn\'t state that an animal can\'t be eaten by another and vice versa. So maybe that\'s allowed.\n\nBut in any case, according to rule11, if the dog meets those conditions, the dog would eat the rabbit. But the question is about the bald eagle eating the rabbit, not the dog. So perhaps this isn\'t directly relevant, but it\'s a side conclusion.\n\nHowever, let\'s see if any other rules can lead to the bald eagle eating the rabbit.\n\nAnother rule: Rule16: If the bald eagle is red and the bald eagle likes the squirrel → the squirrel eats the rabbit.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the bald eagle is red, so that might not apply.\n\nRule15: If someone likes the dog and the dog likes the squirrel → they like the bald eagle.\n\nThe dog does like the squirrel (fact4), so if someone likes the dog, then they like the bald eagle. But again, not sure how this connects to the bald eagle eating the rabbit.\n\nRule14: If someone likes the rabbit → they are young. Not directly helpful.\n\nRule18: If someone sees the bald eagle → the bald eagle is young. The bald eagle sees the dog (fact2), but that\'s the bald eagle seeing the dog, not someone else seeing the bald eagle. Unless the dog sees the bald eagle? Wait, fact2 says the bald eagle sees the dog, but not the other way around. So unless another animal sees the bald eagle, but the problem doesn\'t mention that. So rule18 might not be applicable here.\n\nNow, going back to the question: Does the bald eagle eat the rabbit?\n\nLooking for any rule that could lead to that. Let\'s think again about rule11, but applied to the bald eagle.\n\nThe bald eagle is cold (from rule17\'s conclusion) and does the bald eagle like itself? Not stated. The bald eagle likes the dog (from rule13\'s conclusion), but not necessarily itself. Therefore, the first part of rule11\'s antecedent for X being the bald eagle would require the bald eagle to like the bald eagle, which isn\'t given. So that path doesn\'t work.\n\nAlternatively, is there another rule that could cause the bald eagle to eat the rabbit?\n\nLooking at the initial facts, the bald eagle eats the dog (fact1). There\'s no direct statement about the rabbit, but perhaps some chain of reasoning.\n\nWait, let\'s see if any of the rules can be used in reverse or in combination.\n\nWait, the squirrel likes the bald eagle (fact10). If someone (like the squirrel) is cold and likes the bald eagle, would that make them eat the rabbit? But we don\'t know if the squirrel is cold. So that\'s not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, the dog now likes the bald eagle, and the bald eagle likes the dog. The dog is cold. But the dog eating the rabbit is a consequence of rule11, but again, that\'s about the dog, not the bald eagle.\n\nHmm. Maybe there\'s another angle. Let me check all the rules again.\n\nRule12\'s conclusion gave us that the dog likes the bald eagle. Then, using that, rule17 gave us the bald eagle is cold. Then rule13 gives the bald eagle likes the dog. \n\nNow, does the bald eagle\'s liking the dog lead to any other rules?\n\nRule15: If someone likes the dog and the dog likes the squirrel → they like the bald eagle.\n\nThe dog likes the squirrel (fact4). So if someone likes the dog, then they like the bald eagle. For example, the rabbit likes the dog (fact8), so applying rule15: since rabbit likes dog and dog likes squirrel, then rabbit likes bald eagle. But that\'s a side note.\n\nAlternatively, the bald eagle likes the dog. So, if the bald eagle is the "someone" in rule15, does that apply? The bald eagle likes the dog (so part of the first condition) and the dog likes the squirrel (fact4). Therefore, according to rule15, the bald eagle (the someone) would like the bald eagle. Wait, the conclusion is "they like the bald eagle". So "they" refers to the someone (the bald eagle in this case). So the bald eagle would like the bald eagle? That would mean the bald eagle likes itself. \n\nWait, let me parse that again. Rule15 says: If someone likes the dog and the dog likes the squirrel → they like the bald eagle. \n\nSo "they" here refers to the someone. So if the bald eagle is the someone (since it likes the dog and the dog likes the squirrel), then the bald eagle would like the bald eagle. \n\nSo that would mean the bald eagle likes itself. \n\nWait, but the dog likes the squirrel is given (fact4). The bald eagle does like the dog (from rule13\'s conclusion). So the bald eagle (X) likes the dog (so first condition of rule15 is met) and the dog likes the squirrel (second part of the condition). Therefore, according to rule15, X (the bald eagle) must like the bald eagle. \n\nTherefore, the bald eagle likes itself. \n\nThat\'s an important conclusion. \n\nNow, going back to rule11: If someone is cold and they like the bald eagle → they eat the rabbit.\n\nThe bald eagle is cold (from earlier), and the bald eagle likes the bald eagle (from rule15\'s conclusion). So applying rule11 to the bald eagle (X is the bald eagle):\n\nX (bald eagle) is cold (yes), and X likes the bald eagle (yes, because X likes itself). Therefore, according to rule11, the bald eagle must eat the rabbit. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the bald eagle does eat the rabbit. \n\nBut the question is asking if the statement "The bald eagle does not eat the rabbit" is true, so if the bald eagle does eat the rabbit, then the statement is false. Therefore the answer would be B (False).\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any contradiction here. Let me retrace the