final_output:  ['\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe mouse is green, and since it eats the tiger (given), the conditional "If something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse" applies. This means the tiger visits the mouse. The conditional "If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big" then requires the mouse to be big. Thus, the statement "The mouse is not big" is **false**.', ':\nOkay, let\'s see. I need to figure out if the statement "The mouse is not big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me start by going through each of the provided statements one by one and see how they connect.\n\nFirst, the context says: "The mouse is green." So the mouse has the property of being green. Then there\'s another line: "The tiger is green." So both the mouse and tiger are green. \n\nNext, the first conditional statement: "If something visits the mouse then the mouse is big." Hmm, so if X visits the mouse, then mouse becomes big. But we need to see if there\'s any information about something visiting the mouse. Let me check other parts.\n\nAnother conditional: "If something eats the tiger then the tiger visits the mouse." The mouse eats the tiger? Wait, no. Wait the context says "The mouse eats the tiger." Wait, let me check again. The second statement is "The mouse eats the tiger." So the mouse is the one eating the tiger. So, according to the second conditional, if something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse. Since the mouse eats the tiger, that would mean the tiger must visit the mouse. So the tiger visits the mouse. \n\nWait, so the tiger is visiting the mouse. But the first conditional says if something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big. Here, the tiger is visiting the mouse, so that would mean the mouse must be big. But the question is asking if the mouse is NOT big. So if this is the case, then the mouse would have to be big, which would make the statement "mouse is not big" false. But I need to check if there are any contradictions or other conditions that might affect this.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Let me retrace. The mouse eats the tiger (given). Therefore, since "something eats the tiger" (the mouse does), then the tiger must visit the mouse. Therefore, the tiger visits the mouse. So, substituting into the first conditional, since the tiger is visiting the mouse, that triggers the first condition: if something (the tiger) visits the mouse, then the mouse is big. Therefore, the mouse must be big. Therefore, the statement that "the mouse is not big" would be false, so the answer would be B) False. \n\nBut wait, maybe there\'s another angle here. Let\'s check other conditions to see if they affect this conclusion. Let\'s go through the rest of the statements.\n\nAnother condition: "If the dog is young and the dog does not visit the mouse then the mouse does not visit the rabbit." The rabbit is young (given), but the dog\'s age isn\'t directly stated except that the rabbit is young. Wait the context says "The rabbit is young." The dog\'s age isn\'t mentioned except that the rabbit eats the dog. Hmm, but the dog\'s age isn\'t specified as young or not. So maybe this condition\'s antecedent isn\'t triggered yet. \n\nAnother conditional: "If something is green and it chases the rabbit then the rabbit does not eat the mouse." The mouse is green, and the tiger is green. Do either of them chase the rabbit? The context says "The rabbit does not chase the tiger." But does the tiger chase the rabbit? Not stated. The mouse? Not sure. Let\'s see. The first statement says "The dog does not eat the rabbit." So the dog isn\'t eating the rabbit. The mouse eats the tiger, but not sure about chasing. \n\nAnother condition: "If something is green then it visits the dog." Since the mouse and tiger are green, that means both the mouse and the tiger must visit the dog. So the mouse visits the dog and the tiger visits the dog. Wait, so the mouse is green, so it visits the dog. The tiger is green, so it also visits the dog. So that adds two more facts: mouse visits dog, tiger visits dog. \n\nThen another condition: "If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young then it is round." The rabbit is young. So if something visits the rabbit, then that something is round. \n\nNext: "If something is round and it visits the dog then it chases the tiger." So if X is round and visits the dog, then X chases the tiger. \n\nLastly: "If something is big then it visits the rabbit." So if the mouse is big (which we thought earlier it is because the tiger visits it, leading to mouse being big), then the mouse would have to visit the rabbit. \n\nWait, but let\'s see step by step. Let me try to list all the facts and implications.\n\nFirst, given statements:\n\n1. Dog ≠ eats rabbit.\n2. Mouse eats tiger.\n3. Mouse is green.\n4. Rabbit ≠ chases tiger.\n5. Rabbit eats dog.\n6. Rabbit is young.\n7. Tiger is green.\n\nConditionals:\n\nA. If X visits mouse → mouse is big.\n\nB. If X eats tiger → tiger visits mouse.\n\nC. If (dog is young ∧ dog doesn\'t visit mouse) → mouse doesn\'t visit rabbit.\n\nD. If (X is green ∧ X chases rabbit) → rabbit doesn\'t eat mouse.\n\nE. If X is green → X visits dog.\n\nF. If (X visits rabbit ∧ rabbit is young) → X is round.\n\nG. If (X is round ∧ X visits dog) → X chases tiger.\n\nH. If X is big → X visits rabbit.\n\nNow, let\'s see:\n\nFrom 2: mouse eats tiger. So, applying B: since mouse eats tiger (so X=mouse), then the tiger must visit the mouse. So tiger visits mouse. \n\nTherefore, from A: since tiger (X=tiger) visits the mouse, then the mouse must be big. So mouse is big. \n\nTherefore, the statement "mouse is not big" would be false. So the answer would be B) False. But let\'s check if there\'s any reason this might not hold. \n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a contradiction somewhere. Let\'s check other conditions.\n\nFrom E: since mouse is green, then mouse visits dog. Similarly, tiger is green, so tiger visits dog. \n\nSo mouse visits dog (from E), and tiger visits dog (from E). \n\nNow, from H: If X is big → X visits rabbit. Since we concluded mouse is big, then mouse must visit rabbit. \n\nBut does the mouse visiting rabbit lead to anything? Let\'s see. \n\nFrom F: If X visits rabbit and rabbit is young (which it is), then X is round. So since mouse is visiting rabbit (from H), then mouse must be round. \n\nNow, if the mouse is round and visits the dog (from E, since mouse visits dog), then from G: if (X is round and visits dog) → X chases tiger. So the mouse would chase the tiger. \n\nBut is there any statement that contradicts the mouse chasing the tiger? The original context says "mouse eats the tiger", but chasing is different from eating. The problem doesn\'t say anything about chasing the tiger except the condition in D. Let\'s check D again.\n\nD: If (X is green and X chases rabbit) → rabbit doesn\'t eat mouse.\n\nWe know the mouse is green, but does the mouse chase the rabbit? From G\'s result, the mouse is round and visits dog, so it chases the tiger. But chasing the tiger isn\'t directly linked here. \n\nWait, D requires that X is green and X chases the rabbit. So unless the mouse is chasing the rabbit, this condition wouldn\'t apply. \n\nSo, unless the mouse is chasing the rabbit, which we don\'t have evidence for. The mouse is chasing the tiger (from G), but not necessarily the rabbit. \n\nSo perhaps that\'s okay. \n\nNow, let\'s see if there are any contradictions. \n\nWe concluded that the mouse is big (from A and B). Then H says big things visit the rabbit. So mouse visits rabbit. Then F says that since mouse visits rabbit and rabbit is young, mouse is round. Then G says since mouse is round and visits dog (which it does), then mouse chases tiger. \n\nBut the original context says the mouse eats the tiger. Chasing the tiger might be okay, but eating is separate. \n\nIs there any other condition that would contradict mouse being big? Let me check condition C. \n\nCondition C: If (dog is young ∧ dog does not visit mouse) → mouse does not visit rabbit.\n\nWe know the rabbit is young, but the dog\'s age isn\'t stated. Wait, the dog\'s age isn\'t given except that the rabbit eats the dog. The dog\'s age is unknown. So unless the dog is young, the antecedent of C is not triggered. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog is not young, then the antecedent is false, so the implication is true regardless. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog is young, then we need to see if it doesn\'t visit the mouse. But does the dog visit the mouse? From E, since the dog\'s color isn\'t mentioned, but E says "if something is green, then it visits the dog". Wait, no. Wait E is: "If something is green then it visits the dog." Wait, no, the exact wording is "If something is green then it visits the dog." Wait, no, sorry, let me check again. \n\nWait the exact wording of E: "If something is green then it visits the dog." So, if X is green, then X visits the dog. So the green things (mouse and tiger) must visit the dog. So the mouse visits the dog, tiger visits the dog. \n\nBut the dog\'s color isn\'t stated. So the dog\'s color isn\'t green necessarily. So the dog\'s visiting mouse isn\'t required unless the dog is green, but we don\'t know that. \n\nSo, going back to condition C: If the dog is young and the dog does not visit the mouse → then the mouse does not visit the rabbit. \n\nWe know the rabbit is young, but the dog\'s age is unknown. So unless we can determine the dog\'s age, this might not be helpful. \n\nHowever, since the rabbit eats the dog (from context 5: "The rabbit eats the dog"), does that imply anything about the dog\'s age? Probably not directly. \n\nSo, since the dog\'s age is unknown, condition C\'s antecedent might not be triggered. Therefore, maybe this doesn\'t affect the conclusion. \n\nSo putting it all together: the mouse must be big because the tiger visits it (due to the mouse eating the tiger). Therefore, the statement "mouse is not big" is false. So the answer is B) False. \n\nWait but let me check one more time. The first condition: If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big. Since the tiger visits the mouse (because the mouse ate the tiger), then the mouse must be big. Therefore, the mouse is big, so the statement "mouse is not big" is false. Therefore, the correct answer should be B. \n\nIs there any way the mouse could not be big? Like, maybe the tiger doesn\'t visit the mouse? But the mouse eats the tiger, so according to condition B, that requires the tiger to visit the mouse. So that\'s necessary. Therefore, there\'s no way around it. The mouse must be big. So the answer is B.\n'] 91%|█████████ | 547/600 [15:30:40<1:36:57, 109.77s/it]
