\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path. Let\'s see if any other conditions require the cow to need the tiger.\n\nLooking at statement 11: "If someone needs the tiger then the tiger sees the bear." If the cow needed the tiger, then the tiger would have to see the bear. But we already know that the tiger must see the bear because the bear needs the tiger (from being big via statement 10 and 12), which triggers statement 11. So that condition is already satisfied. But that doesn\'t mean the cow needs the tiger, just that if it did, the tiger seeing the bear is already true. So that doesn\'t help.\n\nHmm. Let me see statement 14: "If someone is young then they need the mouse." So if the cow is young, it needs the mouse. But we don\'t know if the cow is young. Also, needing the mouse isn\'t directly related to needing the tiger. Unless there\'s a connection between needing the mouse and needing the tiger, but I don\'t see it.\n\nLooking at statement 7: the tiger needs the mouse. So the tiger\'s need is for the mouse, but that doesn\'t affect the cow\'s needs.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if the cow\'s chasing the bear (statement 4) could lead to anything. The cow is chasing the bear, but does that trigger any conditions? The only relevant condition is statement 10, which applies to someone chasing the cow. Since the cow is the one being chased by the bear, that gives the bear\'s bigness, but the cow\'s own chasing doesn\'t trigger anything in the given statements.\n\nWait, statement 12 says if someone is big, they need the tiger. So if the cow is big, then it must need the tiger. But unless the cow is big, this doesn\'t apply. Since there\'s no indication the cow is big, then we can\'t assume that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cow is not big, so the cow doesn\'t need the tiger. But that would mean the statement "The cow does not need the tiger" is true. But we need to check if there\'s any other condition that might require the cow to need the tiger regardless of being big.\n\nLooking at all the statements again, I don\'t see any that directly link the cow needing the tiger. The only possible indirect link might be through needing the tiger being required by being big, but since the cow isn\'t established as big, that doesn\'t apply. \n\nWait a second, let me check if there\'s another path. Let\'s see statement 13: If the tiger is big (which we established it is), then... well, statement 13 is the one that made the tiger big. But since the tiger is big, then per statement 12, the tiger needs the tiger? That\'s a bit strange, but maybe that\'s okay. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in assuming the tiger is big. Let me retrace that step. \n\nStatement 13 says: If the tiger sees the bear and the tiger needs the mouse, then the tiger is big. \n\nWe know the tiger needs the mouse (statement 7). \n\nWe also need to confirm that the tiger sees the bear. \n\nThe original context says the bear sees the tiger (statement 3). But does that mean the tiger sees the bear? Not necessarily unless there\'s mutual sight, which isn\'t stated. However, statement 11 says that if someone needs the tiger, then the tiger must see the bear. \n\nEarlier, we derived that the bear is big (from statement 10, since the bear chases the cow), so the bear needs the tiger (statement 12). Therefore, the bear needing the tiger triggers statement 11, which requires that the tiger sees the bear. \n\nTherefore, the tiger must see the bear. So the conditions of statement 13 are met: tiger sees bear and needs mouse, so the tiger is big. \n\nTherefore, the tiger is big, and thus per statement 12, the tiger needs the tiger. \n\nBut that\'s a bit odd, but perhaps acceptable in the problem\'s logic. \n\nNow, getting back to the cow. \n\nIs there any way the cow could need the tiger? Let\'s think of other conditions. \n\nLooking at statement 14: If someone is young, they need the mouse. If the cow is young, then it needs the mouse, but that doesn\'t connect to needing the tiger. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the cow\'s need for the tiger is not required by any rules, so we can\'t assume it does or doesn\'t. \n\nWait, let\'s check statement 9: "If someone chases the mouse and the mouse does not need the bear then the mouse is not young." \n\nThe mouse chases the tiger (statement 5). So the mouse is chasing the tiger, but that\'s not relevant to this statement unless someone else is chasing the mouse. \n\nThe tiger does not chase the mouse (statement 6), so the tiger isn\'t chasing the mouse. \n\nThe cow\'s actions: the cow chases the bear, and the bear chases the cow. \n\nHmm. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cow is required to need the tiger through some other implication. Let me check all the statements again for any clues.\n\nWait, statement 11: "If someone needs the tiger then the tiger sees the bear." We know the tiger sees the bear because of the bear needing the tiger (from being big). But that\'s already satisfied, so that doesn\'t add anything else.\n\nStatement 12: "If someone is big then they need the tiger." The cow is not established as big, so that doesn\'t apply. \n\nStatement 13 is already used to make the tiger big. \n\nSo, perhaps there\'s no direct or indirect information about the cow needing the tiger. Therefore, the statement "The cow does not need the tiger" could be true or false, but there\'s no information to confirm either. Wait, but maybe the default is that it\'s unknown. \n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a way to prove that the cow does need the tiger. Let me think again. \n\nSuppose the cow is big. Then it must need the tiger. But why would the cow be big? To be big, someone must be chasing the cow (statement 10). The bear is doing that, so the bear is big, but the cow itself isn\'t necessarily big unless another condition makes it so. \n\nAlternatively, could the cow be big because it\'s chasing the bear? Statement 10 is about chasing the cow, not the bear. So no. \n\nAnother thought: The problem says "if 