nd white, not red, so this might not apply here.)\n2. All rough things are green.\n3. If something is kind then it is green. (Anne and Fiona are kind, and they are green, which matches.)\n4. Quiet, green things are big. (Fiona is quiet, green, and big? Wait, the context says Erin is big, Fiona\'s attributes include quiet and green, so Fiona must be big? Let me check: "Erin is big" and "Fiona is quiet, green, etc." So the rule says quiet and green things are big, so Fiona must be big. But the context didn\'t explicitly say Fiona is big? Wait, the context says "Erin is big" and "Quiet, green things are big." Since Fiona is quiet and green, she must be big. But the given info about Fiona doesn\'t mention big. Hmm, maybe that\'s a point to check, but maybe that\'s okay because the rule enforces it. But this might not affect Charlie.)\n5. If something is rough and green then it is red.\n6. If something is green then it is rough. (So all green things are rough.)\n7. If Erin is red then Erin is green. (But Erin is already green.)\n8. All red, rough things are quiet.\n9. If something is quiet and not red then it is not white.\n\nNow, considering Charlie\'s attributes: Charlie is not big, not green, and white. Let\'s see if any of these contradict the rules.\n\nFirst, since Charlie is not green, the rules about green things don\'t apply to Charlie. The only possible issue could be the last rule: "If something is quiet and not red then it is not white." But Charlie\'s quietness isn\'t mentioned. The context says Fiona is quiet, but Charlie\'s quietness isn\'t stated. So unless Charlie is inferred to be quiet, which there\'s no info, that rule doesn\'t apply.\n\nWait, the question is about Charlie being white, and the context directly states that Charlie is white. Unless there\'s a rule that would make that impossible. Let me check again.\n\nThe problem says "Charlie is white" in the initial statements. The question is whether that\'s true, so unless the rules contradict that,