es in the given context don\'t mention white. Wait, hold on. Fiona is cold and furry. According to rule 5, all cold and furry things are white. So Fiona must be white, even if it\'s not listed. Because the rule says if something is cold and furry, then it\'s white. So Fiona must be white. But the original context didn\'t list Fiona as white. Hmm, so that\'s an inference. So Fiona is white. But does that affect anything else?\n\n6. All rough, nice things are young. So if something is both rough and nice, then it must be young. Let\'s see. Bob is rough and nice. So Bob has those two attributes. Therefore, according to this rule, Bob must be young. Wait, that\'s important! Because Bob is rough and nice, so he must be young. But the context didn\'t list Bob as young. But according to the rule, he must be. So Bob is young. Therefore, Bob is young. That would mean that the first conditional involving Bob (if Bob is young and cold then furry) now applies. But since Bob is young (from rule 6), and if he\'s cold, then he must be furry. But does Bob have to be cold?\n\nWait, young things are cold (rule 3). Since Bob is young (from rule 6 because he\'s rough and nice), then he must be cold. So Bob is cold (from rule 3 because he\'s young). Therefore, applying the first rule: If Bob is young and cold (which he now is), then Bob must be furry. So Bob must also be furry. But the context only says Bob is nice and rough. So according to the rules, Bob must also be cold, young, and furry. But the problem didn\'t list those, so we can infer them. So Bob\'s actual properties are: nice, rough, young, cold, furry. But the problem didn\'t state that, so we have to go by the rules. \n\nNow, getting back to the question: Is Anne young?\n\nAnne\'s attributes are cold, furry, rough, smart, white. The question is whether she\'s young.\n\nFirst, check if any of the rules can lead us to conclude Anne is young or not.\n\nLet me think about the rules again.\n\nFirst, to be young, a thing must be 