s the rabbit, then the antecedent would require rabbit likes mouse to make the consequent true, but since the consequent is already true (dog likes mouse), that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog eating the rabbit would allow us to use this conditional, but without knowing if the dog eats the rabbit, it\'s not helpful.\n\nLooking at conditional b again: The rabbit is nice (premise 10). If the rabbit eats the mouse, then mouse chases dog. But we don\'t know if the rabbit eats the mouse. Unless there\'s a rule that says chasing leads to eating, but that\'s not stated. The rabbit chases the mouse (premise 9), but that doesn\'t mean it eats it.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if there\'s any way to get the rabbit to like the mouse through some chain. For example, if the rabbit eats the rabbit? No, that doesn\'t make sense. Or maybe via some other condition.\n\nWait, conditional g says if someone eats the rabbit, then they like the rabbit. But unless someone eats the rabbit, that doesn\'t help.\n\nWait, perhaps the dog eats the cat (from h), but does that lead to anything else? The cat is being eaten by the dog, but that might not affect the rabbit\'s feelings towards the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think of all the conditions that could lead to the rabbit liking the mouse:\n\n- If someone eats the cat and chases the rabbit (conditional d), then rabbit likes mouse. So if the dog does both (eats the cat and chases the rabbit), then yes. But we need to know if the dog chases the rabbit.\n\nAlternatively, is there another way? Let me see the other conditionals again.\n\nConditional f: Cat chases rabbit → cat chases dog. That\'s already inferred, so cat does chase the dog. But how does that help?\n\nWait, the dog is being chased by the cat. Then, does that trigger conditional a? Conditional a says if someone chases the dog, then the dog likes the cat. The cat is chasing the dog (from f\'s inference), so that someone is the cat. Therefore, applying conditional a