he dog, not the mouse, so no.\n\nWait, perhaps the mouse being cold (premise 7) is part of some condition, but I don\'t see any conditionals involving being cold except for the mouse and the dog not being cold. So maybe irrelevant.\n\nAlternatively, the dog likes the mouse (premise 6), and if someone likes the mouse they must be blue (conditional c). The dog is blue, so that\'s okay, but that\'s already covered.\n\nWait, let\'s check all the premises and inferences again to ensure I didn\'t miss anything:\n\nPremises:\n\n1. Cat chases rabbit → so by conditional f, cat chases dog.\n\n2. Cat likes mouse → so cat must be blue (from c), which we inferred.\n\n3. Dog is blue → so dog eats cat (from h).\n\n4. Dog not cold.\n\n5. Dog likes cat (given), which is consistent with a (since cat chases dog, so dog must like cat, which is already stated).\n\n6. Dog likes mouse (given).\n\n7. Mouse is cold (given).\n\n8. Mouse doesn\'t like cat (given).\n\n9. Rabbit chases mouse (given).\n\n10. Rabbit is nice (given).\n\nInferences:\n\n- Cat is blue (from 2 and c).\n\n- Dog eats cat (from 3 and h).\n\n- Cat chases dog (from 1 and f).\n\nNow, any other inferences? Let\'s see.\n\nIf the dog eats the cat (inferred), and if the dog also chases the rabbit, then by d, the rabbit would like the mouse. But does the dog chase the rabbit?\n\nIs there any condition that causes the dog to chase the rabbit? Let me check all conditionals:\n\nLooking at conditional e: If dog eats rabbit and rabbit likes mouse → dog likes mouse. But that requires the dog to eat the rabbit, which we don\'t know.\n\nConditional a: If someone chases the dog → dog likes cat. The cat chases the dog (inferred from f), so that\'s already applied.\n\nConditional b: If someone is nice (rabbit is nice) and eats mouse → mouse chases dog. But we don\'t know if rabbit eats mouse.\n\nConditional g: If someone eats rabbit → they like rabbit. But no eater mentioned.\n\nConditional e requires dog eating rabbit, which we don\'t kno