ention Erin's age directly. However, since Erin is red and not round, but does that relate to being young? Let's see.\n\nThe only connection between red and age might be through other attributes. Since all young people are furry (fact 10), but we don't know if Erin is furry. Wait, the context doesn't state anything about Erin being furry or not. So maybe Erin could be young or not?\n\nWait, but if Erin is smart (from the earlier conclusion that she's nice, so smart), then according to fact 13, she must be young. So Erin must be young. But does that lead to any contradictions?\n\nLet's see. If Erin is young, then according to fact 10, she must be furry. But the context doesn't say Erin is furry. Wait, but does that matter? The problem is that the context only lists some attributes. So maybe Erin is furry but it's not stated? Or is there a contradiction here?\n\nWait, the problem states that Fiona is young and furry, Charlie is furry, etc., but Erin's furrieness isn't mentioned. However, if Erin is young (from being smart via being nice), then she must be furry. But since the context doesn't say she's furry, that's okay? Because absence of mention doesn't mean absence of the trait. So maybe that's okay. \n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that says only certain people can be furry? The context says Charlie and Fiona are furry, but others might be as well. Since being young requires being furry, so Erin would have to be furry if she's young. Since we concluded she's young (because she's smart), then she must be furry. But the context doesn't state that, so that's an inferred fact. But since the problem is about whether Erin is smart, maybe that's okay. \n\nWait, but let me check again. Let me go through each step again to make sure I didn't skip anything.\n\n1. Erin is red (fact 5).\n2. Therefore, Erin is not round (fact 8).\n3. Applying the conditional statement (fact 11): If Erin is red and not round (both true), then Erin is nice. Therefore, Erin must be nice.\n4. 