ed. However, if Erin is young (from being smart via being nice), then she must be furry. But since the context doesn\'t say she\'s furry, that\'s okay? Because absence of mention doesn\'t mean absence of the trait. So maybe that\'s okay. \n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that says only certain people can be furry? The context says Charlie and Fiona are furry, but others might be as well. Since being young requires being furry, so Erin would have to be furry if she\'s young. Since we concluded she\'s young (because she\'s smart), then she must be furry. But the context doesn\'t state that, so that\'s an inferred fact. But since the problem is about whether Erin is smart, maybe that\'s okay. \n\nWait, but let me check again. Let me go through each step again to make sure I didn\'t skip anything.\n\n1. Erin is red (fact 5).\n2. Therefore, Erin is not round (fact 8).\n3. Applying the conditional statement (fact 11): If Erin is red and not round (both true), then Erin is nice. Therefore, Erin must be nice.\n4. Since Erin is nice (from step 3), fact 14 says nice people are smart. Therefore, Erin is smart.\n5. Therefore, the statement "Erin is not smart" is false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nBut wait, the question asks if "Erin is not smart" is true, so if our conclusion is that Erin is smart, then the statement is false. So the correct answer would be B) False.\n\nHowever, let me check if there\'s any possibility that being smart requires being young, and if Erin\'s age could be an issue. \n\nFrom fact 13: All smart people are young. Since Erin is smart (from step 4), she must be young. But there\'s no direct information about Erin\'s age. But that\'s okay because the conclusion that she must be young is a necessary conclusion from her being smart. So Erin is young. \n\nBut if Erin is young, then according to fact 10, she must be furry. However, the context doesn\'t say she\'s furry, but that\'s acceptable because the context doesn\'t list all attributes. The 