tement21, they can't visit the lion. But visiting the cow isn't prohibited. \n\nTherefore, if the rabbit is big, they can visit the cow or not. \n\nTherefore, in that scenario, we can't know. \n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit is not big, then they must not visit the cow. \n\nBut since the rabbit's bigness is unknown, we can't determine for sure. \n\nTherefore, the answer is unknown (C). \n\nWait, but let's see if there's a different angle. \n\nLet me try to see if there's any other rule that could affect this. \n\nStatement16: If someone is rough and does not chase the rabbit, then they visit the bald eagle. \n\nBut we don't know who is rough, so maybe irrelevant. \n\nStatement17: If someone chases the lion, then they don't need the cow. \n\nThe bald eagle chases the lion, so the bald eagle does not need the cow (from this). \n\nDoes that help? Maybe not directly. \n\nStatement18: If someone needs the bald eagle and the bald eagle chases the cow, then the bald eagle is not round. \n\nBut the bald eagle doesn't chase the cow (as per given info?), so this antecedent isn't met. \n\nHmm. \n\nAnother approach: Let's see what the question is asking. We need to determine if \"The rabbit does not visit the cow\" is true, false, or unknown. \n\nIf there is any possible scenario where the rabbit does visit the cow, then the answer can't be true (so it's either false or unknown). If there is any scenario where it doesn't, then it's not necessarily false. \n\nBut if the statement can be both true and false in different scenarios given the info, then it's unknown. \n\nAlternatively, if all scenarios given the info lead to it being true, then the answer is true. \n\nLet me try to see possible scenarios:\n\nScenario 1: Rabbit does not need the cow. \n\nThen, by statement19, since rabbit chases bald eagle and doesn't need cow, they must visit the cow. \n\nIf they visit the cow, then by statement20, they must be big. \n\nThus in this scenario, rabbit visits the cow and is big. \n\nSo