      "text": ":\nOkay, let's try to figure this out step by step. The question is whether the bear likes the mouse. The options are True, False, or Unknown. \n\nFirst, I need to go through all the given statements and see if any of them directly or indirectly relate the bear and the mouse's relationship. Let me list out all the premises again to make sure I don't miss anything.\n\nStarting with the bald eagle:\n- The bald eagle eats the bear. \n- It's cold.\n- Doesn't like the bear.\n- Likes the tiger.\n- Visits the bear.\n\nThen the bear:\n- Eats the bald eagle.\n- Is blue.\n- Doesn't visit the tiger.\n\nThe mouse:\n- Likes the tiger.\n- Is eaten by the tiger.\n- Is cold.\n- Likes the bear (wait, no, the tiger likes the bear? Wait let me check again. The tiger likes the bear. The mouse likes the tiger. Wait, the first part says \"The mouse likes the tiger.\" The tiger likes the bear. So the mouse's relation to the bear isn't directly stated here yet.\n\nThen the conditional statements:\n\n1. If the mouse is kind, then the mouse eats the bald eagle.\n2. If something eats the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the tiger, then the tiger likes the mouse.\n3. If something likes the tiger, then it visits the mouse.\n4. If the tiger likes the mouse and the mouse doesn't eat the tiger, then the mouse is big.\n5. If something visits the bear and is kind, then the bear likes the tiger.\n6. Cold, kind things are big.\n7. If the tiger eats the bald eagle, then the tiger doesn't eat the mouse.\n8. If something visits the mouse, then it is kind.\n9. If the bald eagle eats the mouse and the mouse doesn't eat the bear, then the bald eagle is big.\n\nHmm, okay. Let's see. The question is about the bear liking the mouse. The direct statements don't mention the bear's feelings towards the mouse. The bear's relationships are: it eats the bald eagle, is blue, doesn't visit the tiger, and the tiger likes the bear (from \"the tiger likes the bear\"). \n\nWait, the tiger likes the bear.