o by rule 9, since it sees the rabbit (itself), does that imply it must see the squirrel? Wait, no, rule 9 says if something sees the rabbit, then it sees the squirrel. Wait, \"something\" here is any entity. So if the rabbit sees the rabbit (itself), then by rule 9, it must see the squirrel. But the rabbit already does see the squirrel (fact 6). So that's okay. \n\nBut regardless, the rabbit's coldness isn't required for the squirrel's coldness. Since the squirrel's coldness is already established via the mouse's conditions, the rabbit's status doesn't affect that. So the conclusion that the squirrel is cold holds.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false. So the answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check once more for any possible contradictions or missed rules.\n\nAnother rule is rule 13: If the rabbit sees the squirrel and the squirrel chases the rabbit, then the rabbit does not chase the squirrel. \n\nThe rabbit does see the squirrel (fact 6). Does the squirrel chase the rabbit? The squirrel chases the lion (fact 7), but there's no mention of the squirrel chasing the rabbit. So the antecedent's second part (squirrel chases the rabbit) is false. Therefore, the implication holds (since the antecedent is false, the implication is true regardless). So no problem here.\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel did chase the rabbit, then the rabbit would have to stop chasing the squirrel, but that's not the case here.\n\nAnother check: Rule 11 says if something chases the squirrel, it needs the squirrel. The rabbit chases the squirrel (fact 4), so the rabbit needs the squirrel. But the problem states that the mouse needs the rabbit (fact 3). So that's separate. Doesn't conflict.\n\nSo all the rules seem to hold with the conclusion that the squirrel is cold. Therefore, the answer should be B) False, because the squirrel is cold, making the statement \"squirrel is not cold\" false.\n\nWait a second, but let me think again. The problem says \